import Foundation

/// The words of "How it works" and "What's new". Every release adds its entry at the top of `news`
/// and changes the parts of `guide` that it touched.
enum Guide {
    struct Part: Identifiable {
        let title: String
        let text: [String]
        var id: String { title }
    }

    struct Release: Identifiable {
        let version: String
        let date: String
        let items: [String]
        var id: String { version }
    }

    static let guide: [Part] = [
        Part(title: "What Seedboard is for", text: [
            "Seedboard is where a thought starts. You plant it, you let it grow, and when it is ready you hand it to PARAGON.",
            "Each board is an endless sheet. On it you can draw a mind map, lay out a flow with shapes and arrows, and stick notes anywhere. You can put pictures on it too.",
            "A Timeline board is a board of its own kind: a plan over time, with one bar for each thing to do. See Timeline board.",
            "A board can hold a door: a small card that opens another board. So one thought can lead to the next. See Doors to other boards.",
            "Start small. One idea in the middle is enough. Add to it as the thought grows.",
            "Thoughts can also come in from outside: from the Share button of any app, from Shortcuts and Siri, and from the app icon on the iPhone. See Sharing to Seedboard, and Shortcuts, Siri, Raycast and Spotlight.",
            "When the board is ready, press Send to PARAGON. Your notes app gets a note, a project or a few to-dos. Later the board shows how far PARAGON got with them. See What PARAGON did with it.",
            "There is no Save button. Every change is kept as you go, even the words you are still typing.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The home page", text: [
            "On the Mac and the iPad, the home page shows when no board is open, and when you press Home at the top of the list. At the top stand the sprout, the heading Plant a seed and the line Every idea starts on a board. There is no button there: the green New board at the top of the list is the one to press.",
            "Under the small heading STARTERS is one strip of starter cards that slides sideways: the ten that come with Seedboard (Blank board, Mind map, Flow, Pros and cons, Plan in steps, Timeline, Who does what, Decision grid, Weekly review, SWOT) and your own after them. Each card is 132 points wide: a small picture of what you get, with its name under it. On a wide Mac window some show whole and the next is cut off at the edge, which says there are more. Rest the pointer on a card to read what it is for.",
            "The Blank board is always the first card of the strip, even if you moved it elsewhere among your starters. The New board choice and the Starters page keep your own order.",
            "Press a card and the new board opens. Press and hold a card (right-click on the Mac) for Rename…, Change…, Remove… and Edit starters…. The small Edit button at the right of the heading opens the Starters page. See Starters.",
            "Below the strip are your boards as cards: Pinned first, then Recent, then Sent to PARAGON. So your boards come first: on a Mac window of 1280 by 800 points, 12 cards are in sight without sliding.",
            "Each card shows a small picture of the board, its colour dot, its name and when you last changed it, for example Changed 2 days ago. A board that is a note or a project in PARAGON adds how far it is there, for example Changed 2 days ago · 5 of 13 done. When the card is too narrow it says 2 days ago · 5 of 13 done, or only 5 of 13 done. The card of an empty board shows a faint sprout.",
            "Still thinking about these? (0.13): boards not changed for 2 weeks come up at the top of the boards, under that heading. At most 3, the oldest first, each with Changed 3 weeks ago and the buttons Put away and Not now. Not now hides that board there for 2 weeks. Pinned boards, boards sent to PARAGON, boards put away and boards that cannot be changed are left out. On the iPhone it stands at the top of the list. Settings, Home page switches it off.",
            "Press a card to open the board. Press and hold a card (right-click on the Mac) and you get the same menu as in the list: Rename…, Pin to the top or Unpin, Make a copy, Save as a starter…, Colour, Open in PARAGON (a sent board) and Remove….",
            "On the Mac the edge of a card turns green and its shadow grows while the pointer is over it. On the iPad with a trackpad or a mouse the card lifts under the pointer.",
            "The cards follow the search field above the list. See Your boards.",
            "On the iPhone the list of boards is the home. See iPhone.",
            "Seedboard opens where you left. The board you had open last comes back, at the same zoom and the same spot. Every board keeps its own zoom and spot.",
            "Each device remembers its own: the Mac, the iPad and the iPhone do not share this.",
            "A board you never moved or zoomed opens showing all of it. If you left the app with no board open, it starts on the home page. If the board is gone, it starts on the home page too.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Your boards", text: [
            "On the Mac and the iPad the list starts with a Home row. Press it and the home page with the starters and all your boards is back. The list at the side holds all your boards in three groups: Pinned, Recent, and Sent to PARAGON. On the iPhone the list itself is the home.",
            "A pinned board always stays under Pinned, even after it was sent.",
            "Each row shows a coloured dot, the name, and a count such as 12 ideas · 4 shapes · 2 notes. An empty board says Empty. A timeline says, for example, 6 bars · 1 milestone · 2 phases. Doors are counted last, for example 1 note · 2 doors. The dots follow day and night.",
            "The board that is open has a soft green row with its name in bold green.",
            "A tiny amber dot at the end of a row means: the newest change of this board is on this device, but not in iCloud yet. The pictures on the board count too: the dot stays until every picture the board shows is really in iCloud as well. It is always amber, also on the chosen row. It goes away by itself.",
            "Search boards (under New board on the Mac and the iPad, at the bottom on the iPhone) finds boards by their name and by every word written on them: ideas, shapes, notes, text, frames, the words on arrows, the words under a picture, and the names of the boards it has doors to. So timber finds the board with the note Check timber prices in May.",
            "Type two words and only boards with both are shown. Capitals and accents do not matter, and the start of a word is enough. The round cross empties the search; on the Mac, Escape does too. If nothing fits, it says No board has these words.",
            "Press New board (at the top of the list on the Mac and the iPad; the round green + at the bottom on the iPhone). The starters, each a card with a small drawing: the ten that come with Seedboard (Blank board, Mind map, Flow, Pros and cons, Plan in steps, Timeline, Who does what, Decision grid, Weekly review, SWOT) and your own after them. Press Edit there to change them. See Starters. On the iPad and the Mac they show in a small pop-up under the button, three in a row. On the iPhone they come up from the bottom, two in a row.",
            "Flow and Plan in steps come coloured. Change any colour with Colour in the bar.",
            "A new board takes the name of its start. A second one gets a 2, a third a 3. A blank board that is still called New board takes the first words you write.",
            "To change a board, press and hold its row on the iPhone and iPad, or right-click it on the Mac. You can then choose:",
            "Rename. Pin to the top (or Unpin). Make a copy. Save as a starter… Colour (the dot: green, pink, orange, blue, teal or purple).",
            "Open in PARAGON, if the board was sent. Remove.",
            "Open in PARAGON opens the note under the name it has now in PARAGON, also after you renamed it there or moved it to the Archive.",
            "On the Mac and the iPad you can drag a board's row onto the open board: a door to that board lies where you let go. See Doors to other boards.",
            "Make a copy gives the board the same name plus the word copy. A second copy gets a 2. The copy is not pinned and not marked as sent.",
            "Remove asks first. The board leaves all your devices, and is kept for 30 days. You can bring it back in Settings. See Removed boards and earlier versions.",
            "Settings is at the bottom of the list, next to the version number. On the Mac it opens in its own window (also from the app menu, or with ⌘,). On the iPhone and iPad it is a page with Done.",
            "Put away: press and hold a board's row or card (right-click on the Mac), and choose Put away (on the Mac also File, Put Board Away). A pinned board is unpinned first. A line says Board put away. Find it under Put away. At the bottom of the list and of the home page a quiet row, Put away (3), opens the page Put away: newest first, each with its name, Put away 3 days ago and its line, and Bring back. Done (Escape) closes. A put-away board is never deleted and the choice reaches your other devices. It says Nothing is put away. when empty.",
            "The line under each board: an ordinary board says, for example, 34 ideas · 12 done (ideas are things with words; frames, pictures, sketches and doors do not count; done is read from PARAGON on this device). An empty board says Empty. A timeline says, for example, 6 bars · 1 milestone, and once something is done or late it counts bars and milestones together: 7 things · 4 done · 1 late. Who does what keeps its own line. A part with zero is left out. The line is on the list and on the home cards.",
            "Search also finds the words inside pictures, and boards that are put away (in their own section, Put away).",
            "Due day (0.11): More, Due day… on a board (on the Mac File, Due Day…) sets a day of its own. The card and the row show Due 12 Jan in small words, red once the day has passed. No due day takes it away. It does not change the order of the list.",
            "Pictures nobody uses (0.11): in Settings, under Your boards, a line says how many pictures no board uses and how much room they take, with Show and Remove now (asks once, cannot be undone). Pictures used by any board, removed board, earlier version, safety copy or starter stay. If Seedboard cannot be sure, it says cannot tell right now. A picture added in the last hour is not counted.",
            "A board from a newer Seedboard cannot be imported; the line says to update Seedboard first.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Starters", text: [
            "Ten starters come with Seedboard: Blank board, Mind map, Flow, Pros and cons, Plan in steps, Timeline, Who does what, Decision grid, Weekly review, SWOT. You can add your own, and you can rename, move, change and remove all of them.",
            "To make your own, open a board, press More, then Save as a starter…. On the Mac it is also in the File menu, as Save as a Starter…. In the list of boards, press and hold a row (right-click on the Mac) and choose Save as a starter….",
            "A small page asks for the name. The name of the board is filled in. Press Save, or Cancel. If the name is taken, the new one becomes, for example, Trip plan 2. A line then says Saved as the starter “Trip plan 2”.",
            "A board from a newer Seedboard cannot be saved as a starter. It can only be looked at.",
            "Your starters stand beside the built-in ones wherever starters are shown: in the strip on the home page, in the New board choice, in File, New Board, and in the New board action of Shortcuts. A new starter goes last.",
            "A new board from your starter is a fresh copy. It is not pinned, it is not marked as sent to PARAGON, and it has the name of the starter. A starter made from a timeline moves with the calendar: the plan sits as far from today as it did on the day you saved it.",
            "The Starters page: press the small Edit button beside the STARTERS heading on the home page, or the Edit button in the New board choice. On the Mac it is also File, Starters…. A starter card's menu has Edit starters… too.",
            "The page lists every starter in order, each with its small picture, its name and its grey line. Drag a row to move it. On the iPhone and iPad each row has a handle for this. Done closes the page (Escape on the Mac).",
            "Each row has an Edit menu: Rename… (the name and the grey line, with Cancel and Save), Change…, Remove…, and, for a built-in starter you changed, Back to the original.",
            "Remove… asks first: Remove the starter “Name”? The Blank board cannot be removed, because a new board must always be possible. A starter of your own that you removed is kept for 30 days and then it is deleted.",
            "At the bottom of the page, only when a built-in starter was removed, is Bring back the built-in starters. Press it and they are all there again.",
            "Press and hold a starter card (right-click on the Mac), on the home page or in the New board choice, and you can choose Rename…, Change…, Remove… and Edit starters….",
            "Change…: the starter's board opens like any board, with a bar at the top that says Changing the starter “Name”, with Cancel and Save. Save is in full colour. Work as you like, then press Save.",
            "Cancel, after you changed something, asks Discard the changes? You can Discard or Keep changing.",
            "While you change a starter the board has no Send to PARAGON, Pin, Remove or Save as a starter. Rename renames the starter.",
            "A built-in starter that you changed keeps your version. Back to the original brings the first one back.",
            "If you go to another board, or back to the list, or quit the app (Mac) without pressing Cancel or Save, what you changed is kept: the starter is saved, and a line says Saved the starter “Name”. Only Cancel throws the changes away, and it asks first.",
            "If Seedboard is stopped while you change a starter (the iPhone closed it, or it was quit by force), your work is not lost. It is kept on that device as you go, and at the next start the starter opens again, with the line You were changing the starter “Weekly review”. Your changes are back. Save and Cancel work as always.",
            "If that starter was removed on another device meanwhile, your work is kept as a new starter of your own, and the line says The starter “Weekly review” was removed. What you were changing is kept as a new starter.",
            "Starters are kept on all your devices. If two devices change the same starter, the newer change stays. The name and the place come from the newest rename or move, the board from the newest change of the board itself, so a rename on one device never puts an older board back. A board that loses is kept aside for 30 days. Seedboard looks again when it comes to the front, and when the Starters page opens.",
            "A starter made by a newer Seedboard cannot be used. A blank board is made instead, and a red line says This starter was made by a newer Seedboard. Update the app to use it. A blank board was made instead.",
            "The New board action in Shortcuts and Siri offers your own starters too, in your order. See Shortcuts, Siri, Raycast and Spotlight.",
            "0.9: three new starters come with Seedboard, at the end of the built-in ones: Decision grid (a frame Which one? with Option A, B and C down and Price, Effort and Fun across, a note in each cell), Weekly review (four frames: Done, Stuck, Next week and Ideas) and SWOT (four coloured frames: strengths, weaknesses, chances, threats). Pros and cons was already there. The New board action in Shortcuts and Siri offers them too.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The board", text: [
            "At the top right: Undo, Redo, More, and the purple Send to PARAGON button. Everything that leads to PARAGON is purple. The Send button stands by itself in the top bar, in its own full purple, by day and by night. On the iPhone, Undo and Redo sit at the bottom left, above the tools.",
            "More holds: Rename, Pin to the top, Save as a starter, Paste a list as a mind map (on a timeline: Paste a list as bars), Copy the board as text, Make a timeline of it…, Share…, Print… (iPhone and iPad; on the Mac it is in the File menu) and Remove the board.",
            "When you came to this board through a door, the top bar shows ‹ and the name of the board you came from. Press it to go back. On the Mac and with a keyboard, ⌘[ does the same. See Doors to other boards.",
            "A board that was sent to PARAGON as a note or a project has one small purple line under its top bar, for example Project “Summer cabin” · 5 of 13 done · checked 14:02, with Open in PARAGON. See What PARAGON did with it.",
            "At the bottom in the middle: nine tools. Press one and the new thing appears in the middle of what you can see, on a free spot, ready to type.",
            "Mind map: one new idea, the middle of a new mind map.",
            "The shape tile: a plain press adds the shape you picked last, a Box at first. Hold the tile, or press the small corner mark on it (Mac and iPad), and a plate shows all eight shapes. See Shapes and arrows.",
            "Diamond: a diamond, for a yes or no question.",
            "Arrow: switches the arrow tool on. See Shapes and arrows.",
            "Sticky note: a yellow note. Text: free words with no box. Frame: a frame to group things.",
            "Picture: a photo or a screenshot, with a line of words under it if you like. See Pictures.",
            "Draw: the last tool. Lines drawn by hand. See Drawing by hand.",
            "On a narrow iPhone the nine tiles shrink to fit: 40 points wide, else 36, else 32. The row slides sideways only when even 32 does not fit.",
            "At the bottom right: Smaller, the zoom in percent, Bigger, and Show the whole board.",
            "Behind the board lies a soft dot every 24 points. The dots only help the eye. They are never in a picture that is sent or shared.",
            "When something is chosen, a bar shows what you can do with it. On the Mac and the iPad it floats 12 points above the chosen thing, above its plus dots if it has some. If there is no room above, it stands 12 points below. It always stays 8 points inside the visible board and follows the thing while you slide or zoom.",
            "The bar goes out of the way while you drag the thing or type in it, and comes back when you let go or finish typing. With several things chosen it stands over the box round all of them. A chosen arrow has its bar too.",
            "On the iPhone the bar is always exactly one row, at the top under the title. The main buttons keep their word and the others show only a mark. What does not fit goes into a … button that opens the rest as a short list from the bottom. A narrow iPad or Mac window does the same: the bar loses words first, then buttons, never a second row.",
            "Every button of a bar has the colour of what is chosen: pink for an idea, blue for a shape, amber for a note, teal for a frame, grey-blue for an arrow, green for several things. Only two stand apart, at the end behind a thin line: To do is purple and Remove is red.",
            "The bar always has a … button when the chosen thing can be turned into another kind. Turn into, and By colour for several things, sit behind it. See Turn into, tidy and sort.",
            "A button with only a mark shows its name when you rest the pointer on it.",
            "Colour in the bar opens a row of colour dots. A ring marks the colour the thing has now.",
            "While you type, your words go straight onto the board, and they are kept as you type. A shape grows wider first and then taller, a note grows taller, and mind maps make room.",
            "When a mind map grows, the notes, shapes, text and other maps it would cover step aside, just as far as needed. Something you laid on a map yourself stays where you put it.",
            "Return, or pressing elsewhere on the board, keeps your words. Escape cancels: the words go back to what they were, and a thing you just made goes away again.",
            "With a keyboard, Shift Return, or Option Return, makes a new line exactly where the cursor stands, and you go on typing from there. It works in an idea, a box, a note, text and a frame. Return alone still ends the typing; in an idea it starts the next idea. Names of one line only (bars on a timeline, names and messages on a Who does what board, lane names) stay one line.",
            "On the iPhone and iPad a bar sits above the keyboard while you type. Done keeps your words. See The bar above the keyboard.",
            "An empty board invites you. In the middle stand a sprout, the line What is on your mind? and three buttons: Mind map (pink), Sticky note (amber) and Paste a list (green). One press does what the tool does. Paste a list is pale while nothing is copied; pressed anyway, it says Nothing to paste. On the Mac a quiet line adds or double-click anywhere for a sticky note. While Draw is on, the board stays clear.",
            "Short lines at the bottom say what just happened. They slide up and fade in, and fade out when they go. Green says all is well, purple says something went to PARAGON, brick red says something did not work. On the home page and in the iPhone's list such a line stands at the bottom of the window, so a line like Board removed. It is kept for 30 days in the Removed folder. is seen there too.",
            "The board moves softly: a zoom glides, new things grow in, removed things fade out, and things that make room glide to their new place. See How it feels.",
            "0.9, More now also holds (0.10: Colour legend…, Show frames and Small map each have a mark): Colour legend…, Background (Plain, Dots, Squares), Small map, Sort by dots, Clear all dots, Number the steps, Tidy into a grid, Show frames, and Undo list. Several are only for ordinary boards; a timeline and a Who does what board keep to their own tools.",
            "The Undo button: press and hold it (right-click on the Mac) or press ⌥⌘Z for the Undo list. See Choosing, moving, sizing, copying, removing, undo.",
            "VoiceOver (0.11): each thing and each arrow is read on its own, in outline order, for example Idea: Roof, done, 2 votes. The actions are Choose, Edit words and, where the thing can move, Move left, right, up or down by 10 points. Ideas in a mind map place themselves, so they have no Move. Timeline and Who does what boards are read as before.",
            "With the system setting Differentiate Without Colour, a late timeline bar shows slanted white strokes, a late milestone a dashed outline, and the colour legend card writes the colour's name before its words.",
            "A thing placed more than 100,000 points from the middle is brought back to that limit, and sizes are held to 0 to 20,000.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Mind maps", text: [
            "Press the Mind map tool and type. This is the middle of your map.",
            "Press Return. A first branch appears, ready to type. Press Return again for the next idea beside it.",
            "Press Tab for an idea under the one you are typing. Tab is how a map goes deeper.",
            "Press Return on an empty new idea and it goes away again. So Return, Return ends a list of ideas.",
            "Press Escape to cancel: the words go back to what they were, and an idea you just made goes away again. Return keeps the words. On the iPhone, Return on the screen keyboard ends an idea and starts the next.",
            "With a keyboard, Shift Return makes a new line inside an idea, exactly where the cursor stands.",
            "Choose an idea (press it once) and just start typing. Your words go on after the ones already there. Space, or two quick presses, also let you type.",
            "With an idea chosen, Tab makes an idea under it, and Return makes the next idea beside it. On the middle idea, Return makes a first branch.",
            "Shift Tab moves an idea out one level: it becomes the next idea beside the one it hung under. It works while you type too, and you go on typing. A main branch cannot go further out.",
            "The arrow keys walk the map. Right and left go to the first idea under it, or back to the one above. Up and down go to the sister ideas.",
            "A small green plus stands beside a chosen idea. Press it, or pull it, and a new idea appears under that idea. The middle idea has one plus, on its right side. Where the new branch lands follows Both sides: with it off, new branches go to the right. With it on, it goes to the side that has fewer branches.",
            "The bar of a chosen idea offers: Idea under, Next idea, Fold or Unfold, One side or Both sides (middle idea) or Colour (any other idea), To do, Duplicate, Words, Remove. On the iPhone and iPad it also has Move up, Move down and Choose more.",
            "The lines of a map get thinner as it goes deeper: 3.4 points to a main branch, 2.4 to the next level, 1.8 below that. The words sit on the line: the line comes to the foot of an idea and runs on under its words, in the branch's colour, and the ideas under it start from the end of that line. On the left side of a map it runs the other way. Lines from the middle idea still leave the middle of its side.",
            "Fold hides everything under an idea. The ideas slide into it and fade; then a small dot with a number shows how many ideas sit directly under it. Unfold brings them out again.",
            "Both sides splits the branches: the first half to the right, the rest to the left. A map made from a pasted list with more than four branches starts that way.",
            "From then on every main branch stays on its side. A new main branch goes to the side with fewer branches, or to the right when they are even. The next idea beside a branch stays on that branch's side when the sides are even. Adding or removing a branch never makes another one jump across the middle.",
            "Each main branch takes its own colour, in this order: pink, orange, teal, blue, purple, green, red. Colour changes the whole branch.",
            "To put an idea in another place among its sisters, there are three ways.",
            "Keys: ⌘↑ and ⌘↓ move the chosen idea one place up or down. On the Mac they are also in the Board menu, as Move the Idea Up and Move the Idea Down. While you type in an idea on the Mac they move that idea, and you go on typing.",
            "Buttons: on the iPhone and iPad, Move up and Move down in the bar. A button is pale when the idea cannot go further that way.",
            "Dragging: drag the idea up or down between two of its sisters. A thin green line shows where it will land. Let go and it lands there.",
            "A main branch moves among the branches on its own side of the middle idea. At the top or the bottom of a side it crosses over to the other side. You can also drag a main branch straight into the other side's column.",
            "To hang an idea under another idea, with everything under it, drag it onto that idea. A dashed green frame shows where it will hang. It goes last under that idea. Aim at the middle of a sister to hang it under her; her upper and lower edge count as the gap beside her.",
            "On the iPhone and iPad, rest your finger on the idea for a moment (a quarter of a second) before you drag. A quick slide that starts on an idea moves the view instead, so an idea is never torn off by accident. With a mouse, a trackpad or the Pencil the idea follows at once.",
            "You cannot drop an idea onto one of its own ideas. Dropping it on the idea it already hangs under does nothing.",
            "To make an idea a map of its own, drag it well away from its family, about 120 points on the screen, and let go on empty board. A shorter drag springs back. The ideas right under it become main branches with colours of their own.",
            "A line at the bottom then says Made its own map, with an Undo button, in case you did not mean it.",
            "Drag the middle idea and the whole map comes along.",
            "Drop the middle idea onto an idea of another map and the whole map hangs under that idea: the two maps become one. Its ideas take the colour of the branch they are now in. Undo is offered here too. This is also how a torn-off branch goes back.",
            "The map arranges itself. You cannot place the ideas under the middle one by hand.",
            "Remove on an idea removes everything under it. Its parent is then chosen, so you can carry on typing.",
            "Drag a sticky note, a box or a text onto an idea and it becomes an idea under it. Break into notes turns an idea and everything under it into sticky notes. Make a mind map turns chosen notes into one map. See Turn into, tidy and sort.",
            "Balance: choose the middle of a mind map that has two or more branches. Balance (in the bar,  or behind … on a narrow bar; the right-click menu; Mac Board, Balance the Map) turns both sides on and spreads the main branches by size, the heaviest first onto the lighter side, a tie to the right. The order stays on each side. One Undo step.",
            "Fold branch: an idea with ideas under it has Fold branch and Unfold branch in the bar and menu, and on the Mac in the Board menu. A folded idea shows a pill such as +5 at its outer edge, counting every hidden idea; one press opens it. Unfold all opens every folded idea of the map.",
            "Number the steps: More, Number the steps. Numbers start at shapes that have arrows leaving but none arriving (the top one first, then left), follow the arrows one step after another, and sit in a round blue badge at the top left of each flowchart shape. Things no arrow reaches get none. A closed ring starts at its top left shape. Stop numbering the steps takes them away.",
            "Main branches only (0.11): choose the middle of a map and press Main branches only; every first idea with ideas under it folds. The button then says Open all and opens every folded idea of that map. On the Mac the Board menu has Main Branches Only and Open All Branches (any idea of the map can be chosen).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Shapes and arrows", text: [
            "There are eight shapes: Box, Round box, Oval, Diamond, Person, Document, Slanted box, Store. Put words in any of them.",
            "A Person is somebody who does something; the words stand under the figure. A Document is a paper, a form, a file. A Slanted box is something that goes in or comes out. A Store is a place where things are kept: a list, a shelf.",
            "To add a shape, press the shape tile in the tools. To pick another kind, hold the tile, or press the small corner mark on it (Mac and iPad). A plate shows all eight. Press one and it appears in the middle of the view, ready to type.",
            "The tile remembers your pick and shows its mark: the next plain press adds the same kind again. Diamond also keeps its own tile.",
            "On the iPhone the plate comes up from the bottom. On the Mac and the iPad it is a small pop-up. Cancel, or Escape, closes it.",
            "To change a shape into another kind, choose it and press Shape in its bar. The plate shows what it is now in full colour. Press another kind. Its middle, words, colour and arrows stay. A size you gave the shape stays. Only a shape still at its first size takes the new shape's own size.",
            "The new shapes do all a Box does: nine colours, To do, words typed in place, arrows, the green plus, Duplicate.",
            "A small dot stands on each side of a chosen shape that has no arrow yet. Press one and the next shape appears there, already joined by an arrow. On the Mac the dot grows to a plus when the pointer comes near; on the iPhone and iPad it always shows its plus. A side that already has an arrow has no dot.",
            "After a diamond or a round box comes a plain Box. Every other shape is followed by a copy of itself: same kind, size and colour. It stands 70 points away, or 100 when its arrow carries a word, and steps aside if that place is taken. It is ready to type.",
            "The first arrow out of a diamond says Yes and the second says No, by themselves. You can change the words like any others.",
            "Pull a plus onto another shape to join the two with an arrow. Let go on empty board and a new shape appears right there, joined. A dashed outline shows where it will land, in line with the shape it came from.",
            "With the keyboard: choose a shape and press Tab. The next shape appears with its arrow, in the direction the flow is running, ready to type. Type, press Tab again, and so on. It works while you type in a shape too. On the iPhone and iPad the bar above the screen keyboard has Next shape (on the iPhone just Next) for the same.",
            "⌘ with an arrow key makes the next shape in that direction, for example ⌘→ to go right.",
            "Press Tab on a diamond a second time for the No side. It goes down, or to the right when the flow runs up or down.",
            "Sticky notes have the four plus signs too. Free text has two: right and down. The plus signs are small, and shrink a little when you zoom out.",
            "The Arrow tool is the other way. Press it, then press the first shape, then the second. If a shape is already chosen, the arrow starts there.",
            "You can also pull from the first shape onto the second. The tool goes off by itself once the arrow is made.",
            "Press Cancel, press empty board, or press Escape to leave the arrow tool.",
            "An arrow can join shapes, notes, text, ideas, pictures and doors. It cannot join a frame: if you press a frame with the arrow tool on, the press is passed over and the tool stays on. The same arrow is never made twice. The head points at the second one.",
            "An arrow finds its own way. It leaves the side that faces the other shape, bends with soft corners, and goes round a shape, note or idea that stands in its way, 12 points off.",
            "Arrows follow when you move a shape. An arrow ends on the real edge of a shape: on the slanted side of a Slanted box, on the wave of a Document, at the shoulders of a Person.",
            "Several arrows on one side of a shape stand 14 points apart, so each can be seen and pressed. An arrow there and an arrow back are two lines side by side.",
            "Press an arrow once to choose it. The bar has 7 buttons: Words; Line, which opens Right angles, Straight, Curved; Ends, which opens One way, Both ways, No ends; Dashed; Turn round; Colour; Remove.",
            "One way has a head at the far end. Both ways has a head at each end. No ends is a plain line. Dashed turns the line into dashes; the heads stay solid.",
            "A chosen arrow shows a small dot at each end. Drag a dot to another side of the same shape, or onto another shape. A dashed line follows, and the shape the end would land on lights up. Let go on empty board and the end springs back.",
            "A side you chose like this is kept when the shapes are moved. Drag the dot back to the side the app would pick, and the arrow chooses its own side again.",
            "Words puts a few words on the arrow. Two quick presses on an arrow, or Return, do the same. The words stand beside the line near its start: above a level line, to the right of an upright one.",
            "Turn round swaps the two ends, so the head points the other way.",
            "Arrows start as Right angles, One way and grey. Colour has No colour and nine colours.",
            "To make a shape bigger, pull one of the four small squares at its corners. The opposite corner stays exactly where it is. Shapes also grow by themselves when the words need room: while you type, wider first (a box, a document and a store up to 300 points, an oval 320, a slanted box 330, a diamond 340, a person 220), then taller.",
            "By day every shape has a faint shadow, so it lies a little above the board.",
            "Remove on a shape also removes its arrows.",
            "Words on an arrow stand in the middle of the line, on a small patch in the board's colour. The box you type in is there too.",
            "A diamond's first arrow says Yes and its second says No by itself; you can change them. Arrows go round shapes in their way, keeping 12 points clear.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Sticky notes, text and frames", text: [
            "A sticky note is yellow. Choose it and press Colour for yellow, pink, blue, green, orange or purple. Words start at the top left. At night the notes keep bright paper and dark words.",
            "On the Mac, two quick clicks on empty board make a sticky note right there, whatever was chosen before: the first click lets go of what was chosen, the second makes the note. On the iPhone and iPad, two quick presses on empty board show the whole board instead.",
            "While you type in a sticky note, Tab keeps the words and makes the next note, and you go on typing in it. The next note has the same colour and size and stands 16 points to the right, top edges in line. If that place is taken, it goes under the first note of the row, 16 points below the tallest one, and further down if that is taken too. On the iPhone and iPad the bar above the screen keyboard has Next note (on the iPhone just Next) for this.",
            "A next note that you leave empty goes away again and leaves no step for Undo; the note before it is chosen.",
            "Free text sits on the board with no box. It is as wide as its words. Paragraphs pasted from another app become one text, with their lines kept; it wraps at 360 points.",
            "A frame is a big soft box with its name at the top left. It lies under everything else.",
            "A frame has nine colours. A new frame is teal. A frame cannot be marked To do and cannot have arrows.",
            "A thing belongs to the frame its middle is in. While you drag a thing, the frame under its middle lights up softly, so you see where it will belong.",
            "Drag the frame and everything that belongs to it comes along, mind maps too. Sizing the frame moves nothing else. A frame inside another frame travels with the bigger one only when it lies wholly inside.",
            "To choose or drag a frame, press its name band at the top, or its edge. Pressing inside it reaches the things inside, not the frame.",
            "Make a frame bigger with the small squares at its four corners. It cannot get smaller than 120 by 80.",
            "The bar of a chosen frame offers: Lanes, Colour, Duplicate, Words, Remove. See Lanes in frames.",
            "Two quick presses on a note, text or frame, or the Words button, let you change its words. Return on a chosen note, text or frame does the same.",
            "Press and hold a sticky note (right-click on the Mac) for Choose all of this colour: every sticky note of the board with the same paper is chosen.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Marks on ideas, legend, focus and show", text: [
            "Vote dots: choose a thing and press Vote (its mark is a note with a dot on its corner; Mac: ⌥⌘V, Edit, Vote Dot). Each press adds a small orange dot at its top right, up to 99; more than 5 shows 5 and a number. Press and hold, or right-click, gives Remove a dot and Clear dots. More: Sort by dots puts the dotted things in a row, most first, 16 points apart, from the first one's corner; pinned things stay. Clear all dots.",
            "Colour legend: More, Colour legend… . For each colour a field says What does yellow mean? (40 letters at most). Hide legend, Cancel, Save (Escape is Cancel). The menu line and the page's heading wear a mark: a small card with two colour dots. The card in the bottom left corner shows each coloured note's colour dot and words (only colours that have words); press it to change them. A note with no colour counts as yellow.",
            "Tick list: in a note, a line that begins with [ ] or [x] is drawn as a hand-drawn box and its words; a ticked one is a box with a tick and slightly greyer words. Press the box to tick or untick. The note's bar has Tick list (mark: two ticked lines; Mac Edit, Tick List): every line that has words becomes a tick line, or all go back to plain lines when they all were. To PARAGON a note goes as - [ ] and - [x] lines.",
            "Web address: the first http or https address in the words fetches the page's title once, after you stop typing (never while typing, never on a board you cannot change). Under the thing a small line shows a tiny arrow and the title (the tidied address while there is none), in blue. Press it, or use Open link, to open the browser.",
            "Pin in place: the button Pin in place and Unpin (Mac Edit, Pin in Place). A pinned thing is not moved by dragging, by the size knobs (hidden), by the arrow keys, Line up, Even gaps, Same size, Tidy into a grid or Sort by dots. A tiny pin shows at its corner while it is chosen.",
            "Background: More, Background: Plain, Dots (the standard, as before) or Squares (lines every 48 points, never closer than 24 on screen, fading when you zoom out below 40 %). Only on the live board, never in a picture that is sent or shared.",
            "Focus: choose a frame, then Focus (mark: frame corners round a ring with a dot; Mac: ⌥⌘F, Board, Focus on the Frame; also on the press-and-hold or right-click menu). The view glides to the frame and the board outside it fades to 25 %; things inside can still be changed. Not on a timeline or Who does what board. The pill Focus · name with Done, or Escape, ends it, and the view comes back.",
            "Show frames: More, Show frames (mark: frame corners round a play triangle; Mac: ⌥⌘P; grey when there is no frame). The frames come top rows first, then left to right, each zoomed to with some air round it; tools and the bar are hidden. The bottom bar says ‹ 2 of 5 › and Done. Next: arrow right, space or a swipe left; back: arrow left or a swipe right; Escape or Done ends it. During a show the top bar is hidden.",
            "Tidy into a grid: More (Mac ⌥⌘G). The chosen things, or when nothing is chosen the loose notes, go into a grid of about as many columns as the square root of the count, 16 points apart. Pinned things stay where they are. One Undo step.",
            "Small map: a small picture of the whole board, 120 by 80 points (90 by 60 on the iPhone), bottom right above the tools, things as tiny coloured boxes and the visible part outlined. It shows only when the things spread over more than twice the screen, and not while drawing, during a show, or on a timeline or Who does what board. Press or drag on it to jump there. More, Small map (mark: a board with a small map in its lower right corner) turns it on or off; it is on at first, and each device keeps its own choice.",
            "Colour names (0.11): when the legend names a colour, a sticky note's colour dots say that word (also read by VoiceOver) and a small line with the word shows under them. PARAGON's note puts the word after each sticky note of that colour, for example - Call Alex (Question). A note sent as a tick list is sent line by line, without the word. The legend card carries the legend mark at its top left.",
            "Tick sound (0.11): ticking a to-do box, or a tick line in a note, plays a soft two-note tick (not when unticking). Settings, Sounds, Tick sound switches it; on at first on iPhone and iPad, off on the Mac.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Turn into, tidy and sort", text: [
            "None of this is on a timeline or a Who does what board: they place their things themselves.",
            "Turn into: a note, a box or any other shape, a text or an idea can become another of these four kinds: Sticky note, Box, Text or Idea. It is in the menu of a thing (press and hold, or right-click), in the menu of several things, and in the bar behind the … button. The kind the thing already is is left out.",
            "What stays: the words, the To do tick, the place (its middle), the arrows and the colour. A blue box becomes a blue note; a colour that notes do not have changes to the nearest paper (teal becomes green, red becomes pink, grey and no colour become yellow). The new thing takes its kind's own size and grows with its words.",
            "Turned into an idea, a thing becomes the middle idea of a small map of its own. An idea turned into something else leaves its map: the ideas under it move up to the idea it hung under. The middle idea of a map with branches cannot be turned; Break into notes is the way for a whole map.",
            "Drag onto an idea: drag one sticky note, a plain Box or a text onto an idea. The idea wears a dashed green frame. Let go, and the thing becomes an idea under that idea, after the ideas already there. Its words, To do tick and arrows stay. A line says Now an idea, with Undo, for 6 seconds.",
            "Dropped on the middle idea it becomes a new main branch. It keeps its colour when branches have that colour; a plain yellow note or a box with no colour takes the next free branch colour. Dropped on any other idea it takes that branch's colour.",
            "Make a mind map: choose two or more sticky notes or texts and press Make a mind map in the bar (also in the menu of several things). A small page asks for The middle idea, filled in with the board's name, and says, for example, The 4 chosen notes become the branches of one mind map round it. Cancel or Save (Return saves, Escape cancels).",
            "Save: the notes become the main branches of one new map, in reading order (row by row from the top, each row from the left). The middle idea stands in the middle of where the notes lay. An empty name gives Ideas. More than four branches go to both sides.",
            "Notes that lie in a frame with a name hang under a branch that carries the frame's name. A frame that was chosen too and held nothing but these notes goes away; any other frame stays. Words, To do ticks and arrows stay. Afterwards the middle idea is chosen and in sight.",
            "Break into notes (in the menu of an idea): the idea and every idea under it become sticky notes in one column, 16 points apart, starting where the idea stood, in the order of the map. Each is 150 points wide and as tall as its words need, in the paper of its branch's colour. To do ticks and arrows stay; the rest of the map closes up. It works on a middle idea too: the whole map becomes notes.",
            "Colour for several: with several things chosen, Colour colours every chosen thing that can carry a colour at once (an idea colours its whole branch; texts, pictures and sketches are left alone). With a sticky note among them the dots are the six papers. The ring sits on a dot only when all chosen things show that colour.",
            "Tidy: with three or more sticky notes, shapes or texts chosen, Tidy lays the pile out as a neat grid where it lies. The top left corner stays, 16 points between the things, at most 5 side by side, the rows filled evenly (6 lie 3 and 3, 7 lie 4 and 3) and the reading order kept. Frames, pictures, sketches and mind maps among them stay where they are.",
            "By colour (behind the … button, and in the menu of several things): one column per colour, in the order of the colour dots, things without a colour last. Each column keeps the reading order. The top left corner stays.",
            "Each of these is one step for Undo.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Lanes in frames", text: [
            "Lanes split a frame into named rows or columns, for example Customer, Shop, Warehouse.",
            "Choose a frame and press Lanes. Pick Rows or Columns and how many, 2 to 5. No lanes takes them away again.",
            "The lanes share the frame evenly and stretch when you size the frame.",
            "Press a lane's name twice to type it. Return keeps it. Tab keeps it and goes on to the next lane. Escape leaves it as it was.",
            "Drag the line between two lanes to make one bigger and the other smaller. No lane gets narrower than 60 points.",
            "A thing belongs to the lane its middle is in. While you drag a thing, that lane lights up.",
            "To choose or drag a frame with lanes, you can also press a lane's name band.",
            "In PARAGON, a note lists the things of a frame lane by lane, under the lane's name. A project makes one group of tasks for each lane, named after the frame and the lane. Once an idea on the board is marked To do, only the marked shapes in a lane become tasks; the others stay as plain lines.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Lining up and guide lines", text: [
            "Things fall in line by themselves. Drag a shape, note, text, frame or the middle idea of a mind map near another thing. When an edge or the middle comes within 6 points of a neighbour's, the thing jumps onto that line.",
            "A thin pink line shows what is in line. When the gap to its neighbours is the same as the gap between them, small pink marks show the equal gaps. They vanish when you let go.",
            "The same happens when you pull the corner square to size a thing, and when you pull a new shape out of a green plus.",
            "On the Mac, hold ⌘ while dragging to place a thing freely.",
            "Ideas inside a mind map are placed by the map and do not take part. Frames catch by their edges only. The arrow keys move by 10 points with no catch.",
            "To tidy several things at once, choose two or more. The bar then shows Line up, Even gaps and Same size, and from three notes, shapes or texts also Tidy (see Turn into, tidy and sort).",
            "Line up puts them on one line. Spread more sideways than up and down, they become a row. Otherwise they become a column.",
            "Even gaps needs three or more. The two outer things stay. The ones between are moved so every gap is the same.",
            "Same size gives every shape, note and frame the size of the largest one. Each keeps its middle. Ideas and free text are as big as their words and stay as they are.",
            "Undo takes each of these back in one step. On the Mac the three are also in the Board menu.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Choosing, moving, sizing, copying, removing, undo", text: [
            "Press a thing once to choose it. A green line goes round it. Press empty board, or press Escape, to choose nothing.",
            "On the Mac, hold Shift and press to add a thing to the choice or take it out. Drag on empty board to draw a box that chooses everything it touches. A frame is chosen only when the box holds all of it.",
            "With a trackpad or a mouse on the iPad it is the same: Shift and a click adds to the choice, and a click and drag on empty board draws the choosing box.",
            "With a finger on the iPhone and iPad, press Choose more in the bar of the chosen thing. Then each press adds a thing to the choice, or takes it out. Press Choose more again, or press empty board, to stop.",
            "⌘A chooses everything on the board.",
            "With several things chosen, the bar says how many and offers Line up, Even gaps, Same size, Tidy (from three), Colour (when one of them carries a colour), Make a mind map (from two notes or texts), Duplicate, a … button (By colour, Turn into), To do and Remove, and Choose more on the iPhone and iPad.",
            "Drag a chosen thing to move it. If several are chosen, they all move.",
            "Ideas inside a map are placed by the map. Drag one to another place. See Mind maps.",
            "With things chosen, the arrow keys move them by 10 points. Hold Shift for 40. (With one idea chosen, the arrow keys walk the map instead.)",
            "Duplicate in the bar, or ⌘D, makes a second one a little down and to the right. It is chosen. Arrows between copied things are copied too, and an idea comes with everything under it. Duplicate used to be called Copy in the bar. Real Copy, Cut and Paste are new. See Copy, cut and paste.",
            "Copying one idea inside a map makes its next sister idea.",
            "Remove, or the Delete key, takes the chosen things away. It does not ask first, because Undo brings them back.",
            "Undo and Redo are the two buttons at the top (on the iPhone, at the bottom left), or ⌘Z and Shift ⌘Z. They keep the last 120 steps of the board you have open.",
            "On the iPhone and iPad, the device's own gestures work too: swipe three fingers to the left to undo and to the right to redo, tap with three fingers for the device's small bar, or shake the device for its Undo question. One swipe always takes back exactly one step.",
            "While you type, these gestures undo the typing, as before. While a third finger is down, and for 0.3 seconds after, two fingers do not slide the board, so the end of a swipe never shoves it.",
            "Opening another board starts the step list over. Undo does not work while you type.",
            "Undo after Send to PARAGON never makes a task go twice: what went to PARAGON is kept through Undo and Redo.",
            "The Undo list: press and hold the Undo button (right-click on the Mac), press ⌥⌘Z, or on the Mac choose Edit, Undo List…. It shows your last steps, newest first, for example Moved 3 things · 21:40, with the line Press a step to go back to just before it. You can still Redo afterwards. The list starts again when another device's newer version replaces the board.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Moving around", text: [
            "iPhone and iPad: slide with one finger on empty board to move the view. A quick slide that starts on an idea moves the view too.",
            "Put two fingers on the board to slide and zoom at the same time. The board follows your fingers and grows or shrinks round the point between them.",
            "Two fingers never choose or move anything. If your first finger nudged a shape before the second one landed, the shape goes back.",
            "Press empty board twice quickly to see the whole board (iPhone and iPad).",
            "With a trackpad or a mouse on the iPad: two fingers on the trackpad, or the mouse wheel, slide the board. Pinch on the trackpad, or hold ⌘ and scroll, to zoom round the pointer.",
            "The Pencil chooses and moves things like a finger. A hand resting on the glass while the Pencil is down is ignored.",
            "On empty board the Pencil draws at once. See Drawing by hand.",
            "Once you have moved or zoomed the view, it stays where you left it, also when the keyboard comes up or the window changes size.",
            "Mac: two-finger scroll moves the view. Pinch, or hold ⌘ and scroll, to zoom around the pointer. Dragging on empty board draws a choosing box instead.",
            "Mac: hold Space and the pointer turns into an open hand. Drag, and the board slides with the hand, wherever the drag starts, also on a shape. Let go of Space and the pointer is an arrow again.",
            "The three zoom buttons: Smaller, Bigger, and Show the whole board. Smaller and Bigger go a quarter at a time.",
            "The view glides instead of jumping. A zoom step (the buttons, ⌘+, ⌘- and ⌘0) takes 0.22 seconds, and the point in the middle stays where it is while the percent counts along. Show the whole board (the button, ⌘1, the menu, two presses on empty board) takes 0.30 seconds. Bringing a new idea into sight takes 0.18 seconds.",
            "Press a zoom button again while it glides and the next step counts from the end of the running one, so three quick presses are three full steps. A finger, a click, a scroll, a pinch or a key stops a glide at once, where it is. A board opens where it was left, without a glide. A timeline keeps its own steps.",
            "The zoom goes from 20 % to 300 %. Show the whole board never goes bigger than 100 %, and never smaller than 50 %. On a small screen, the middle of a large board shows and you move around.",
            "On the iPhone a big board opens at 75 % on its main idea, so the words can be read. A big Who does what board opens at 75 % on its first name. A board that fits at 75 % or more opens showing all of it.",
            "On the iPhone, when the view is smaller than 100 % and you start typing, it glides to 100 % round the thing you type in, in 0.18 seconds. When you finish, it glides back.",
            "When you add an idea, the view moves just enough to show it.",
            "With Reduce Motion switched on in the device's settings, every glide is a jump, as before.",
            "With a keyboard: ⌘+ and ⌘- zoom, ⌘0 shows the board at its real size (100 %), and ⌘1 shows the whole board. On a timeline, ⌘0 goes back to the standard size, the Weeks scale.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Timeline board", text: [
            "A timeline is a plan over time: one row for each thing to do, a bar from its first day to its last. Start one with New board, then Timeline. It opens with a small example that begins today.",
            "The names stand in a column at the left and the dates in a band at the top. Both stay in place while you slide.",
            "At the bottom are four tools: Bar, Milestone, Phase, Comes after.",
            "Bar (or the key B) adds a row. The bar appears under the chosen row, starting today, and you type its name straight away.",
            "Milestone (M) is a single day, shown as a purple diamond.",
            "Phase (P) is a heading. The rows under it, down to the next phase, belong to it and get its soft colour. The phase shows one thin bar from its first to its last day.",
            "To move a bar, drag its middle sideways to another day. Drag it up or down to put it in another row. While you drag, its dates show above it. A bar is always whole days.",
            "To make a bar longer or shorter, choose it and drag the round grip at one of its ends. A bar is never shorter than one day.",
            "Comes after: choose a bar and pull the small dot behind its end onto the bar that comes after it. Or press Comes after (A), then the bar that comes first, then the one after it. An arrow joins them.",
            "From then on, when you move or lengthen the first bar, the ones after it are pushed along. A line at the bottom says how many moved. A bar cannot start before the bar it comes after has ended.",
            "To take an arrow away, press it and press Remove.",
            "The next bar: choose a bar and press Return. A new bar appears in the row below, starting the day after, already joined, and you type its name.",
            "To rename, press a name or a bar twice, or choose it and just type. On the iPhone, choose it and press Name. Return ends the typing, Escape cancels it.",
            "To reorder the rows, drag a name in the left column up or down.",
            "Drag a phase by its name and it carries every row under it, as one block. The block only lands where it splits no other phase: before another phase, at the very end, or among the rows above the first phase. Dragged only a little into another phase it stays; dragged past that phase's middle it jumps past the whole of it. A bar or a milestone still moves alone.",
            "Dates: choose a bar and press Dates. Set Starts and Ends, then Save. Changing Starts moves the whole bar and its length stays. Changing Ends makes it longer or shorter. Cancel, or Escape on the Mac, leaves it as it was.",
            "The bar for a chosen row shows its dates in words and offers Name, Colour (nine colours), Dates, Done, To do, Duplicate and Remove. It is orange, with Remove in red. A phase has no Done.",
            "Done: marks the bar or milestone done today. The bar turns paler, a tick in its own colour stands at its end, and its name in the left column turns grey. Done is then in full colour; press it again to take the mark off. With several rows chosen, Done marks them all, or takes the mark off all when all were done. A copy of a done bar is not done.",
            "The day it was done: choose a done bar or milestone and the Done button says the day, for example Done 18 Nov, with the year when it is not this year, for example Done 3 Dec 2025. With several rows chosen it just says Done. The Dates page of a done bar or milestone has one more line under the dates, for example Done on 18 Nov. A bar that was ticked off only in PARAGON shows no day, because Seedboard keeps none for it.",
            "The day on the board: a done bar or milestone also shows its day as a small grey label a little past its end, for example 18 Nov (with the year when it is not this year). Taking the Done mark off removes it; a bar ticked off only in PARAGON has none. The board's picture, in PARAGON and in Share as Picture and PDF, shows it too.",
            "The day on the board, 0.9: the small label now sits on a soft rounded background in the board's colour, so the red Today line never runs through the words. Next to the day it says how it compares with the bar's planned last day: 18 Nov · 8 days late (in faint red) or 18 Nov · 3 days early (in faint green). Done on the planned day, it says only the day. One day is 1 day late. The Dates page line says, for example, Done on 18 Nov · 8 days late.",
            "Today: the button Today in the zoom group at the bottom, the key T, or on the Mac View, Go to Today, brings the timeline back to today. Today stands a third of the way in from the names.",
            "Shading: weekends are shaded softly in Days and in Weeks, and the Swedish public holidays a little stronger. Months have none.",
            "Months: the marks say, for example, Jan 2027. A timeline longer than about half a year that you never moved opens in Months. Days, Weeks and Months are in the zoom group, and pinching changes them too.",
            "Phases fold: the small arrow before a phase's name folds its rows into one band with the count, for example 4 bars, 1 milestone. Press again to open. Fold all phases and Unfold all are in More, in the Mac File menu and in the right-click menu. What a fold hides is no longer chosen; a new row under a folded phase goes after the hidden ones. The picture of the board shows every row.",
            "Add to Calendar…: in More (on the Mac also File, Add to Calendar… and the right-click menu). The page lists Milestones and then Bars, each with a tick box and its days; new and changed milestones are ticked at first, bars never. Return is Add, Escape is Cancel. Adding the same thing again makes a second event. Cancel or Add. Each becomes an all-day event with the note From the Seedboard board “name”. Seedboard only adds, never reads your calendar. What was added shows Added 18 Nov, and a thing whose days changed shows Day changed. It is remembered on this device only. If the calendar says no, a red line says where to allow it.",
            "By itself (0.13): in Settings, Calendar, the switch Timeline days in Calendar (off at first) puts every milestone and bar into its own calendar, Seedboard, and keeps it right: move, rename or remove a thing and its event follows. A milestone is a one-day event, a bar one all-day event over its days. Done things stay, with (done) after the name. Put-away boards are left out. Turn it on on one device only; the calendar brings the days to the others. Switching it off takes the events out again. While it is on, Add to Calendar… only says Your timeline days go to the Seedboard calendar by themselves (Settings ▸ Calendar).",
            "A done bar goes to PARAGON already ticked, with the day it was done. It never goes to the Inbox as a new to-do, has no Start: reminder and is not offered by Add what is new. See Send to PARAGON.",
            "Late: a bar whose last day is before today and that is not done has a red end and a red name. A late milestone has a red edge and a red name.",
            "The part of a bar that lies before the red Today line and is not done is darker, so a bar in work is dark up to today.",
            "In the corner above the names stands one line, for example 3 of 8 done · 1 late · ends 20 Feb. Bars and milestones are counted. 1 late shows only when something is late, in red. The end is the last day of the latest bar, with the year when it is not this year. The same line is in the picture for PARAGON and in the small pictures.",
            "Catch up: while something is late, a red button Catch up stands right after Today (on the iPhone only its mark). It stretches every late bar to end today, starts a late bar that comes after another late bar right after it, moves a late milestone to today, and pushes along what comes after. Done bars are not touched. A line says, for example, 4 bars moved. The plan now ends 27 Feb (was 20 Feb). One Undo takes it all back.",
            "A bar whose task is ticked off in PARAGON is drawn done too, counts as done in the line, is never late and is left alone by Catch up. Taking the Done mark off such a bar says It is ticked off in PARAGON, so it stays done.",
            "To do marks the row with the purple to-do ring (see To do). Duplicate puts a copy in the row below. Delete on the keyboard removes.",
            "Instead of a zoom in percent there are three scales: Days (a day is 28 points wide), Weeks (a week is 56) and Months (a month is about 72). The two zoom buttons step between them. So do a pinch, and ⌘+ and ⌘-.",
            "On the Weeks scale every Monday carries its week number, for example w 49, small and grey above its date. Weeks are counted as in Sweden: a week begins on Monday, and week 1 holds the year's first Thursday.",
            "A timeline opens as you left it: the same scale, the same days beside the names, the rows slid as far as before. Each device remembers its own. If you last showed the whole timeline, or never moved it, it opens whole.",
            "The frame button shows the whole timeline, at the closest scale at which everything fits.",
            "On the Mac, ⌘0 goes back to the standard size, the Weeks scale, and the day in the middle stays put. ⌘1 shows the whole timeline.",
            "A red line marks today. Today (T) brings it into view.",
            "To slide, drag an empty place or the dates. On the Mac, scroll with two fingers. On the iPad with a trackpad or a mouse, two fingers or the wheel slide it too, and ⌘ with the wheel zooms round the pointer.",
            "Keys: ↑ and ↓ choose the row above or below. ← and → move the chosen bar a day, with Shift a week. Return makes the next bar. Space renames. Delete removes. Escape chooses nothing. ⌘A chooses all rows. ⌘D copies.",
            "Several rows can be chosen, with Shift on the Mac or Choose more elsewhere. They move together by days.",
            "A list pasted onto a timeline becomes bars: ⌘V on the Mac, More, Paste a list as bars, or on the Mac Board, Paste a List as Bars (Shift ⌘V). Each line is a bar of 7 days, each coming after the one before. A line with lines pushed in under it becomes a phase, and the lines under it its bars. Bullets, numbers and tick boxes are taken away.",
            "With nothing chosen the bars go under the last row and start today. With a row chosen they go right under it, and after a bar or a milestone the chain goes on from it. A line says, for example, 5 bars pasted. or 7 bars in 2 phases pasted. The new bars are chosen. One Undo takes them away.",
            "A board that is a mind map or a flow can become a timeline. See Make a timeline of it.",
            "To PARAGON as a project: each phase becomes a heading, each bar a task. The tasks stand in date order.",
            "PARAGON has no start day for a task. So a bar goes over with its last day as the due date, and its first day in words: Foundation (starts 8 Dec). A milestone is a task on its day.",
            "The project's own due date is the last day on the timeline. What comes after what is listed under Notes.",
            "To PARAGON as a note: the picture of the timeline, then every row with its dates, for example Permit: 1 Dec – 5 Dec 2026 · 5 days, then what comes after what.",
            "Only the to-do boxes: as on every board, the rows marked To do go to the Inbox.",
            "A row sent as a to-do keeps its days in the Inbox, for example Permit (starts 1 Dec), due 5 Dec. On a timeline's Send page the switch Also remind me when a bar starts adds a Start: task on the first day of every bar longer than 3 days. See Send to PARAGON.",
            "On a timeline that was sent, the purple line from PARAGON sits at the bottom, just above the tools, because the top holds the dates.",
            "Dates page (0.11): a year before 1900 or after 2200 is refused with a red line, Pick a date between the years 1900 and 2200. The timeline never draws more than 1500 date marks. The small picture of your own timeline starter shows its dates moved to from today.",
            "Done and PARAGON (0.11): when a bar or milestone that was sent to PARAGON as a task is marked Done, PARAGON ticks that task the next time it opens (PARAGON 341 or newer; only if exactly one open task has the same words). Taking the Done mark off here changes nothing in PARAGON.",
            "Done and PARAGON switches (0.13): Settings, PARAGON has Done ticks the PARAGON task (on at first; off means Seedboard leaves PARAGON alone) and Read back from PARAGON (on at first; off means the lines about PARAGON, such as 5 of 13 done, disappear).",
            "With Differentiate Without Colour a late bar has slanted white strokes on its red end and a late milestone a dashed outline.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Make a timeline of it", text: [
            "More, Make a timeline of it… (on the Mac also File, Make a Timeline of It…) makes a new timeline board from an ordinary board, called, for example, Summer cabin timeline. It opens at once. The board it was made of stays as it is. Nothing is asked.",
            "It follows the same rule as As a project, so you get as many bars as the project would have tasks: a main branch with ideas under it becomes a phase, each idea under it a bar in that phase, in the map's order and the branch's colour. A main branch with nothing under it becomes a bar above the first phase, and so does a note or a text marked To do.",
            "A flow shape with words becomes a bar, and an arrow from one shape to another makes the second bar come after the first. A frame with lanes gives one phase per lane. The middle idea gives the name, not a bar.",
            "The ticks decide as for a project: with nothing ticked, every idea and every shape becomes a bar; with at least one idea ticked, only the ticked things do. With things chosen, the choice decides instead.",
            "Every bar is 7 days long and starts today. Inside a phase each bar comes after the one before, joined by an arrow. Then drag them to their days.",
            "A line says what was made, for example 12 bars in 3 phases. Drag them to their days. When things stayed behind it says so: 4 bars in 2 phases. 4 things without a tick stayed on the board.",
            "If nothing can become a bar, no board is made, and a red line says Nothing here can become a bar. Bars are made of ideas, shapes and things marked To do.",
            "It is not offered on a timeline, on a Who does what board, or while a starter is being changed, and it is grey on an empty board.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Drawing by hand", text: [
            "On the iPad with the Apple Pencil: just draw on empty board. The Pencil on a shape, note or idea chooses or moves it, as a finger does. A finger never draws by itself.",
            "With a finger, the mouse or a trackpad: press Draw (on the Mac, the key S), the last tool at the bottom. Now a drag draws, also across things. Two fingers still slide and zoom the board. Press Draw again, Done or Escape when you are finished.",
            "The plate above the tools: Pen or Rubber, three thicknesses (Thin 2 points, Medium 4, Thick 7.5), and 10 colours, plain ink first and then the 9 board colours. Choosing a thickness or a colour takes the pen again. The thickness and colour you used last come back next time on that device.",
            "Rubber: rub across a line and the whole line goes. Everything you rub out in one go comes back with one Undo.",
            "Lines drawn within 5 seconds of each other and no more than 40 points apart are one sketch. Wait a little longer, or draw somewhere else, and a new sketch begins.",
            "A sketch is chosen only by pressing one of its lines, never anywhere inside its box. So a note or a word inside a drawn ring is still easy to press. A finger catches a line within 22 points on the screen, the mouse or the Pencil within 6.",
            "The Pencil over a sketch you already made adds to it. To choose a sketch with the Pencil, tap one of its lines; then you can drag it. A finger chooses and moves a sketch at any time.",
            "A sketch is moved, copied, put in a frame, lined up, removed and undone like a sticky note. Drag the small knob at its corner and its lines stretch. Its bar has Duplicate, Choose more and Remove. It has no words, no To do and no colour button.",
            "Each line you draw is one step for Undo.",
            "The Pencil feels its pressure: pressing lightly makes the line up to 35 % thinner, pressing hard up to 35 % thicker.",
            "Double-tap the Pencil (or squeeze a Pencil Pro) and it does what you chose in Settings, Apple Pencil: switch between tool and eraser swaps pen and rubber; show colour palette opens or closes the Draw plate; off does nothing. When the Pencil rubs and no plate is showing, a small line says The Pencil rubs out, with a Pen button.",
            "If Only Draw with Apple Pencil is on in the iPad's Settings, fingers never draw. A finger slides the board even while Draw is on, and the plate says so.",
            "A line keeps a colour name, so it changes with day and night like everything else. Plain ink is dark by day and light by night.",
            "The board's count line counts sketches, for example 2 notes · 3 sketches.",
            "Sent to PARAGON, a sketch is in the picture. In the note or project text it is one plain line under Notes: A sketch (see the picture). A sketch never gets the To do tick and never becomes a task.",
            "Seedboard 0.1 shows a board with a sketch on it, but cannot change it.",
            "To write into a box with the Pencil, open its typing field (two quick presses on the thing). The iPad's own Scribble should then work there. Writing straight onto a shape, without opening it first, is built but switched off for now: it needs a real Pencil to be tried.",
            "0.9, the plate has more: Pen, Highlighter (key H; mark: a broad-tipped pen over a band), Rubber and Lasso (key L; mark: a rope loop with a loose end); one is on at a time. The keys work while you draw.",
            "Highlighter: a wide, see-through stripe (35 % of the colour, about three times the thick pen), in yellow, green, pink or blue. It always lies under the ordinary pencil lines of the same drawing.",
            "Rubber: Small, Medium and Large choose how near a line it must pass. Medium is as before. It still takes whole lines.",
            "Lasso: draw a loop round pencil lines. Those with more than half of their length inside are chosen and move together; if only some lines of a sketch were chosen they are split off into a sketch of their own. A loop smaller than 12 points counts as a tap.",
            "Make typed: choose a sketch and press Make typed (mark: a squiggle turning into a typed T; Mac: Make Sketch Typed). The handwriting is read on the device and becomes a Text thing in the same place under the same name, so arrows stay. If nothing is read: No words found in this sketch.",
            "Ruler (0.11; its own mark, a slanted ruler with marks, since 0.12): Draw plate, the ruler mark. A straight edge of 360 points lies in the middle of the board. Move it by its middle dot, turn it with two fingers on the middle dot or by the round dot at its end. A line drawn within 16 points of the edge the whole way comes out straight on the edge; one that wanders off stays as drawn. It is never saved and never in a picture. It goes when you leave Draw, or press Ruler again.",
            "Fade (0.11; its own mark, a pencil line fading into dots, since 0.12): Draw plate, the fade mark shows every pencil line at 30 % so notes on top can be read. Press again, or press Show in the small line Pencil lines are faded after the plate is closed. Nothing is saved.",
            "Read this (0.11; its own mark since 0.12: a scribble and a note with lines): choose a sketch and press Read this; the words found come up as a yellow sticky note 16 points to its right, and the sketch stays. One Undo takes the note away. No words: the line No words found in this sketch. Make typed still replaces the sketch.",
            "Rubber size (0.11): while you rub, the ring shows its size in words: Small, Medium or Large (20, 40 or 80 points on the screen).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Tidy shapes", text: [
            "Tidy shapes turns a box, an oval or a diamond drawn by hand into a real one, and a line drawn from one thing to another into a real arrow. It comes switched OFF, because it still has to be tried with a real Pencil.",
            "The switch is in the Draw plate, between the colours and Done: Tidy shapes. Soft when off, full colour when on. A press turns it on, another press off. Each device remembers its own. On the Mac, rest the pointer on it to read what it does.",
            "With it on: draw a box, an oval or a diamond in one line, hold the tip still for a moment at the end (0.4 seconds), then lift. The line becomes a real Box, Oval or Diamond of that size and place, in the colour you drew with.",
            "Draw one line from one thing to another, hold still at the end, lift: it becomes an arrow from the first thing to the second.",
            "Lift without the pause and the line stays the sketch it is, exactly as before. So the pause is the way to say: tidy this one.",
            "One Undo gives the raw line back. Redo makes it the shape again.",
            "Only a line that begins a new sketch is looked at, so a drawing you are still making is never touched. A loop drawn round something that is already there stays a ring. A triangle, a scribble, a spiral or a small loop like handwriting stay sketches.",
            "It works for whatever draws: the Pencil on empty board, and a finger or the mouse while Draw is on. So it can be tried on the Mac with the mouse: hold the button still for a moment before letting go.",
            "Not on a timeline or a Who does what board.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Pictures", text: [
            "A picture is a photo or a screenshot that lies on the board like a note. You can move it, make it bigger or smaller, copy it, remove it and undo. It travels to your other devices with the board.",
            "Press the Picture tool, the ninth tool. On the iPhone and iPad Apple's photo picker opens: choose up to 12 pictures at once. No permission question is asked. On the Mac a window called Choose a picture opens: choose one or several files and press Choose.",
            "On the iPhone and iPad, hold the Picture tile for a moment (or, on the iPad, press its small corner mark) and a plate called Add a picture shows three ways: Photos, Take a photo and Scan a page. Cancel closes it.",
            "Take a photo opens the camera. Use Photo puts the photo on the board, chosen, at a free place near the middle of the view. The first time, the device asks once whether Seedboard may use the camera.",
            "Scan a page opens the device's page scanner: it finds the paper or the whiteboard and straightens it. Several pages can be scanned in one go. Save puts every page on the board as a picture of its own. At most 12 pages land; with more, a line says, for example, A scan puts 12 pages on a board at most. The first 12 of 15 are here.",
            "A device without a camera says This device has no camera.",
            "Pictures can also come from other apps through their Share button. See Sharing to Seedboard.",
            "Paste: ⌘V, Paste in the Edit menu, or Paste in the board's menu puts a screenshot or a copied picture in the middle of the view. On the Mac, picture files copied in the Finder come in too. If you copied words, the words win: a table or a web page you copy brings a picture of itself along.",
            "Drop: drag a picture file, or a picture from another app, onto the board (Mac and iPad). It lands where you let go. Several pictures land 28 points further down and right, one after the other.",
            "Mac: press P with nothing chosen, or choose Insert, Picture…. Right-click on empty board and choose Picture…, and the picture lands where you pressed.",
            "A new picture is laid at most 320 by 320 points big and keeps its shape. A tiny one is made at least 120 long. It is chosen at once.",
            "To make a picture bigger or smaller, drag one of its corner squares. It keeps its shape and the opposite corner stays where it is. It never gets smaller than 44 by 28 points.",
            "Arrows can start and end at a picture, as at a shape. Line up and Even gaps work on pictures. Same size leaves them alone, because a picture keeps its own shape.",
            "The bar of a chosen picture has Caption, Crop, Duplicate, Choose more (iPhone and iPad) and Remove. Its menu has Caption, Crop, Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Arrow from here and Remove. A picture has no colour and no To do. A letter key does not start typing.",
            "Caption: a picture can have a line of words under it, for example After the makeover. Press Caption in the bar or in the menu (press and hold, or right-click), or press twice quickly on the picture. The typing starts right under the picture. Return ends it. Escape cancels it. Shift and Return make a new line. Empty words mean no caption.",
            "The caption stands a little under the picture, in the middle, as wide as the picture, in small grey letters. Longer words wrap onto more lines. The caption moves, grows and is copied with its picture. Press on the caption and the picture is chosen. The board's whole view, the picture for PARAGON, Share and Export, and the small pictures in the list all leave room for it.",
            "A caption is found by Search boards. Copy the board as text writes it, for example A picture: After the makeover. A picture without words is still written A picture.",
            "Crop: show only a part of a picture. Press Crop in the bar or in the menu. A page called Crop the picture opens. It shows the whole picture, with a frame over it and a round grip in each corner. Everything outside the frame is dimmed. Drag a grip to change the frame, or drag inside the frame to move it. The frame stays inside the picture and is never very small.",
            "The page has Cancel (Escape on the Mac), Whole picture and Save. Whole picture makes the frame cover everything. Save is in full colour. The page opens with the part the picture shows now.",
            "After Save the picture on the board shows only that part, at the same size as before. The part you kept stays exactly where it was. The picture itself is never cut. Press Crop again and Whole picture, then Save, and everything is back. One Undo takes a crop back too.",
            "If you make a cropped picture bigger or smaller by its corner, it keeps its cropped shape. Duplicate, Copy and Paste take the crop and the caption along. Crop is grey while a picture says Picture not here yet.",
            "The room kept for a caption is a careful guess. On a very narrow picture with long words the real words can take one line more, and the last line can reach a little past the edge that Show the whole board measures. Line up and Even gaps look at the picture only, not at its caption.",
            "The board's count line says 1 picture, or 2 pictures.",
            "Picture not here yet: when a picture has not arrived on this device yet, a soft plate with a picture mark says so. The board fills in by itself when the picture comes. While a picture is only being read, the plate shows the picture mark without words.",
            "A board can hold many photos and they all stay in sight. The board keeps a lighter copy of each picture at hand, and reads the full picture again when you zoom in on it, so it stays sharp.",
            "If you close a board while its pictures are still loading, they are not put on it. A red line says The pictures were not laid: the board was closed. This keeps a closed board from ever saving over newer changes.",
            "If something cannot be read as a picture, a line says That could not be read as a picture. For several, it says, for example, 3 could not be read as pictures.",
            "A picture cannot be put on a Timeline board or on a Who does what board. The line says: A picture cannot be put on this board.",
            "A big picture is made smaller when you put it on: its long side is at most 2048 dots. A see-through picture gets a white background. The picture is saved once and never changed. Pictures are kept on all your devices, next to your boards.",
            "Removing a picture from a board never deletes the picture itself, so Undo always works. A picture that no board uses any more is tidied away after 30 days, and only when no board, no removed board and no safety copy and no starter uses it. Seedboard checks this once, a little after it starts. If anything is unclear, it deletes nothing.",
            "Sent to PARAGON, a picture is in the board's picture. In the note or project text it is one plain line under Notes: A picture (see the picture), or with a caption, A picture: After the makeover (see the picture). It never becomes a task.",
            "Seedboard 0.2 shows a board with a picture on it, but cannot change it. Seedboard 0.3 and older show a board with a cropped picture, but cannot change it.",
            "Draw on it: choose a picture and press Draw on it (mark: a picture with a pencil at its corner; Mac: Draw on Picture). The Draw tool opens and says Drawing on the picture. Press Done when you are finished. The lines are kept in the picture, from its corner: they move, size and crop with it, and the picture clips them.",
            "Words in pictures: each picture is read once on your device when a board opens, and the words are kept with the board. Board search finds them; a board found only by them says · in a picture.",
            "Captions (0.11): the room under a picture is measured with the caption's own lettering, so nothing overlaps. Line up and Even gaps count a picture and its caption as one thing.",
            "Copy words and Words to a note (0.11): a chosen picture that has found words has two more buttons in its bar and menu. Copy words puts them on the clipboard (Words copied). Words to a note makes a yellow sticky note 24 points to the right of the picture and its caption, chosen. Neither shows when there are no words.",
            "At most 12 sharp full-size photos are open at once; the others show their small copy until they are among the 12 most recently in view.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Who does what", text: [
            "A Who does what board shows who asks whom for what, and in which order. The names stand in a row at the top, and the messages run from top to bottom, numbered by the app. Start one with New board, then Who does what. It opens with a small example: Alex, Bank, Builder.",
            "Each name stands in a box of its own colour (the first 8 names get blue, teal, orange, purple, green, pink, red, yellow), with a thin dashed line hanging down. The lines stand 150 points apart, and further apart only where the words between them need room. Nothing is moved by hand.",
            "A message is a level arrow from one dashed line to another, with its words above it and a number in a small round mark. It takes the colour of the name it comes from. An answer is drawn with dashes and an open head. A message to itself is a small loop on the name's own line.",
            "A note is a soft band across all the lines. A section is a heading on a thin line across the whole diagram, for a new part. Neither is numbered.",
            "At the bottom are four tools: Who, Message, Note, Section.",
            "Add a name: press Who (or the key W). The new name appears at the right end of the row and you type it at once. Press Tab while typing and the next name begins beside it. Return ends the typing.",
            "Add a message by pulling: put the pointer or your finger on the dashed line under a name and pull sideways to another dashed line. A dashed line follows, the name you head for gets a dashed ring, and a thin line shows between which two messages the new one will stand. The height where you let go decides its place. Then type its words.",
            "Or without pulling: choose a name, press Message to…, and press the name it goes to. Or press Message (M), then the name it comes from, then the name it goes to. Such a message goes under the last one.",
            "The next message: choose a message and press Return (or Tab while you type its words). The next message appears right under it, going the other way. After a question it is an answer, after an answer a new question.",
            "Answer: choose a message and press Answer. Press it again to make it a plain message. A message you pull back the other way right under a question becomes an answer by itself.",
            "A message to itself: choose the name, press Message to…, and press the same name again. Or pull out from its dashed line and back onto it in a small loop.",
            "Turn round: the chosen message now comes from the name it went to.",
            "Another order: drag a message, note or section up or down. A thin line shows where it will land, and the numbers follow. On the Mac you can also hold Option and press ↑ or ↓. On the iPhone and iPad rest your finger on the message for a moment before you drag (a quick slide moves the view), or press Move up or Move down.",
            "Another place in the row: drag a name box sideways. A thin upright line shows where it will stand, and its messages come along. On the Mac: Option with ← or →.",
            "A note: press Note (N). It makes a soft band across the lines, under the chosen message or at the end. A section: press Section (S).",
            "Change the words: press a name or a message twice, or choose it and just type. On the iPhone choose it and press Name or Words. Return ends the typing, Escape cancels it.",
            "Colour: a name has its own colour, and a message takes the colour of the name it comes from. Choose a message and press Colour to give it a colour of its own. The first dot, No colour, gives it back the colour of its name.",
            "The bar of a chosen message: Words, Answer, Turn round, Colour, To do, Duplicate, Remove. The bar of a chosen name: Name, Colour, Message to…, Remove. On the iPhone and iPad a message also has Move up and Move down.",
            "To do marks a message with the purple to-do ring (see To do), and it goes to PARAGON as a task. Duplicate puts a copy right under the message. Remove, or the Delete key, takes it away.",
            "Removing a name removes every message from it and to it too. A line at the bottom says how many went, with Undo, for example: Bank went with its 2 messages.",
            "Zoom and slide work as on every board. The frame button shows the whole diagram (⌘1); ⌘0 shows it at its real size. On the iPad with a trackpad or a mouse, two fingers or the wheel slide it, and ⌘ with the wheel zooms round the pointer. When you slide down a long diagram, the name boxes stay pinned at the top.",
            "Keys: ↑ and ↓ choose the message above or below, ← and → the name before or after. Space or Return, on a name, a note or a section, starts typing. Any letter starts typing in the chosen thing. Delete removes. Escape chooses nothing.",
            "A message counts only when both its names are on the board. A message with a missing name, for example one that came in from elsewhere, is not drawn, not counted, not numbered, not sent, and Select all and the arrow keys skip it. It is not thrown away.",
            "Every change is one step for Undo. The boards list counts it in its own words, for example 4 people · 9 messages · 1 note · 1 section. Search finds names and words.",
            "To PARAGON as a note: the diagram's picture, the names, then the messages as a numbered list, for example 1. Alex → Bank: Ask for loan, then 2. Bank answers Alex: Yes, then 3. Bank, by itself: Check the papers. A note stands as a quoted line and a section as a heading, each in its place. Ticked messages are listed again under Tasks.",
            "To PARAGON as a project: the messages in their order under Tasks. The ones you ticked To do become tasks and the others stay plain lines. With nothing ticked, all of them become tasks. Sections are headings, and notes go under the project's Notes.",
            "Only the to-do boxes: the ticked messages go to the Inbox as tasks, with their own words.",
            "A list cannot be pasted onto a Who does what board.",
            "0.9: a name with no colour of its own takes one by its place, and its messages have the same colour. Under each name a small line says how many messages it sends, for example sends 4. It is left out when the name sends none.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Copy, cut and paste", text: [
            "⌘C copies what is chosen, ⌘X cuts it and ⌘V pastes. They work on the Mac, and on the iPad or iPhone with a keyboard. They are also the Edit menu's Cut, Copy and Paste, and they are in the menu of a thing and of empty board.",
            "Copy takes the chosen things with the arrows between them. A chosen idea is copied with everything under it, folded or not. It goes onto the clipboard in 3 forms at once: Seedboard's own (to paste into a board), the words (for a text field, Notes or Mail; one thing gives its words, several give the same outline as Copy the board as text), and a picture.",
            "Paste makes new things, with the arrows between them kept, where the pointer is, or at the spot you pressed in the menu, or else in a free place near the middle of the view. Paste the same again at the same spot and each one lands 28 points further right and down. It works in another board too.",
            "Ideas pasted while an idea is chosen hang under that idea. A branch pasted on empty board becomes a small map of its own, in the branch's colour.",
            "Text copied in another app: one line becomes a sticky note, at the pointer or the pressed spot. A real list becomes a mind map: lines that start like a list (a dash, a star, a bullet, a number such as 1. or 1), a letter such as a), a tick box, or a #), lines pushed in, or lines that are all short (80 letters at most). Anything else of two lines or more is paragraphs and becomes one text, with its lines kept.",
            "A copied picture or screenshot is pasted as a picture, and so are picture files copied in the Finder (Mac). See Pictures.",
            "Drag words from Safari, Notes or any app and drop them on the board: a sticky note appears at that spot, and a list becomes a mind map. A picture dropped on the board lands as a picture.",
            "Mac: hold Option while you let go of a drag, and a copy lands there while the things themselves go back. It is one step for Undo.",
            "iPhone and iPad: switch on Choose more, then drag a finger over empty board to draw a choosing box round the things you want.",
            "While you type, ⌘C, ⌘X, ⌘V and ⌘A belong to the words. A sentence appears after Copy: Copied. Paste it here or in another board.",
            "On a Who does what board, Copy, Cut and Paste do nothing (grey on the Mac): its own bar has Duplicate. On a timeline, Copy and Cut do nothing either; Paste (⌘V on the Mac) puts a copied list on it as bars. See Timeline board.",
            "A door is copied, cut and pasted like a note, also into another board.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The menu of a thing", text: [
            "Mac: right-click, or hold Control and click. iPad with a trackpad or mouse: click with two fingers. Finger or Pencil: rest it on the thing for half a second without moving.",
            "On a thing: Words, Colour, To do, Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Arrow from here, Turn into (a note, a shape, a text or an idea), Move to a new board…, Share this… and Remove. An idea also has Idea under, Next idea, Fold and Break into notes. A sticky note has Choose all of this colour. A frame has Lanes. A shape has Shape. A picture has Caption and Crop.",
            "On a door: Open, Bring back (when its board was removed), Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Arrow from here, Move to a new board… and Remove. Its heading says Door.",
            "With several things chosen: To do, Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Line up, Even gaps, Same size and Remove, and where they fit Colour, Tidy, By colour, Make a mind map and Turn into, then Move to a new board… and Share this….",
            "On an arrow: Words, Line, Ends, Dashed, Turn round, Colour and Remove.",
            "On empty board: Paste, New note here, New box here, New idea here, Picture…, Door to a board…, Select all and Show the whole board. The new thing, or the paste, lands exactly where you pressed.",
            "On the Mac the menu of empty board also has Import from iPhone or iPad, right after Picture…. See On the Mac.",
            "On the Mac it is the system's own menu. On the iPhone and iPad it is a small plate just above your finger, in the colour of what you pressed, with Cancel. It grows in softly and fades when it closes. Colour, Line, Ends, Shape, Lanes and Turn into open in place, with a way back.",
            "Pressing and holding does not fight dragging. A press that moves is a drag, as before. Only a press that has not moved for half a second opens the menu. If you then move your finger after all, the menu goes away and the drag carries on.",
            "On a board that cannot be changed, the menu of a thing has only Copy, and empty board has Select all and Show the whole board.",
            "Mac: the pointer shows what it will do. Over a size square it becomes a diagonal resize arrow. Over a plus dot or an arrow's end dot it becomes a pointing hand, and the plus dot grows.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Paste a list", text: [
            "Copy a list from anywhere. On the Mac, press ⌘V with a board open. On the Mac and with an iPad keyboard, Shift ⌘V in the Board menu does it too. On any device, press More, then Paste a list as a mind map.",
            "Plain ⌘V now pastes whatever you copied, a list included. See Copy, cut and paste. This part is about lists.",
            "A list is two or more lines. Each line is an idea. A line pushed in with tabs or spaces hangs under the line above it.",
            "Plain ⌘V and Paste take two or more lines as a list only when they look like one: a line starts with a list mark, a number, a letter with a bracket, a tick box or a #, or a line is pushed in, or every line is short (80 letters at most). Paragraphs become one text instead. Paste a list as a mind map, under More, always makes a map, of paragraphs too.",
            "Dashes, stars, bullets, numbers like 1. or 1) and letters like a) are taken away. So are tick boxes, empty or ticked. Blank lines are ignored.",
            "A line starting with # is a heading. Lines under it hang under the heading.",
            "A line may only go one step deeper than the line above it.",
            "If the list has one top line, that is the middle of the map. With several top lines, the middle says Ideas and the top lines are the main branches.",
            "More than four main branches: the map goes to both sides.",
            "One single line, with no idea chosen, becomes a yellow sticky note.",
            "With one idea chosen, a pasted list or line hangs under that idea instead.",
            "The other way round: Copy the board as text, under More, puts the board's words on the clipboard. Maps come as bulleted lists, then Flow, Notes and Shapes, then Doors with the name of each board a door leads to. A timeline comes as its rows with their dates.",
            "Things in a frame with a name stand under that name: Notes: with the loose lines first, then, for example, Pros: and its lines, Cons: and its lines.",
            "On a timeline, the line under More reads Paste a list as bars. See Timeline board.",
            "Copy the board as an email (0.11): under Copy the board as text, More has Copy the board as an email. The first line says Here is my board “Plan”:, then comes the outline, and the last line says Made with Seedboard. Paste it into Mail.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "To do", text: [
            "Choose any idea, shape, note or text and press To do (its mark is a plain ring). A small hollow purple ring appears at its corner: a to-do that is still only here. Press To do again to take it away.",
            "Once it went to PARAGON, the mark is full. There are four marks:",
            "Hollow purple ring: to do, not sent yet.",
            "Purple with a white arrow pointing up and out: it went on to PARAGON and is open there.",
            "Green with a white tick: it is done in PARAGON. The thing's words turn grey.",
            "Grey with a white dash: it was cancelled in PARAGON. The words turn grey too.",
            "So: hollow means still only here; full means PARAGON has it, and the colour says how it stands. Seedboard learns done and cancelled by reading the note in PARAGON. See What PARAGON did with it.",
            "A thing that went into a note or a project shows its mark too, also when it was never ticked To do. The same marks are on timeline bars and on the messages of a Who does what board, in the small pictures and in the picture for PARAGON.",
            "A frame, a picture, a sketch or a door cannot be marked. If several things are chosen, To do puts it on all of them, or takes it off all of them if they all had it.",
            "With a keyboard, Shift ⌘T does the same.",
            "Taking the To do off a box forgets that it was sent. Tick it again and it is sent again.",
            "A to-do box goes to PARAGON as a task. In a project, the ticks on ideas also decide which ideas become tasks. See the next part.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Send to PARAGON", text: [
            "Press the purple Send to PARAGON at the top right. On the iPhone the button says Send. With a keyboard, ⌘ Return opens the page too.",
            "On the iPhone the Send page fills the screen. On the iPad it is a card just as tall as its words. If the room is short, it scrolls.",
            "You choose one of three. Name is the name the note gets. It starts as the board's name.",
            "As a note: the board's picture and its words as a tidy outline. It goes into PARAGON's Resources. A sticky note with several lines becomes one line, the lines joined with a space.",
            "The note has the sections Outline, Flow, Notes, Doors and Tasks. A frame with lanes gets a section of its own, lane by lane. A section with nothing in it is left out. Tasks are the boxes marked To do.",
            "If this device can reach PARAGON's Inbox, the note also leaves one line there that points to it.",
            "As a project: it goes into PARAGON's Projects. Outcome is the middle idea of the first map.",
            "The To do ticks decide what becomes a task. Tick the ideas that are real tasks. Then the main branches become headings, your ticked ideas become tasks, and the other ideas stay under their heading as plain lines.",
            "Tick no idea, and every idea becomes a task. Deeper ideas are tasks inside tasks.",
            "A line under As a project tells you which of the two will happen, with the counts. For example: 2 ticked things become tasks, 11 stay as plain lines. Or: Nothing is ticked: all 7 ideas and 5 shapes become tasks.",
            "Shapes inside the lanes of a frame count as shapes in that line, for example: Nothing is ticked: all 2 shapes become tasks.",
            "A ticked main branch becomes a task itself, with its ideas below it. A branch with nothing under it is one line by itself.",
            "Shapes in arrow order are listed as Steps. Shapes in a lane are listed under the frame's and the lane's name instead. Notes and text marked To do are listed as To do.",
            "A project also has Notes and a Log line. The picture is in it too. No line goes to the Inbox.",
            "Only the to-do boxes: each box marked To do goes to your PARAGON Inbox as one task. All the tasks go in together: either every one is on its way, or none.",
            "Boxes already sent are not sent twice. Boxes that went inside a note or a project count as sent too. If you take the tick off a box and tick it again, it is sent again. Undo after a Send does not change this: what went to PARAGON is kept through Undo and Redo.",
            "A box that already went to the Inbox is not made a second task when you later send the board as a note or a project: there it is a plain line, for example Permit (already in the Inbox). The row As a note then counts only the boxes the Inbox does not have, for example The 2 to-do boxes become tasks in it.",
            "When every ticked box is already in PARAGON, the page says, for example, All 4 to-do boxes are already in PARAGON. With nothing ticked it says No to-do boxes are waiting. Choose a box and press To do first.",
            "Say where they come from: a switch shown with Only the to-do boxes (and with Add what is new when the tasks go to the Inbox). It is on at first, and each device remembers your choice. On, every to-do in the Inbox ends with its way back: a link with the board's name that opens the board in Seedboard, for example Order timber, then Summer cabin as a link. When the board is a project in PARAGON, the to-do points to that project instead.",
            "A timeline bar sent as a to-do keeps its days, for example Permit (starts 1 Dec), due 5 Dec, so it lands in the Inbox with its date.",
            "A bar or milestone marked Done is not a to-do any more. In a note or a project it arrives already ticked, with the day it was done, for example Permit (starts 1 Dec), due 5 Dec. It gets no Start: line, never goes to the Inbox with Only the to-do boxes, and Add what is new does not offer a bar that was marked done after the sending. If you take the Done mark off again, Add what is new offers it again.",
            "Also remind me when a bar starts: a switch on a timeline's Send page, off each time the page opens. On, every bar longer than 3 days gets one more task right before its own, on its first day, for example Start: Foundation, due 8 Dec. It works for a note, a project, the to-dos and Add what is new.",
            "On a timeline, As a project says, for example, All 6 bars and 1 milestone become tasks with their days.",
            "Send this branch: choose one idea, then press Send to PARAGON. The page has a switch, Only the branch, with the idea's words. It is off at first.",
            "Switched on, only that idea and what hangs under it is sent, as a note, as a project, or its to-do boxes. The name becomes the idea's words, and you can change it. The picture shows only that branch.",
            "The link in such a note still opens the whole board. The board does not move to Sent to PARAGON and shows no Last sent line for a branch.",
            "A timeline is sent in its own way. See Timeline board. A Who does what board too. See Who does what.",
            "A sketch is in the picture. In the note or project text it is one plain line under Notes: A sketch (see the picture). It never becomes a task.",
            "A picture is in the picture too, cropped as it shows on the board, with its caption. In the note or project text it is one plain line under Notes: A picture (see the picture), or with a caption, A picture: After the makeover (see the picture). It never becomes a task.",
            "A door is a line under Doors, after Notes, with the other board's name as a link: in PARAGON it opens that board in Seedboard. A door never becomes a task.",
            "A frame with a name is a heading. In the note, under Notes, first come the things that lie in no named frame, then the frame's name as a heading with its things under it, for example Pros and Cons. In a project, ticked notes and text inside a named frame become tasks under that frame's name. Everything stands in reading order: rows from the top, each row from the left. A frame with lanes keeps its own lane by lane order; a frame with no name gives no heading.",
            "PARAGON files the Inbox lines itself. They appear in PARAGON's Inbox when you switch to PARAGON (PARAGON 340 or newer).",
            "If the Inbox cannot be reached from this device, Send stays greyed for the third choice. A note or a project still works.",
            "The picture is the board in daylight colours on white, without the dots.",
            "Seedboard only adds. It never opens a note you already have to write in it, never changes it and never replaces it. Sending again as a note or a project makes a second note, unless you choose Add what is new: then PARAGON itself puts the new tasks into the note you have. See Add what is new.",
            "If the name is taken, the new note is called, for example, Summer cabin (board 2).",
            "Every note has a line, Open the board in Seedboard. In PARAGON (build 339 or later), tap it on the iPhone and iPad, or hold ⌘ and click it on the Mac, and the board opens here. It works while you read the note and while you write in it. It also works when Seedboard was not running.",
            "If that board is not on the device yet, Seedboard tells you so, and opens it by itself when it arrives. If the board was removed, a line at the top says when, with a Bring back button.",
            "When the Send went through, the Send button shows a white tick for a moment, the iPhone gives a small tap, and the page closes. A purple line then says where it went, for example “Summer cabin” is in PARAGON under Projects., with a small Open button that opens the new note in PARAGON. It stays 6 seconds. For Only the to-do boxes it has no Open: the line says, for example, 2 tasks are on the way to the PARAGON Inbox. They appear there when you switch to PARAGON (PARAGON 340 or newer).",
            "If the Send did not work, the device gives a short buzz and the page stays, with a red line that says why.",
            "After a note or a project, the board moves to Sent to PARAGON (unless it is pinned).",
            "The Send page then says when and how it was last sent, and has Open in PARAGON. You also find Open in PARAGON in the board's menu in the list.",
            "Seedboard looks for PARAGON's notes again at every Send. Send is greyed out if PARAGON was not found. The page says why. Settings, under PARAGON, has the same words and a Check again button.",
            "If the note cannot be saved, the picture made for it is taken away again, so nothing is left behind in PARAGON.",
            "Cancel, or Escape on the Mac, closes the page and changes nothing.",
            "Only this frame: choose a frame and press Send this frame to PARAGON… (Mac File, Send This Frame to PARAGON…; the right-click menu; or the Send button). The Send page has the switch Only this frame (name), on at first, with the line: Just what lies inside this frame is sent, with a picture of the frame alone. The board is not marked as sent. The note takes the frame's words as its name.",
            "Due day: a task with a due day in PARAGON shows it small beside the to-do ring on the board: 5 Dec, Today in purple, faint red when past and not done, grey when done. Only tasks in a note or a project that was sent show it. It is read from PARAGON each time and kept on this device, never in the board.",
            "Today's three: choose one to three things (more than three: not offered) and press Today's three (Mac File, Today's Three to PARAGON…). The page says These go to the PARAGON Inbox as tasks due today, lists them with Today, and has the switch Say where they come from. Cancel or Send (full purple). A task is never sent twice, and shows the in PARAGON mark.",
            "Chosen things (0.11): choose two or more things, press Send, and switch on The 3 chosen things (the number follows your choice). Only they go, as a note or as to-dos; an idea brings what hangs under it. Project and Add what is new go away while it is on. The board is not marked as sent.",
            "Waiting line (0.11): under the board a small purple line says how sent to-dos stand: 3 waiting for PARAGON, 3 in PARAGON once PARAGON filed them, 3 not filed yet — open PARAGON after a day. Seedboard only reads PARAGON's waiting list. If it cannot, it says only waiting. A board sent only to the Inbox has no note to read back, so it may say waiting and later not filed yet even when PARAGON filed it.",
            "Plain problem lines (0.11): a failed Send says why and what to do, for example The Inbox could not be saved: the disk is full. Free some space, then send again. Nothing was changed. The causes: disk full, folder locked or read-only, file in use by another app, PARAGON's folder missing, iCloud full, iCloud off or not reachable. Anything else gets the system's reason.",
            "Done reaching PARAGON (0.11): a timeline bar or milestone marked Done, if it went to PARAGON as a task, is ticked there the next time PARAGON files its waiting list (PARAGON 341). Nothing happens if there is no such task, if there are two, or if it is already ticked.",
            "A board with a due day sends it as the project's due day (on a timeline it replaces the last bar's day).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "What PARAGON gets", text: [
            "Under the rows and switches of the Send page stands a box, What PARAGON gets. It shows the very words Send will write, read out of the same text, so what arrives cannot differ from it. It follows the row you choose, the name and the switches.",
            "For a note or a project: the board's small picture (for a sent branch the words The picture), the parts such as Outcome, Tasks, Notes and Log in bold, headings in purple, each task with an empty purple box and its day at the right (for example 12 Dec), a done task with a filled, ticked purple box and its words struck through, plain lines in grey, pushed in by level. For the to-dos and Add what is new: the task lines. Links show as their name.",
            "The box is 168 points high and scrolls. Under it a line counts, for example 13 tasks · 3 headings · 1 already in the Inbox · last day 20 Feb. Parts that are 0 are left out; with no tasks it says No tasks. The tasks that arrive done are counted in it, for example 5 tasks (1 done).",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Add what is new", text: [
            "When a board already went to PARAGON as a note or a project and has things that became tasks since, the Send page has a new first row, chosen when the page opens: Add what is new to “Summer cabin”.",
            "It says, for example, 3 new tasks go into the project. Nothing that is there is touched.",
            "For the first time, something you send from Seedboard lands inside a note you already have. But it is PARAGON that writes it there: Seedboard only adds the new lines to PARAGON's waiting list, aimed at that note, and PARAGON puts them at the end of the note's tasks itself, when you switch to PARAGON (PARAGON 340 or newer). Seedboard never opens a PARAGON note to write in it. It only reads first, to see that the note is still where it was.",
            "If the note is not where it was (renamed, moved or removed in PARAGON), the row says, for example, The project is not where it was: the 3 tasks go to the Inbox.",
            "What cannot follow is said under the box, for example 2 things were renamed and 1 bar moved since 18 Nov. PARAGON keeps the old ones. Beside the row stands Send a fresh copy: it goes back to As a project or As a note, and sending makes a second note with everything as it is now.",
            "With Add what is new chosen, the Name field is hidden, and the line at the bottom reads Seedboard only hands PARAGON the new lines. PARAGON puts them at the end of the tasks itself; Seedboard never changes a note you already have.",
            "After Send a line says, for example, 1 new task is on the way into “Summer cabin”. They appear there when you switch to PARAGON (PARAGON 340 or newer). When nothing is new: Nothing on the board is new for PARAGON.",
            "New tasks land at the end of the tasks, not under their own branch or phase heading. New bars of a timeline go with their days. Sending one branch alone never offers this row.",
            "For a board sent by 0.4, Seedboard reads the note to see which tasks are already there. If that note cannot be read, the row is not offered: better no row than the same tasks twice.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "What PARAGON did with it", text: [
            "A board that went to PARAGON as a note or a project shows how far it is there. Seedboard reads the note in PARAGON. It never writes in it, never moves it, never renames it.",
            "Under the board's top bar stands one small purple line, for example Project “Summer cabin” · 5 of 13 done · checked 14:02, with Open in PARAGON. A note says Note “…” instead. A note with no task of the board says only Note “Ideas” · checked 14:02. Read on an earlier day it says, for example, checked 17 Nov 14:02.",
            "When PARAGON's notes are not on this device, or iCloud has not brought the note yet, the line says Project “Summer cabin” · not checked on this device yet. When no note in PARAGON holds the board any more, it says Project “Summer cabin” · no longer in PARAGON's notes, with no Open button.",
            "On a narrow iPhone the line is shorter: Project “Summer cabin” · 5 of 13 done, with Open. On a timeline and a Who does what board it sits at the bottom, just above the tools.",
            "5 of 13 done counts the board's things that are tasks in the note, and how many of them are ticked off there. Tasks cancelled in PARAGON, tasks you re-worded there, and things no longer on the board are left out. PARAGON's own count can differ, because it counts only the top tasks.",
            "The things on the board get their marks: purple with a tick while in PARAGON, green when done there, grey with a dash when cancelled there. See To do. A bar ticked off in PARAGON is drawn done on the timeline.",
            "The same 5 of 13 done stands on the board's card on the home page and in the iPhone's list, after Changed 2 days ago.",
            "If you renamed the note in PARAGON or moved it to the Archive, Seedboard looks through PARAGON's notes for the one that holds the board's link, and keeps showing it. Open in PARAGON opens it under its new name: on this line, on the Send page and in the board's menu in the list.",
            "When it looks: when a sent board opens, right after a Send, and when Seedboard comes to the front (at most once in 15 seconds). It reads quietly in the background. What it read is kept on this device only, never in the board and never in iCloud.",
            "A to-do that went to the Inbox stays purple, in PARAGON, until you file it into the board's own note there. A branch sent alone gets no line.",
            "Read back switch (0.13): Settings, PARAGON, Read back from PARAGON. Off, and Seedboard no longer reads the notes in PARAGON: the line under the top bar, 5 of 13 done on the cards, the marks that come from PARAGON and the due days go away. On puts them back.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Switches in Settings", text: [
            "Settings has on/off switches. They are kept on this device: the iPhone, the iPad and the Mac each have their own.",
            "Tick sound (Sounds and taps): the soft tick when you tick a box. Taps you feel (on at first): the small taps on the iPhone, or a Mac trackpad, when things snap, land or a Send goes through.",
            "Timeline days in Calendar (Calendar, off at first): your timeline days go into a calendar called Seedboard by themselves. See Timeline board.",
            "Still thinking about these? (Home page, on at first): boards you left for 2 weeks come up at the top. See The home page.",
            "Done ticks the PARAGON task and Read back from PARAGON (PARAGON, both on at first). See What PARAGON did with it.",
            "Boards in search (Search, on at first): your boards show up when you search on this device. Off takes them out of the search.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Safety", text: [
            "Every board is its own file. It is kept on the device at once, and in iCloud a moment later. Saving never waits for iCloud. Your Mac, iPad and iPhone show the same boards.",
            "Without iCloud the app still works. Boards then stay on that device.",
            "A tiny amber dot at the end of a board's row means its newest change is not in iCloud yet. Settings, under Your boards, says how many boards wait, and that they are safe on this device. While pictures wait, a second line says, for example, 1 picture is not in iCloud yet. It is safe on this device. A photo is much bigger than a board, so it can take longer. A picture that never reached iCloud's folder is put there again when the app starts.",
            "A picture counts as in iCloud only when it really is there. If iCloud is full, the dot stays and Seedboard tries again by itself: every 3 seconds for the first minute, then every 30 seconds. Pictures laid before iCloud was switched on go up as soon as it is.",
            "If a board cannot be saved, Seedboard says so, in Settings and once in a line on the board, and tries again by itself.",
            "If two devices change the same board, the newer change is kept. The other one is not thrown away: it is kept under Earlier versions for 30 days, and a line at the top of the window tells you. See Removed boards and earlier versions.",
            "When a newer version of the board you have open arrives from another device, it takes the place of yours, and Undo starts over.",
            "While you type or drag, the open board is left alone. The other device's version waits and is offered again the moment you stop typing. If you changed nothing, it is shown. If you changed something, yours stays and the other is kept under Earlier versions.",
            "What you type is kept while you type. When you leave the app or close it, the typing is finished and everything is written at once.",
            "Once a day, before the first change, a safety copy of all boards is made on the device. The last 14 days are kept. You can open them under Earlier versions.",
            "Settings, under Your boards, says how many boards there are and when the last safety copy was made. It also says when iCloud is off. On the Mac, Show the boards folder opens the folder.",
            "When you remove a board, it goes from every device and is kept for 30 days, counted from the day of removal. Bring it back in Settings. See Removed boards and earlier versions.",
            "A removal never beats a newer change. If the board was changed on another device after it was removed, the board stays and comes back on every device.",
            "A board made by a newer Seedboard is shown, so you can look at it, but not change it. This version never writes over it. Update the app on that device to change the board.",
            "Boards changed by this version are stored in a fifth form, because of doors, done bars and the list of what became a PARAGON task. They cannot be changed in Seedboard 0.4 or older. They show there, to look at, until that device is updated.",
            "A board that is closed never saves. Pictures that arrive after their board was closed are not laid on it, and a red line says so, so nothing ever writes over a newer change.",
            "A starter you are changing is kept on the device as you go, so a stop of the app does not lose it. See Starters.",
            "A picture file is deleted only when no board, no removed board, no safety copy and no starter has used it for 30 days, and only after it was found unused twice, at least 7 days apart.",
            "What a newer Seedboard wrote into a board and this version does not know is carried along untouched, never dropped.",
            "A file that cannot be read is never written over either.",
            "Seedboard sends nothing anywhere by itself: only to PARAGON when you press Send, and what you share or print yourself. It reads PARAGON's notes to show how far your tasks are, and never writes in them.",
            "Things that cannot be read (0.11): if one thing or arrow in a board file cannot be read, Seedboard keeps the whole file as it was, once, under Earlier versions before anything is saved over it, shows a line in Settings and in the flash at start, for example 1 thing on “Plan” could not be read. The whole board as it was is kept under Earlier versions., and carries on with everything it could read. An unreadable created time takes the file's own date.",
            "A board from a newer Seedboard says 1 board was made by a newer Seedboard — update Seedboard first. A damaged seedboard:// link flashes in red: That board link is not complete.",
            "Undo keeps 120 steps or about 40 MB, whichever comes first; the Undo list says when older steps were let go.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Removed boards and earlier versions", text: [
            "Settings, Your boards, Removed and earlier versions. Done closes the page (Escape on the Mac).",
            "Removed: a board you remove is kept for 30 days. Each row shows the board's small picture, its name, what is on it, the day it was removed and how many days are left, for example removed 12 Nov · 18 days left. The last 3 days are written in colour.",
            "Press Bring back and the board is in your list again, the same board as before, on every device. A link from a PARAGON note opens it again. A line at the bottom confirms it; Open takes you straight to the board.",
            "Remove for good deletes the board's kept copies now. Seedboard asks first, because this cannot be undone.",
            "Earlier versions: Seedboard keeps a copy of every board once a day, 14 days back. It also keeps a version that lost when two devices changed the same board, for 30 days.",
            "Press a board's name to see its versions, newest first. Each says where it came from and its date: from another device, this device's version, daily copy, or set aside.",
            "Open as a copy makes a new board called, for example, Summer cabin (version from 18 Nov 10:00) and opens it. Your board stays exactly as it is, so you can look at both and take over what you need.",
            "Of this device's own set-aside versions the newest 10 for each board are listed. A line says how many more are kept.",
            "When two devices changed the same board, the newer change stays. A line at the top of the window says: Another device changed the board. The other version is kept under Earlier versions. Press Show to see that version, or the cross to close the line.",
            "When a link in a PARAGON note leads to a board you removed, a line at the top says when it was removed. Press Bring back and the board returns and opens. After 30 days it can no longer be brought back, and the line says so.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Export", text: [
            "Settings, Your boards, Export all boards makes one packed file (a zip) with all your boards, and asks where to save it: in Files on the iPhone and iPad, in a save window on the Mac.",
            "The file is called, for example, Seedboard boards 2026-11-18. Each board is in it twice: as a Seedboard file, and as a plain text outline you can read anywhere.",
            "The pictures your boards show are in the packed file too, each as a picture file beside the boards.",
            "Your own starters, and every built-in starter whose board you changed, are in the packed file too, in a folder Starters, each as a board file that carries the starter's name. Import boards brings such a file back as a board; Save as a starter… makes it a starter again.",
            "As soon as the file is made, the line below the button says what is in it, for example Exported 14 boards, 2 starters and 31 pictures. If pictures are missing, it says so: 3 pictures are not on this device yet and are not in the file. Seedboard then asks iCloud for them, so they are here for the next export.",
            "Otherwise the line shows when you last exported on this device, or No export yet.",
            "Import boards reads Seedboard files from such an export. Unpack the packed file first, with a tap in Files or a double-click in the Finder. You can choose several files at once.",
            "Each file comes in as a new board. Import never replaces a board. A name that is taken gets (imported).",
            "To bring pictures back too, choose the picture files from the export together with the boards. A picture comes back exactly as it was. A picture that is already there, on this device or in iCloud, stays as it is.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Doors to other boards", text: [
            "A door is a small card on a board that opens another board. It shows that board's small picture, its colour dot, its name (two lines at most) and a small › at the right, with an edge in that board's colour. It always shows the other board as it is now: renamed, recoloured or changed.",
            "Open it: two quick presses on the door (two clicks on the Mac), or Return with the door chosen, or Open in its bar or menu.",
            "The way back: the top bar then shows ‹ and the name of the board you came from. Press it, or ⌘[ on the Mac and with a keyboard, to go back. Through several doors it goes back step by step. Opening a board any other way (the list, the home page, a link) ends the way back, and a new start of the app has none.",
            "Making a door, three ways. 1. Press and hold empty board (right-click on the Mac) and choose Door to a board…. A page asks Press the board the door leads to. It lists your other boards, pinned first, with a search field, and a green New board button. Press a board and the door lies where you pressed. New board makes a new empty board (named after the search words, if any) without opening it, and puts the door to it there. Cancel, or Escape, closes the page.",
            "2. On the Mac and the iPad, drag a board's row from the list onto the open board. The door lies where you let go.",
            "3. Choose one or several things and choose Move to a new board… in their menu. It asks for the Name of the new board, filled in with the words of the chosen thing, and says, for example, 5 things move there. A door to it is left in their place. Press Save, or Cancel.",
            "On Save the things move to a new board of that name, keeping their places, colours, ticks and what PARAGON already has of them: an idea with everything under it, a frame with what lies in it. Arrows between them go along; an arrow to something that stays now ends at the door. One door is left where they were, chosen. The new board joins the list but does not open. A line says Moved to the new board “Kitchen plan”, with Undo for 6 seconds.",
            "Undo puts the things back and takes the door away. The new board stays in the list as a copy: nothing is ever thrown away by itself.",
            "A door can be moved, copied, cut, pasted (also into another board), duplicated, lined up and removed. Arrows can start and end at a door. It has no words to type, no colour of its own, no To do and one size.",
            "Its bar has Open, Bring back (only when its board was removed), Duplicate, Choose more (iPhone and iPad) and Remove.",
            "A door to a board that was removed is a quiet grey card that says This board was removed. Bring back in its bar returns the board to the list, and the door shows it again. A door to a board that is not on this device yet says Not on this device yet; opening it says That board is not on this device yet. It opens when it arrives.",
            "A board cannot have a door to itself. A timeline and a Who does what board take no doors.",
            "In PARAGON a door is a line under Doors, with the board's name as a link that opens it in Seedboard. Copy the board as text lists the doors by name. The search finds a board by the names of the boards it has doors to.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Share, PDF and print", text: [
            "Share… (in More, and in the File menu) opens a small plate. On the Mac it hangs from the board's window, on the iPhone it comes up from the bottom, on the iPad it is a card.",
            "What: Whole board; What is chosen (only when something is chosen; an idea brings everything under it, a frame what lies in it); Each frame a page (only when the board has a frame with a name).",
            "As: Picture, PDF or Words. One grey line under the tiles says what you get, for example The whole board on one page, sharp at any size. With Each frame a page, a smaller line says what stays out, for example 3 things lie outside the named frames and stay out.",
            "Press the green Share, and the device's own share choice opens: AirDrop, Mail, Messages, Save to Files and the rest. While the file is made the button says Making…. If you leave the share choice without sharing, the plate stays, so you can try another form. Cancel, or Escape, shares nothing.",
            "Picture: the board's picture as before, in the day look on white, named after the board. Each frame a page gives one picture per named frame.",
            "PDF: one file named after the board. It is drawn as lines and real letters, so it stays sharp however far you zoom, and its words can be found. White ground, day look. A4 paper (Letter in the United States, Canada and Mexico). A page lies on its side by itself when what is on it is wider than tall; a timeline always does. At the top a small grey line: the board's name at the left, the date at the right.",
            "Each frame a page: one page per named frame, in reading order, with the frame's name as the page's heading.",
            "The board is never drawn bigger than life size; a bigger one is made smaller until it fits. On paper the soft shadows are left out.",
            "Words: a small text file with the list Copy the board as text makes. Each frame a page writes the words frame by frame, each under its frame's name.",
            "Share this… in the menu of a thing (press and hold, or right-click) opens the plate with What is chosen already picked. The form you shared in last is picked the next time on that device.",
            "On a timeline and a Who does what board only Whole board is offered.",
            "Print…: on the Mac File, Print… (⌘P); on the iPhone and iPad Print… in More. It prints the whole board's PDF on one page, fitted, on its side by itself when the board is wider than tall.",
            "Photos on the board are read from their stored files for sharing and printing, so they come out at their best.",
            "Print and Share as PDF (0.11) show Making the pages… (Making… on the Share plate) while they work, and open only the photos of the page being drawn. Export all boards writes each picture to a temporary folder one at a time, so it needs much less memory.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Sharing to Seedboard", text: [
            "Seedboard is in the Share button of every app on the iPhone, the iPad and the Mac: Safari, Photos, Mail, Notes, the Finder and the others. It takes words, a link and up to 10 pictures.",
            "Press Share in the other app and choose Seedboard. A page called Send to Seedboard shows what came (for example A link, words and 1 picture), with the first words or the link's title and the pictures as small squares.",
            "Below it: New board, in full green and picked at first, then your Pinned and Recent boards, each with a ring, its colour dot and its name. A timeline and a Who does what board are not offered.",
            "Press Send. The small grey line says It is on the board the next time you open Seedboard. Cancel leaves everything as it was.",
            "When Seedboard next comes to the front, it lays what came on that board. Words become a sticky note, a list a mind map. A link becomes a sticky note with the page's title and its address. A picture becomes a picture. Everything lands in a free column to the right of what is on the board, so nothing is covered or moved.",
            "New board makes one new board for each Send, named after the first line of the words, else the link's title, else the site's name, else Shared pictures.",
            "A line says, for example, 4 things came in from Share., with Show (on the board that is open) or Open (another board). On the open board the things that came are chosen, and one Undo takes them away again.",
            "Nothing is lost on the way: what came waits until its board has been saved on the device, and only then leaves the waiting place.",
            "If something could not be taken, the page says so in red, for example Seedboard takes 10 pictures at a time. 2 more were left out.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Shortcuts, Siri, Raycast and Spotlight", text: [
            "Seedboard can be reached from outside. In the Shortcuts app, look for Seedboard. There are six actions, and Find Boards.",
            "New board from text takes a title, if you like, and any text. A real list becomes a mind map; paragraphs become one text; a single line becomes one sticky note. With no title, the board is named after the middle idea or the first line. Seedboard opens on the new board.",
            "New board starts a board with the starter you choose: every starter on your Starters page, the built-in ones as you changed or renamed them and your own, in your order. Left empty, it is a blank board.",
            "Open board offers your boards by name.",
            "Add to board adds a thought to one of your boards without opening Seedboard: Add [Text] to [Board], with an optional Picture and the switch Open the board afterwards (off at first). One line becomes a sticky note, a list a mind map, a picture a picture. On a board that is one mind map and nothing else, one line becomes a new main branch.",
            "With no board chosen, the thought goes to the board you changed last that can take it. It lands beside what is on the board, covering nothing. The answer is, for example, Added to Summer cabin. The next time you open that board on this device, what was added is chosen, and the view moves just enough to show it.",
            "Add to board refuses a timeline and a Who does what board, with a plain line, for example Nothing was added. “Trip plan” is a timeline and places its things itself. Choose another board.",
            "Board as text gives back the board's words, the same as Copy the board as text. Board as picture gives back the board's picture, the same as PARAGON gets. Both leave Seedboard in the back.",
            "Find Boards finds your boards by name, by the day they were changed, and by whether they are pinned or were sent to PARAGON. So boards changed this week is: Find Boards where Changed is after a date.",
            "Put an action on the Action button, in a shortcut, or say it to Siri. The phrases include: New Seedboard board from text. Start a thought in Seedboard. New Seedboard board. New board in Seedboard. New Mind map in Seedboard (or any other starter). Open a Seedboard board. Open Summer cabin in Seedboard. Add a thought in Seedboard. Add to Summer cabin in Seedboard. Get Summer cabin as text from Seedboard. Get Summer cabin as a picture from Seedboard.",
            "Raycast runs these through its Shortcuts command.",
            "The search on your device (Spotlight) finds a board by its name and by every word on it, and shows its small picture. Choose it and the board opens. A removed board leaves the search.",
            "A shortcut saved before 0.5 with a starter picked in New board may show Start with empty once: pick the starter again.",
            "A thought added on a device that has not yet received a newer version of that board ends as a clash: the other version is kept under Earlier versions, and the line at the top says so. Nothing is lost.",
            "Spotlight 0.9: a board's result tells its kind (Board, Timeline, Who does what) and a line of its words, and Spotlight finds every word on the board, also words read inside pictures. Boards that are put away stay findable.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Handoff", text: [
            "The board you have open offers itself on your other devices: on the Mac Seedboard's icon shows at the end of the Dock, on the iPad in its Dock, on the iPhone in the app switcher. Take it up there and the same board opens at the same zoom and the same spot.",
            "The view travels a moment after you stop moving the board.",
            "If that device does not have the newest version of the board yet, it shows the board but does not let you change it, with the line Waiting for the newest version from iCloud… at the top. When the newest version arrives the line goes. After 20 seconds at most it opens with what is there.",
            "Example boards and a starter you are changing are not offered.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "How it feels", text: [
            "On the iPhone the hand feels what happens, and on the Mac the trackpad does (the iPad has no motor for this): a soft tick when a guide line catches, a light lift when a thing starts to move, a firm tick when an idea lands under another or an arrow joins two shapes, a tick when To do goes on, a click when a pinch passes life size or reaches the smallest or largest zoom, and a success or an error at Send. Nothing is felt while the Pencil draws, and nothing for plain buttons.",
            "Every button shows the press: it shrinks a little and its colour deepens. On the Mac, and on the iPad with a trackpad, a button lights up softly when the pointer is over it. A full-colour button stays solid when pressed, just a little darker, never see-through.",
            "Things land and leave softly: a new thing grows in, a removed one fades out, a dragged thing lifts with a deeper shadow (at night its outline gets lighter instead), and things that make room glide to their new places, with their arrows following. Folding slides the ideas into their idea.",
            "With Reduce Motion on, on a board of more than 150 things, and on a timeline or a Who does what board, things simply jump, as before. The picture for PARAGON and the small pictures always show the board at rest.",
            "Sound (0.11): a soft tick when a to-do box or note tick line is ticked. Settings, Sounds, Tick sound; on for iPhone and iPad, off for the Mac; it follows the silent switch and never stops music.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Menus and keys on the Mac and with an iPad keyboard", text: [
            "Everything the buttons do is also in the menus. On an iPad with a keyboard, hold ⌘ to see the keys.",
            "File: New Board ⌘N (shows the starters). New Blank Board Shift ⌘N. Rename. Send to PARAGON ⌘ Return. Share…. Make a Timeline of It…. Save as a Starter. Starters. Remove Board (asks first). Print… ⌘P.",
            "File also has Import from iPhone or iPad (Mac). Help has How Seedboard Works (⌘?) and What's New. See On the Mac.",
            "Edit: Undo ⌘Z. Redo Shift ⌘Z. Cut ⌘X. Copy ⌘C. Paste ⌘V. Delete. Select All ⌘A. Duplicate ⌘D.",
            "View: Zoom In ⌘+. Zoom Out ⌘-. Actual Size ⌘0 (100 %). Show the Whole Board ⌘1.",
            "Insert: the seven tools with their letters, below a line Round box and Oval, and below another line Picture (P) and Draw (S).",
            "Board: To Do Shift ⌘T. Line Up. Even Gaps. Same Size. Move the Idea Up ⌘↑. Move the Idea Down ⌘↓. Paste a List as a Mind Map Shift ⌘V (on a timeline: Paste a List as Bars).",
            "⌘[ goes back to the board you came from through a door.",
            "A grey line cannot act right now: nothing is chosen, or no board is open.",
            "With nothing chosen, one letter makes a thing: M mind map. B or R box. U round box. D diamond. O oval. N sticky note. T text. F frame. A or L arrow tool. P picture. S draw (S for Sketch). The four new shapes have no letter. On a timeline or a Who does what board these letters do other things or nothing: see their own keys.",
            "With one thing chosen, a letter starts typing in it instead. Choose nothing first (press Escape) to use the letters. The Insert menu shows the letters only while pressing one would make the thing.",
            "Tab: an idea under the chosen idea. On a chosen shape: the next shape of the flow. Shift Tab: the chosen idea moves out one level.",
            "⌘ with an arrow key, on a chosen shape: the next shape in that direction. ⌘↑ and ⌘↓ on a chosen idea: one place up or down among its sisters.",
            "Return: the next idea beside the chosen idea. On any other chosen thing, or a chosen arrow: type its words.",
            "Space: type words in the chosen thing. On the Mac, hold Space and drag to slide the board; a short press still starts typing.",
            "Delete or Forward Delete: remove what is chosen.",
            "Escape: choose nothing, and leave the arrow tool.",
            "Arrow keys: walk a mind map, or move the chosen things by 10 points. With Shift: 40 points.",
            "⌘Z undo. Shift ⌘Z redo. While you type, they undo and redo the typing.",
            "With a keyboard on the iPad, all the ⌘ keys work. Press the board once if the keys do not answer.",
            "⌘C copy, ⌘X cut, ⌘V paste, on the Mac and with an iPad keyboard. A list is pasted as a mind map. Shift ⌘V, or More, Paste a list as a mind map, still does it too. See Copy, cut and paste.",
            "Shift and a press: add to the choice (Mac, and the iPad with a trackpad or a mouse). With a finger use Choose more.",
            "Hold ⌘ while dragging (Mac): place a thing freely, with no falling in line.",
            "While you type: Return keeps the words and ends the typing. In an idea it also starts the next idea. Shift Return or Option Return makes a new line exactly where the cursor stands, in an idea too.",
            "Tab, while you type in an idea, ends it and starts an idea under it. While you type in a shape, it makes the next shape. While you type in a sticky note, it makes the next note. In anything else Tab just ends the typing.",
            "Return on a chosen door opens its board.",
            "Shift Tab, while you type in an idea, moves it out one level, and you go on typing.",
            "Escape, while you type, cancels: the words go back to what they were, and a thing you just made goes away again.",
            "While you type, letters, Space, ⌘A and ⌘Z belong to the words.",
            "On the Send page: Return sends and Escape cancels. In Settings, Escape closes the page on the Mac.",
            "A Timeline board has keys of its own. See Timeline board. A Who does what board has keys of its own too.",
            "New in 0.9: T Today (timeline); ⌥⌘V Vote Dot; ⌥⌘F Focus on the Frame; ⌥⌘P Show Frames; ⌥⌘G Tidy into a Grid; ⌥⌘Z Undo List…; H Highlighter and L Lasso while drawing. New lines: View, Go to Today; Board, Balance the Map, Fold Branch, Unfold Branch, Unfold All, Number the Steps, Edit, Vote Dot, Pin in Place, Tick List, Draw on Picture, Make Sketch Typed, Undo List…; File, Fold All Phases, Unfold All Phases, Add to Calendar…, Put Board Away, Send This Frame to PARAGON…, Today's Three to PARAGON….",
            "0.11: File has Open Recent (the last 5 boards opened, newest first) and Due Day…. The Dock menu ends with Opened Recently. The Board menu has Main Branches Only / Open All Branches.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "On the Mac", text: [
            "Help menu: How Seedboard Works (⌘?) and What's New. Either opens the guide in a small window of its own, so it can stay open beside the board. At its top two plates switch between How it works and What's new. Escape closes it. The pages are the same as in Settings, Help.",
            "Dock: press Seedboard's icon in the Dock long, or right-click it, for New Board, then your pinned boards (at most 10), then the five boards you changed last. A board's line opens it.",
            "A picture from the iPhone or iPad: File, Import from iPhone or iPad, or the same line in the right-click menu of empty board, right after Picture…. For each iPhone or iPad near the Mac you can choose Take Photo, Scan Documents or Add Sketch. What you take on the iPhone lands on the board: from the right-click menu at the spot you clicked (when it comes within 2 minutes and the same board is still open), otherwise near the middle of the view. Each scanned page becomes a picture of its own, 12 at most. The line is pale when no iPhone or iPad is near, and missing on a timeline, a Who does what board and a board that cannot be changed.",
            "Services: mark words in any app, right-click, Services, New Seedboard Board from Selection. Seedboard comes to the front with a new board: a list becomes a mind map, anything else one sticky note, named after the first line. With no words it says There were no words to make a board from. A new Service shows only after Seedboard was started once; if it is missing, it can be switched on in System Settings, Keyboard, Keyboard Shortcuts, Services.",
            "The board's name: click the name in the window's top bar to rename the board (the same question as More, Rename…). Right-click it for Rename…, Pin to the top (or Unpin), Make a copy and Save as a starter…. On the iPad the name carries the same small menu.",
            "Space on one chosen picture: a short press of Space shows the whole picture large in the Mac's own Quick Look window, also when it is cropped on the board. The arrow keys step through all the board's pictures, in reading order. Space or Escape closes it. Everything else Space did stays: held with a drag it slides the board, and with anything else chosen it starts typing. A picture not yet on this Mac says This picture is not on this Mac yet.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "The bar above the keyboard", text: [
            "On the iPhone and iPad a bar sits above the screen keyboard while you type.",
            "While you type an idea it has four buttons. Idea under keeps the words and starts an idea under this one, the same as Tab. Next idea keeps them and starts the next idea beside it, the same as Return. Out one level moves this idea out one level and you go on typing, the same as Shift Tab. Done keeps the words and puts the keyboard away.",
            "On the iPhone the words are short: Under, Next, Out. On the iPad they are written out.",
            "Out one level is pale for a middle idea and for a main branch: there is no level further out.",
            "The keyboard stays up while you go from idea to idea. The Return key of the screen keyboard still means Next idea.",
            "For a sticky note the bar has Next note and Done, for a shape Next shape and Done; on the iPhone both say just Next. Next note and Next shape do what Tab does on a keyboard, and the keyboard stays up from one to the next. For text and arrows the bar has just Done.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "iPhone", text: [
            "The boards list comes first. Press a board and it opens over the list. Go back to see the list again.",
            "The list starts right under the title, with Pinned, Recent and Sent to PARAGON. Search and the New board button sit at the bottom, under your thumb: on the left the round search field Search boards, on the right a round green + that opens the starters. Settings and the version number stay the very last line.",
            "While you type in the search, the keyboard comes up and the search field and + ride on top of it; the Settings line steps aside until you are done. The + also works with the keyboard up.",
            "Every row shows the board's small picture at the right and, under the name, when it was changed and what is on it, for example Changed 2 days ago · 5 shapes · 5 notes. A sent board adds how far it is in PARAGON: Changed 2 days ago · 5 of 13 done · 5 shapes · 5 notes.",
            "With no board at all, the list shows the sprout, Plant a seed, Every idea starts on a board., and the starters themselves, two in a row. Press one and your first board opens.",
            "Press and hold Seedboard's app icon for: New board, New mind map (only while fewer than two boards are pinned), New board from the clipboard, and your first two pinned boards. New mind map takes your own version of the Mind map starter. New board from the clipboard follows the rule of New board from text; with no words on the clipboard you get a blank board and the line There were no words to paste. This is a blank board. The menu has at most four lines, the most the iPhone and iPad show: with two boards pinned, New mind map is left out so that both pinned boards fit; with fewer than two pinned boards you get all of the first three lines and the pinned boards after them.",
            "Hold the Picture tile for Photos, Take a photo and Scan a page. See Pictures.",
            "Swipe three fingers left to undo, right to redo, or shake to undo. See Choosing, moving, sizing, copying, removing, undo.",
            "The board is the same as on the iPad and the Mac. Tools, bar, plus signs, folding, arrows, notes, lanes, timelines and Send all work.",
            "Every button on the board has a press area of at least 44 points, also when it looks smaller. A small thing on a zoomed-out board is caught in a 44-point area round it; between two close things the nearer one wins.",
            "The nine tools shrink a little when the screen is narrow: 40 points wide, else 36, else 32. They slide sideways only when even 32 does not fit. Undo and Redo sit at the bottom left, above the tools, and the zoom buttons at the bottom right. The send button says Send. Print… is in More.",
            "The colour dots take two rows.",
            "A big board opens at 75 % on its main idea. While you type, the view is at least life size and goes back when you are done. See Moving around.",
            "To end typing in a box, note or text, press Done above the keyboard, or press empty board. The screen keyboard's Return starts a new line there. Both keep your words.",
            "To choose several things at once, press Choose more in the bar. The letter keys need a keyboard.",
            "The swipe back from the left edge of the screen goes back to the list and leaves the board untouched.",
            "Settings is a page with Done. It has no boards-folder button there.",
        ]),
        Part(title: "Version", text: [
            "The version is shown at the bottom of the boards list, and in Settings, under About.",
        ]),
    ]

    static let news: [Release] = [
        Release(version: "0.13", date: "2026-11-19", items: [
            "NEW. Switches in Settings. Before: Settings had only the Tick sound switch. Now: six more on/off switches, so you decide what Seedboard does. They are kept on this device: each iPhone, iPad and Mac has its own. See How it works, Switches in Settings.",
            "NEW. Taps you feel (Settings, Sounds and taps). Before: the small taps you feel on the iPhone, and on a Mac trackpad, when things snap, land or a Send goes through, could not be switched off. Now: the switch Taps you feel is on at first. Off means no taps at all. The section was called Sounds and is now called Sounds and taps; Tick sound is as before.",
            "NEW. Timeline days in Calendar, by themselves (Settings, Calendar). Before: you added timeline days to your calendar by hand, one time, with Add to Calendar…. Now: a new switch, Timeline days in Calendar, is off at first. Turn it on and every milestone and every bar of your timeline boards goes into a calendar of its own called Seedboard. Seedboard makes it the first time, with its own colour, so you can hide it in Calendar or Fantastical in one tap.",
            "The Seedboard calendar follows your board by itself. Move a bar and its event moves. Rename it and the event's name changes. Remove it and the event goes. A milestone is a one-day event. A bar is one event over all its days. All are all-day events.",
            "Done bars and milestones stay in the calendar, with (done) after the name. The notes of an event say From the Seedboard board “name”. Boards that are put away are left out.",
            "Turn it on on one device only. The calendar itself brings the days to your other devices. Switching it off takes Seedboard's events out again, and the Seedboard calendar too when nothing else is in it. Events in that calendar that Seedboard did not make are never touched.",
            "It asks once for full calendar access, because it has to read its own events to move them. A line under the switch says, for example, 14 events in the Seedboard calendar · updated 11:42. If you said no, the line says Seedboard may not use your calendar yet. Allow it in Settings ▸ Privacy ▸ Calendars (on the Mac: System Settings ▸ Privacy & Security ▸ Calendars).",
            "The older page More, Add to Calendar… stays for adding by hand. While the new switch is on, that page only says: Your timeline days go to the Seedboard calendar by themselves (Settings ▸ Calendar).",
            "NEW. Still thinking about these? (home page and iPhone list). Before: a board you forgot just sank down the list. Now: boards you have not changed for 2 weeks come up at the very top of your boards, under the heading Still thinking about these?. At most 3 show, the oldest first. Each says, for example, Changed 3 weeks ago, and has two buttons: Put away and Not now. Press the board to open it.",
            "Not now hides that board there for 2 weeks; then it may ask again. Left out: pinned boards, boards sent to PARAGON, boards already put away and boards that cannot be changed. Settings, Home page has the switch Still thinking about these?, on at first. Off means the heading never shows.",
            "NEW. Two switches for PARAGON (Settings, PARAGON), both on at first. Done ticks the PARAGON task: when you mark a timeline bar or milestone Done, its task in PARAGON is ticked too (needs PARAGON 341); off means Seedboard leaves PARAGON alone. Read back from PARAGON: your boards show what PARAGON did with them (5 of 13 done, the Open in PARAGON line, due days); off means Seedboard no longer reads PARAGON's notes and those lines disappear.",
            "NEW. Boards in search (Settings, Search), on at first. Your boards show up when you search on this device (Spotlight). Off takes them out of the search; on puts them back.",
            "Nothing else changed. The version shows as 0.13.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.12", date: "2026-11-19", items: [
            "Three buttons of 0.11 have their own hand-drawn marks now. Before: Ruler and Fade on the Draw plate were word buttons, and Read this borrowed the Make typed mark. Now: Ruler is a slanted ruler with three marks on it, Fade is a pencil line that fades into dots, and Read this is a scribble beside a note with lines.",
            "The Draw plate: Ruler and Fade are now mark tiles like Pen, Highlighter, Rubber and Lasso (were words), so the plate is shorter: 6 mark tiles and Done, plus the pen sizes and colours. Hold the pointer over a tile on the Mac to read its name.",
            "Words to a note (the bar of a picture whose words were found) uses the same mark as Read this, because both put found words on a note. Copy words keeps the Make typed mark.",
            "The shared icon library holds 3 more Seedboard marks: seedRuler, seedFade and seedReadThis.",
            "Nothing else changed: same board format (7), same data. The version shows as 0.12 (12).",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.11", date: "2026-11-19", items: [
            "Important: boards are now stored in format 7, because a board can have its own due day (see below). A board that 0.11 changes opens read-only in an older version, so you can look at it but not change it. Update every device (iPhone, iPad and Mac) to 0.11 before you go on. PARAGON 341, which ships the same day, is needed for the tick that travels back (see Done on a board ticks it in PARAGON).",
            "NEW. Ruler (Draw plate). Before: a line drawn by hand stayed as wobbly as your hand. Now: a new Ruler button puts a straight edge, 360 points long, in the middle of the board. Drag its middle dot to move it; turn it with two fingers on the middle dot, or by dragging the round dot at its end. A line drawn along it, staying within 16 points of the edge the whole way, comes out as one straight line laid on the edge. A line that wanders off stays as you drew it. The ruler is never saved and is not part of any picture; it goes when you leave the Draw tool, or press Ruler again.",
            "NEW. Fade (Draw plate). Before: pencil lines could cover the notes beneath them. Now: Fade shows all pencil lines (sketches, and marks on pictures) at 30 % until you press it again. When the plate is closed, a small line says Pencil lines are faded, with a Show button. It changes only how the board looks; nothing is saved.",
            "NEW. Read this (bar for a chosen sketch). Before: Make typed turned a sketch into a text and the drawing was gone. Now: Read this leaves the sketch where it is and puts the words it finds on a yellow sticky note 16 points to the right of it. One Undo takes the note away. If nothing is found, a line says No words found in this sketch.",
            "NEW. Rubber size has a word. Before: the ring that shows while you rub had no word. Now: it says Small, Medium or Large under the ring (the ring reaches 20, 40 or 80 points on the screen, as set on the plate).",
            "NEW. Send only the chosen things. Before: you could send a whole board, one branch or one frame. Now: choose two or more things and press Send; the page has a new switch, for example The 3 chosen things. Only those go, as a note or as to-dos (an idea brings what hangs under it). Project and Add what is new go away while it is on, and the board is not marked as sent.",
            "NEW. A line that says where your to-dos stand. Before: after Send, a to-do in the Inbox only got a filled ring. Now: a small purple line on the board says, for example, 3 waiting for PARAGON, then 3 in PARAGON once PARAGON has filed them, or 3 not filed yet — open PARAGON if a whole day has gone by. Seedboard only reads PARAGON's waiting list; it never changes it. If it cannot read it, the line says only waiting.",
            "NEW. Done on a board ticks it in PARAGON (needs PARAGON 341). Before: a bar or milestone marked Done on a timeline stayed open as a task in PARAGON. Now: if it was sent as a task, PARAGON ticks that one task the next time it files its waiting list, but only when exactly one open task has exactly those words. Otherwise nothing changes and nothing new is made. Taking the Done mark off changes nothing in PARAGON. An older PARAGON (340) ignores the message harmlessly.",
            "NEW. Copy the board as an email (More, under Copy the board as text). Before: only plain text. Now: the first line says Here is my board “Plan”: for example, then the outline, then Made with Seedboard on the last line. Paste it into Mail.",
            "NEW. Due day for a board. Before: a board had no day of its own. Now: More, Due day… (on the Mac, File, Due Day…) opens a small page with a date picker, Cancel, Save and No due day. The card and the row show Due 12 Jan in small words, red once the day has passed (with the year when it is not this year, for example Due 12 Jan 2027). Send as a project gives PARAGON that day as the project's due day; on a timeline it replaces the last bar's day. The order of the list does not change.",
            "NEW. Names for colours. Before: a sticky note's colour dots always said Yellow, Green and so on; your words from the Colour legend only showed on the legend card. Now: when you named a colour, the dots and a small line under them show your word (for example Question), and PARAGON's note puts it after each sticky note of that colour, for example - Call Alex (Question). Nothing new is saved. A note sent as a tick list goes line by line, without the word.",
            "NEW. Main branches only (mind maps). Before: ideas folded one at a time, and Unfold all only opened. Now: choose the middle of a mind map and press Main branches only: every first idea that has ideas under it folds, and the +N marks show. Pressed again it says Open all and everything of that map opens. On the Mac the Board menu has Main Branches Only and Open All Branches, and works with any idea of the map chosen. A map with nothing under its first ideas has no such button.",
            "NEW. Tick sound. Before: ticking was silent (a light tap on the iPhone only). Now: a soft two-note tick plays when a to-do box is ticked, and when a tick line in a sticky note is ticked (not when it is unticked). Settings has a new part, Sounds, with the switch Tick sound: on at first on the iPhone and iPad, off on the Mac. It follows the silent switch and never stops your music.",
            "NEW. Open Recent on the Mac. Before: no list of opened boards (the Dock menu showed the 5 changed last). Now: File, Open Recent lists the last 5 boards you opened, newest first, and the Dock menu ends with Opened Recently with the same list (boards already shown higher in the Dock menu are left out there). Removed and put-away boards are left out. It is kept on this Mac only.",
            "NEW. Pictures nobody uses (Settings, Your boards). Before: the weekly clean-up of unused pictures could not be looked at. Now: a line says, for example, Pictures nobody uses: 3 (12 MB), with Show (the pictures, small) and Remove now. Remove now asks once: Remove 3 pictures nobody uses? This cannot be undone. A picture used by any board, removed board, earlier version, safety copy or starter is never counted or removed. If Seedboard cannot be sure (a board still waits for iCloud, a file cannot be read, a picture is still only in iCloud), it says cannot tell right now and removes nothing. A picture added in the last hour is not counted yet. The weekly clean-up works as before (30 days old, two looks a week apart).",
            "NEW. Copy words and Words to a note (chosen picture with found words). Before: the words found in a picture could only be searched. Now: Copy words puts them on the clipboard (Words copied), and Words to a note makes a yellow sticky note 24 points to the right of the picture and its caption, with those words. They are not shown for a picture with no words.",
            "NEW. VoiceOver reads the board. Before: VoiceOver saw a whole board as one thing called Board. Now: every thing and every arrow on an ordinary board is read on its own, in outline order (mind maps first, branch by branch; then the rest row by row; then the arrows), for example Idea: Roof, done, 2 votes. Each has the actions Choose, Edit words and, where it can move, Move left, right, up and down (10 points each). Ideas in a mind map place themselves, so they have no Move; pinned things do not move. A timeline or Who does what board is still read as one thing, as before.",
            "NEW. Differentiate Without Colour. With that system setting on, a late timeline bar shows slanted white strokes on its red end, a late milestone has a dashed outline, and the colour legend card writes the colour's name before your words (Yellow: Ideas). Done bars already had a tick mark. With the setting off (the standard) nothing looks different.",
            "SAFER. A thing that cannot be read is no longer lost. Before: a thing or arrow that could not be read was left out, and the next save wrote the board without it, silently. Now: the file as it was is kept once under Earlier versions before anything is saved over it, and a line says so: 1 thing on “Plan” could not be read. The whole board as it was is kept under Earlier versions. (3 things on … when there are more). The board stays editable. The line lasts for this run of the app; Earlier versions remember it for good.",
            "SAFER. A board's “created” time. Before: an unreadable created time counted as the year 1970. Now: it takes the file's own date, so the newer board still wins when two devices disagree.",
            "SAFER. Absurd places and sizes. Before: a thing placed absurdly far away (for example 10 followed by 307 zeros) could stop the dot grid and the timeline. Now: places are held to within 100,000 points of the middle and sizes to between 0 and 20,000; the grid never draws more than 400,000 dots, and the timeline never turns a giant number into a day.",
            "SAFER. Timeline dates. Before: a bar date in year 1 or 9999 could make the timeline draw a mark for every day in between. Now: the Dates page refuses a year outside 1900 to 2200 with the red line Pick a date between the years 1900 and 2200, and the timeline never draws more than 1500 date marks at once. A stored day outside those years is ignored.",
            "SAFER. Boards from a newer Seedboard. Before: importing one said was not a board. Now: 1 board was made by a newer Seedboard — update Seedboard first.",
            "SAFER. A damaged seedboard:// link. Before: it did nothing. Now: a red flash says That board link is not complete.",
            "SAFER. Send problems say why. Before: a full disk, a locked file and a closed iCloud all said This device cannot reach PARAGON's Inbox. Now: six causes in plain words: disk full, folder locked or read-only, file in use by another app, PARAGON's folder missing, iCloud full, iCloud off or not reachable (and a plain line with the system's reason for anything else). For example: The Inbox could not be saved: the disk is full. Free some space, then send again. Nothing was changed. A missing Inbox folder now says: PARAGON's Inbox folder is missing on this device. Open PARAGON once, then check again in Settings. A note or a project can still be sent.",
            "SAFER. Less memory. PDF and print: before, every photo stayed open at full size until the last page was done (20 phone photos could use up the memory); now only the photos of the page being drawn are open, and they are let go after each page. Print and Share as PDF show Making the pages… (Making… on the Share plate) while they work, and the screen gets a turn between pages. Export all boards: before, the pictures, the packed file and the final copy were all in memory together; now each picture is written to a temporary folder one at a time, zipped, and saved straight from that file (the folder is cleared afterwards). Undo: before, 120 steps whatever their size; now 120 steps or about 40 MB, whichever comes first (the newest step is always kept), and the Undo list says Older steps were let go to save memory when that happened. Photos on a board: before, any number of sharp full-size photos could be asked for at once; now at most 12 are open, and the rest show their small copy until they are among the 12 most recently in view. One very big page is still drawn in one go.",
            "FIXED. Picture captions. Before: the room under a picture counted 6.6 points a letter, so a long caption could touch what lay below. Now: it is measured with the caption's own lettering (12 points), the same lines the screen draws. Line up and Even gaps now count a picture together with its caption (before: the picture alone).",
            "FIXED / TIDY. PARAGON wording for pictures and sketches now comes from one rule: A picture (on the board), A picture: After the makeover (see the picture), A sketch (on the board). The words themselves did not change. The small Colour legend card in the corner now shows the legend mark at its top left, like its menu line.",
            "CHECKED, NOT CHANGED. Fixed letter sizes: all 29 are drawn board text, an invisible 1-point test label, or a typing field that must match drawn text. None is a button, chip or bar label (those already use Apple's text styles in 96 places). So nothing changed, and the standard look is untouched.",
            "The small picture of a timeline starter you made yourself. Before: it showed the dates it was saved with. Now: it shows them moved to from today, as a new board from it would get.",
            "Tests. A new set of 5 small tests (AMSSeedboardStoreTests) runs the saving of boards, the joining of iCloud's numbered copies and the setting aside of old versions on a throwaway folder with a stand-in for iCloud, in about 0.3 seconds. One more test reads about ten damaged files (cut off, wrong types, huge numbers, empty values, broken arrows).",
            "Version: 0.11 (build 11). Settings shows it. How it works changed in: Your boards, The board, Mind maps, Marks on ideas, legend, focus and show, Timeline board, Drawing by hand, Pictures, Paste a list, Send to PARAGON, Safety, Share, PDF and print, Menus and keys, How it feels.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.10", date: "2026-11-19", items: [
            "Eleven buttons now have a hand-drawn mark of their own. Before: some borrowed the mark of another button, and some showed only a word. Now: each one has its own mark, drawn in the same style as all the others. Nothing else about how they work has changed, and the board format is still 6.",
            "Balance (in the mind map bar and in More). Before: it showed the Even gaps mark. Now: a middle idea with two branches on each side.",
            "Highlighter (on the Draw plate). Before: only the word Highlighter. Now: a broad-tipped pen over a coloured band.",
            "Lasso (on the Draw plate). Before: only the word Lasso. Now: a rope loop with a loose end.",
            "Make typed (in the handwriting bar). Before: it showed the Words mark. Now: a squiggle that turns into a typed T.",
            "Draw on it (in the picture bar). Before: the plain Draw pencil, the same as the Draw tool. Now: a picture with a pencil at its corner.",
            "Vote (in the bar). Before: a plain dot. Now: a note with a vote dot on its corner.",
            "Tick list (in the note bar). Before: the ordinary list mark. Now: two lines, each with a tick.",
            "Colour legend (the Colour legend… line in More and the heading of the Colour legend page). Before: no mark of its own. Now: a small card with two colour dots.",
            "Focus (in the bar for a chosen frame). Before: the Fit window mark, corners round a square. Now: frame corners round a ring with a dot, so it no longer looks like Fit window.",
            "Show frames (in More). Before: no mark at all. Now: frame corners round a play triangle. It also stands at the left of the small bar while frames are shown.",
            "Small map (the switch in More). Before: no mark at all. Now: a board with a small map in its lower right corner.",
            "Version: 0.10 (build 10). Settings shows it. How it works changed in: The board, Mind maps, Drawing by hand, Pictures, Marks on ideas, legend, focus and show.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.9", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            "Important: boards now have a new format, number 6. A board that 0.9 changes opens read-only in an older version, so you can look at it but not change it. Update every device (iPhone, iPad and Mac) to 0.9 before you go on.",
            "Timeline: the day a bar was done now sits on a soft rounded background in the board's colour. Before: it was plain grey words, and the red Today line could run right through them. Now: the line passes behind the label and the words stay clear.",
            "Timeline: the day shows how late or early the work was. Before: only the day, for example 18 Nov. Now: it compares with the bar's planned last day: 18 Nov · 8 days late in faint red, or 18 Nov · 3 days early in faint green. Done on the planned day, it says only the day. One day is 1 day late. The Dates page says, for example, Done on 18 Nov · 8 days late.",
            "Timeline: a Today button. Before: the button was there but only in the zoom group, and it had no key. Now: the button is called Today, and on the Mac and with an iPad keyboard the key T takes the timeline to today. On the Mac there is also View, Go to Today. Today now lands a third of the way in from the names, not in the middle.",
            "Timeline: weekends and Swedish holidays are shaded softly. Before: only weekends, and only when you looked at days. Now: weekends are shaded in the Days view and the Weeks view, and the Swedish public holidays (16 a year, for example 1 May and Midsummer Eve) are shaded a little stronger. The Months view has no shading.",
            "Timeline: long plans open in months. Before: the month marks said only Jan, and the board opened as it fitted. Now: the marks say Jan 2027, and a timeline that lasts more than about half a year (183 days) and that you never moved or zoomed opens in the Months view. If you moved or zoomed it, it opens as you left it. Show the whole timeline fits as before.",
            "Timeline: phases fold away. Before: a phase heading was only a heading. Now: a small arrow before its name folds all its bars and milestones into one soft band from its first to its last day, with the count, for example 4 bars, 1 milestone. Press it again to open. Fold all phases and Unfold all are in More, in the Mac File menu and in the right-click menu on the Mac. The arrows between bars still show, and the picture of the board always shows every row.",
            "Timeline: milestones and bars into your calendar. New: More, Add to Calendar… (on the Mac also File, Add to Calendar…, and the right-click menu) opens a page with the milestones and then the bars, each with a tick box and its days. Press Add and they go into your calendar as all-day events. Seedboard can only add: it never reads your calendar. It remembers what it added, so a thing already added shows Added 18 Nov, and one whose days changed since shows Day changed — add again. New and changed milestones are ticked at first; bars never. The first time, your iPhone, iPad or Mac asks to allow it.",
            "Vote dots: choose a note, an idea or a shape and press Vote (on the Mac ⌥⌘V) to give it a small orange dot; press again for more, up to 99. More than 5 shows 5 dots and the number. The press-and-hold or right-click menu has Remove a dot and Clear dots. In More: Sort by dots (most dots first, in a row) and Clear all dots.",
            "Colour legend: More, Colour legend… opens a page where you say what each sticky-note colour means (What does yellow mean?, up to 40 letters). A small card in the bottom left corner shows the colours that have words, and a press on it opens the page. Hide legend takes it away. A note with no colour counts as yellow.",
            "Tick list inside a note: a line that starts with [ ] or [x] shows as a hand-drawn box with its words. Press the box to tick it or untick it. The new button Tick list turns every line of a chosen note into a tick line, or back. A note with tick lines goes to PARAGON as a list of tasks, one per line.",
            "Web addresses: when a note or idea holds a web address, the page's title shows in small blue words under it, with a tiny arrow. Press it to open the page, or use Open link in the bar. The title is fetched once, after you stop typing. On the Mac Seedboard now may reach the internet for this.",
            "Pin in place: the button Pin in place (Unpin) fixes a thing so dragging, sizing, arrow keys and tidying never move it. Choose it and a tiny pin shows. Moving a group moves the others only.",
            "Background: More, Background offers Plain, Dots and Squares. Before: the soft dots were always there. Now: Dots stays the standard, so every board looks as before. The background is never in the picture that goes to PARAGON.",
            "Focus: choose a frame and press Focus (on the Mac ⌥⌘F) and everything outside it fades to a quarter. A small pill, Focus · the frame's name, with Done, ends it; so does Escape. The board comes back as it was.",
            "Show frames: More, Show frames (on the Mac ⌥⌘P) walks through the frames one by one, like slides. A bar at the bottom says, for example, ‹ 2 of 5 › with Done. The arrow keys, the space bar, a swipe on the iPhone and iPad, and Escape work.",
            "Tidy into a grid: before this was called Tidy. Now: Tidy into a grid (More, and on the Mac ⌥⌘G) lines a messy pile up into neat rows and columns, 16 points apart; with nothing chosen it tidies the loose notes. Pinned things stay.",
            "A small map in the bottom right corner of a big board (when the things spread over more than twice the screen): press or drag on it to jump there. More, Small map turns it on or off for this device (on at first).",
            "New starters: Decision grid, Weekly review and SWOT join the starters that come with Seedboard.",
            "Balance: choose the middle of a mind map with two or more branches and press Balance (on the Mac, Board, Balance the Map). The main branches spread evenly left and right, the heaviest first. One Undo step.",
            "Fold a branch away: before, a folded idea showed a dot with the number of its direct ideas, and pressing it did nothing. Now: a small pill such as +5 counts all the hidden ideas, and one press opens them. The menu says Fold branch and Unfold branch, and there is a new Unfold all, also in the Mac Board menu.",
            "Words on arrows: before, the words stood near the start of the line, with no background. Now: they stand in the middle of the line on a small patch in the board's colour, and the box you type in is there too.",
            "Number the steps: More, Number the steps (and Stop numbering the steps) puts a small round blue number on each flowchart shape, starting where arrows only leave, and following the arrows. Things no arrow reaches get no number.",
            "Already there, now proven by tests: a diamond gives Yes and No to its first two arrows, and arrows go round shapes instead of through them.",
            "Each name has its own colour: a name with no colour of its own now takes one by its place (before it was always blue), and its messages follow it. Under each name a small line says how many messages it sends, for example sends 4. Nothing shows when it is 0.",
            "Highlighter: while drawing, the new Highlighter (key H) draws a wide, see-through stripe in yellow, green, pink or blue. It lies under your ordinary pencil lines.",
            "Rubber sizes: the rubber now has Small, Medium and Large. Medium is what it always was. It still takes whole lines.",
            "Make typed: choose a sketch with handwriting and press Make typed, and the handwriting becomes a Text thing with the words, in the same place and with the same name. If nothing can be read it says No words found in this sketch.",
            "Draw on a picture: choose a picture and press Draw on it. Your lines become marks on the picture and move and size with it. Press Done when you are finished.",
            "Lasso: while drawing, press Lasso (key L) and draw a loop round pencil lines. The lines that are more than half inside are chosen and can be moved together.",
            "Send only a frame: choose a frame and press Send this frame to PARAGON… (also on the right-click menu, and on the Mac File, Send This Frame to PARAGON…). The Send page has the switch Only this frame, on at first: only what lies inside goes, with a picture of the frame alone, and the board is not marked as sent. Before: only one idea's branch could be sent alone.",
            "Due days from PARAGON: when a task has a due day in PARAGON, the board shows it small beside the to-do ring: 5 Dec, Today in purple, faint red when it is past and not done, grey when done. It is read each time and not kept in the board.",
            "Today's three: choose one to three things and press Today's three. A page lists them; the switch Say where they come from adds the board's name. Send puts them into PARAGON's Inbox as tasks due today, and never twice.",
            "Put away old boards: press and hold a board (right-click on the Mac) and choose Put away. It leaves the list but is never deleted. A quiet row, Put away (3), at the bottom of the list and the home page opens a page where each board has Bring back. Search still finds them.",
            "A line under each board: before, only 12 ideas · 4 shapes · 2 notes. Now an ordinary board says 34 ideas · 12 done; a timeline says, for example, 7 things · 4 done · 1 late (bars and milestones counted together, so it fits on an iPhone). It shows on the list and on the home cards.",
            "Search finds words inside pictures: each picture is read once on your device, when a board is opened. A board found only that way says · in a picture.",
            "Spotlight: a board in Spotlight now tells its kind (Board, Timeline, Who does what), and Spotlight finds the words in it, also those in pictures. It works on iPhone, iPad and Mac.",
            "Undo list: press and hold the Undo button, or ⌥⌘Z, or on the Mac Edit, Undo List…. It lists your last steps, newest first, for example Moved 3 things · 21:40. Press a step to go back to just before it; you can still redo.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.8", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            "The day it was done is drawn on the board. Before: a bar or milestone marked Done showed its day only when you chose it (the Done button, or the Dates page). Now: it is a small grey label a little past its end, for example 18 Nov. It stays in the same place when you choose the bar. When the day is not in this year, the year is added, for example 3 Dec 2025.",
            "Taking the Done mark off removes the label again. A bar that you ticked off only in PARAGON gets no label, because Seedboard keeps no day for it.",
            "The board's picture shows the label too, in the picture that goes to PARAGON and in Share as Picture and PDF.",
            "The course: on the pretend iPhone, the bar for a chosen timeline row now stands at the bottom, just above the tools, as in the real app. Before: it stood at the top.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.7", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            "Done shows its day. Before: a bar or milestone marked Done kept the day it was done, but you never saw it; only PARAGON got it. Now: when you choose a done bar or milestone, the Done button in the bar below says the day, for example Done 18 Nov. When the day is not in this year, the year is added, for example Done 3 Dec 2025.",
            "Several chosen: with more than one bar or milestone chosen, the button just says Done, as before, because they can have been done on different days.",
            "Dates page: a done bar or milestone has one more line under its dates, for example Done on 18 Nov. It is only to read; Cancel and Save work as before.",
            "A bar that you ticked off only in PARAGON shows no day. Before and now: Seedboard does not know the day for it, so the button says Done and the Dates page has no extra line.",
            "The course now shows the iPad and the Mac too. Before: it showed only the iPhone. Now: a switch at the top, iPhone, iPad and Mac, changes the pictures to that device.",
            "The course has a new chapter, iPad and Mac. It shows the list of boards beside the board, the Mac menu bar, the Mac keys, Escape for Cancel, and the Pencil and the trackpad.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.6", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            "Fixed: a bar or milestone marked Done went to PARAGON as an open task. Before: in a note or a project it arrived with an empty box, as if it still had to be done. Now: it arrives already ticked, with the day it was done, written the way PARAGON writes a done task itself.",
            "Fixed: a done bar no longer asks to be started. Before: with Also remind me when a bar starts on, a done bar still got a Start: line. Now it gets none.",
            "Fixed: a done bar never goes to the Inbox. Before: Only the to-do boxes sent it as a new task. Now it is left out, because it is not a thing to do any more.",
            "Fixed: Add what is new no longer offers a bar that you marked Done after the sending, because there is nothing left to do. If you take the Done mark off again, it is offered again.",
            "Send page: a done task shows as done in What PARAGON gets. Before: every task had an empty box. Now a done task has a filled, ticked purple box and its words are struck through, and the count line says, for example, 5 tasks (1 done).",
            "Fixed: the app icon's menu on the iPhone and iPad. Before: it offered five lines but the device shows only four, so your second pinned board fell off the end. Now it offers at most four: New board, New mind map, New board from the clipboard, then your first two pinned boards. When two boards are pinned, New mind map makes way, so both boards fit; it is back as soon as fewer than two are pinned.",
            "PARAGON: the link back to the board in a note can be pressed. Before: it worked only while you read the note. Now, in PARAGON build 339 or later, tap it on the iPhone and iPad, or hold ⌘ and click it on the Mac, and the board opens here, also while you write in the note.",
            "A course: a short interactive tour of the whole app, with a practice screen.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.5", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            // Feel (picks 1 to 6)
            "Feel: the board glides. Before: a zoom step, Show the whole board, bringing a new idea into sight and the iPhone's zoom for typing jumped in one go. Now: a zoom step glides in 0.22 seconds with the middle held still and the percent counting along, Show the whole board in 0.30, a new idea slides into sight in 0.18, and the iPhone's typing zoom in 0.18. A finger, a click, a scroll or a key stops a glide at once. Three quick presses on Bigger are three full steps.",
            "Feel: things land, lift, leave, make room and fold softly. A new thing grows in from 92 % in 0.16 seconds; a removed one fades out in 0.12; a dragged shape, note or picture lifts with a deeper shadow (at night its outline gets lighter instead); things that make room, Line up, Even gaps, Same size, Undo and Redo glide to their new places in 0.20 seconds, with their arrows; folding slides the ideas into their idea. Off with Reduce Motion, on boards of more than 150 things, and on timeline and Who does what boards.",
            "Fixed: Show the whole board moved only the bar beside a chosen thing softly, while the drawing jumped. Now the drawing, the chosen frame, the bar and the typing field always move together.",
            "Feel: the hand feels it. On the iPhone, and on the Mac's trackpad: a guide line catching, a thing lifting, an idea landing under another, an arrow joining two shapes, To do going on, a pinch passing life size or reaching 20 % or 300 %, and a Send that went through or did not. Nothing while the Pencil draws, nothing for plain buttons.",
            "Feel: every button answers the press. Before: plain buttons gave no sign, and the full-colour buttons turned 75 % see-through. Now: a button shrinks to 95 % and its tint deepens; full-colour buttons stay solid, 12 % darker and 97 %. With the pointer over it (Mac, iPad trackpad) a button lights up softly; a home card's edge turns green and its shadow grows, and on the iPad it lifts.",
            "Feel: Send is a hand-over. Before: the page closed and a green line said where the board went. Now: the Send button shows a white tick for 0.45 seconds, the iPhone gives a success tap, and the line that follows is purple, with an Open button that opens the new note in PARAGON, for 6 seconds. Only the to-do boxes gets the purple line without Open.",
            "Feel: an inviting empty board. Before: grey words, An empty board. Press a tool below to start. Now: a sprout, What is on your mind? and three buttons, Mind map, Sticky note and Paste a list. Paste a list is pale while nothing is copied. On the Mac a quiet line adds or double-click anywhere for a sticky note.",
            "Feel: the iPhone's first start. Before: the grey line No boards yet. Press New board. Now: the sprout, Plant a seed, Every idea starts on a board. and the starters themselves, two in a row: one press makes the first board. An empty board's card on the home page shows a faint sprout instead of the word Empty.",
            "Feel: eight rough edges smoothed. The Send button stands by itself in the top bar, so its purple is its own by night too. The amber not-in-iCloud dot is amber also on the chosen row (it was white). The list's colour dots follow day and night. A line such as Board removed. It is kept for 30 days in the Removed folder. is now seen on the home page and in the iPhone's list (before, only inside a board). Problems are said in brick red, PARAGON in purple, the rest in green. Lines slide up and fade in, and fade out. The press-and-hold menu grows in and fades out. The small Undo and zoom boxes have the same soft shadow as the bar.",

            // Home page and mind maps (picks 7, 8)
            "Home page: your boards come first. Before: the starters filled two rows of big cards, about 360 points high, and only 2 board cards were whole in sight. Now: the starters are one strip that slides sideways, cards 132 points wide with their picture and name, about 150 points high in all, and on a 1280 by 800 Mac window 12 board cards are in sight. The Blank board is always the first card of the strip.",
            "Home page: the header keeps the sprout, Plant a seed and its line, and has no button any more: New board at the top of the list is the one green button. A board card pressed long (right-click on the Mac) now has the list's menu: Rename…, Pin to the top, Make a copy, Save as a starter…, Colour, Open in PARAGON and Remove….",
            "Mind maps: branches thick to thin, with the words on the line. Before: every line 2.6 points, ending at the middle of the idea's side. Now: 3.4 points to a main branch, 2.4 to the next level, 1.8 below. The line comes to the foot of an idea and runs on under its words as an underline in the branch's colour; the ideas under it start from its end. The small pictures, the shared picture and the picture for PARAGON look the same.",

            // Thinking (picks 10, 11, 12)
            "Thinking: the next note while typing. Tab in a sticky note keeps the words and makes the next note, same colour and size, 16 points to the right (or under the first note of the row when that is taken), and you type on. Left empty, it goes away and leaves no Undo step. On the iPhone and iPad the bar above the keyboard has Next note for a note and Next shape for a shape (both Next on the iPhone). Before: only Done.",
            "Thinking: on the Mac, two clicks on empty board always make a sticky note. Before: only when nothing was chosen before, so right after typing a note it took three clicks.",
            "Thinking: Turn into. A sticky note, a box or any other shape, a text or an idea can become a Sticky note, a Box, a Text or an Idea, in the menu of a thing, of several, and behind the bar's … button. The words, the tick, the place, the arrows and the colour stay.",
            "Thinking: drag a sticky note, a plain box or a text onto an idea and it becomes an idea under it, with the dashed green frame while you drag, and the line Now an idea with Undo.",
            "Thinking: Make a mind map. With two or more sticky notes or texts chosen, the bar has Make a mind map. A small page asks for The middle idea (the board's name filled in), with Cancel and Save. The notes become the main branches in reading order; notes in a named frame hang under a branch with the frame's name.",
            "Thinking: Break into notes, in the menu of an idea. The idea and everything under it become sticky notes in one column, in the paper of their branch's colour.",
            "Thinking: sort a pile. Colour now works for several things at once. Tidy (from three notes, shapes or texts) lays them out as a neat grid, at most 5 side by side, 16 points apart, where the pile lay. By colour (behind the … button) puts them in one column per colour. Choose all of this colour (in the menu of a sticky note) chooses every note with the same paper.",
            "Thinking: the bar of a chosen thing always has a … button when it can be turned into another kind. Before: the … showed only when the bar was too narrow.",
            "Fixed: pasted paragraphs became a mind map. Before: two or more lines of anything became a map called Ideas, with whole paragraphs as branches. Now: only a real list does (lines with list marks, numbers, tick boxes, a #, lines pushed in, or only short lines of 80 letters at most). Paragraphs become one text with their lines kept. Paste a list as a mind map still always makes a map.",

            // Doors (pick 13)
            "Doors: a door to another board. A new thing: a small card with the other board's small picture, colour dot and name, always as that board is now. Two presses, Return, or Open open that board, and the top bar shows ‹ and the name of the board you came from to go back (⌘[ on the Mac).",
            "Doors: three ways to make one. Door to a board… in the menu of empty board, with a page to choose the board or make a New board; dragging a board's row from the list onto the board (Mac and iPad); and Move to a new board… for chosen things, which moves them to a new board and leaves a door in their place, with Undo.",
            "Doors: a door to a removed board says This board was removed and has Bring back. A door to a board that has not arrived says Not on this device yet. Arrows can start and end at a door. The list counts doors (1 note · 2 doors) and the search finds a board by the names of its doors.",
            "Doors: in PARAGON a door is a line under Doors with the board's name as a link that opens it in Seedboard. Copy the board as text lists the doors by name.",

            // Timeline (picks 15, 18)
            "Timeline: Make a timeline of it…, in More and in the Mac's File menu. Before: a plan had to be typed again on a new timeline. Now: a new board, for example Summer cabin timeline, opens with a phase for each main branch and a bar for each idea, 7 days each, one after the other; flow shapes become bars that follow their arrows; the ticks or the chosen things decide, as for a project. A line says what was made and what stayed on the board.",
            "Timeline: a list can be pasted onto a timeline. Before: A list cannot be pasted onto a timeline. Now: ⌘V (Mac), More, Paste a list as bars, or Board, Paste a List as Bars: one bar per line, 7 days each, in a chain; lines pushed in under a line make a phase.",
            "Timeline: Done. A new button in the bar of a chosen bar or milestone. A done bar is paler with a tick at its end and a grey name. A bar ticked off in PARAGON is drawn done too.",
            "Timeline: late bars are red. A bar whose last day has passed and that is not done has a red end and a red name. The part of a bar before the Today line is darker, so a bar in work shows how far it should be.",
            "Timeline: one line in the corner above the names, for example 3 of 8 done · 1 late · ends 20 Feb. It is in the picture for PARAGON too.",
            "Timeline: Catch up. While something is late, a red Catch up button stands after Today. It stretches the late bars to today and pushes along what comes after, in one Undo step, and says, for example, 4 bars moved. The plan now ends 27 Feb (was 20 Feb).",
            "Timeline: week numbers. On the Weeks scale every Monday shows its week number, for example w 49, counted as in Sweden.",
            "Timeline: a phase carries its rows. Before: dragging a phase moved only its heading. Now: the heading and every row under it move as one block, and it never lands inside another phase.",
            "Timeline: it opens as you left it, with the same scale and the same days in sight, on each device.",
            "Fixed: before 0.5 a timeline that had been slid could come back after a restart showing the year 2000. Now it comes back where you left it, or whole.",

            // PARAGON (picks 16, 17, 19, 20)
            "PARAGON: the board shows what is sent and done. Seedboard now reads the note a board became in PARAGON (it never writes in it). A purple line under the top bar says, for example, Project “Summer cabin” · 5 of 13 done · checked 14:02, with Open in PARAGON. The board's card and the iPhone's row add 5 of 13 done.",
            "PARAGON: four to-do marks. Before: one mark, a purple ring with a tick, whether the thing was sent or not. Now: a hollow purple ring = to do, still only here; purple with a white tick = in PARAGON; green with a white tick = done there; grey with a white dash = cancelled there. Done and cancelled grey the thing's words. On ideas, shapes, notes, timeline bars and Who does what messages, in the small pictures and in the picture for PARAGON.",
            "PARAGON: Open in PARAGON follows the note. Before: it opened the note by the name it was sent under, so after a rename in PARAGON it opened nothing. Now: it opens the note under its present name, also after it was renamed or moved to the Archive, from the purple line, the Send page and the list's menu.",
            "PARAGON: Add what is new. Before: sending a board again always made a second note. Now: when a board is already a note or a project in PARAGON and has new tasks, the Send page offers Add what is new to “Summer cabin” as its first row. For the first time, something sent from Seedboard lands inside a note you already have: PARAGON itself writes the new tasks at the end of that note's tasks, from its waiting list, when you switch to it. Seedboard still only adds lines to that waiting list and never opens a PARAGON note to write in it. If the note has gone from its place, the tasks go to the Inbox. What cannot follow is said (2 things were renamed and 1 bar moved since 18 Nov. PARAGON keeps the old ones.), with Send a fresh copy.",
            "PARAGON: What PARAGON gets. Before: the Send page described what would happen in a sentence. Now: a box shows the very words Send will write, with the picture, headings, tasks with their days and plain lines, and a line that counts, for example 13 tasks · 3 headings · 1 already in the Inbox · last day 20 Feb.",
            "PARAGON: to-dos say where they come from. A new switch, Say where they come from, on at first: every to-do in the Inbox ends with a link back to its board (or, for a board that is a project in PARAGON, to that project).",
            "PARAGON: Also remind me when a bar starts. A new switch on a timeline's Send page, off at first: every bar longer than 3 days gets a Start: task on its first day, right before its own.",
            "Fixed: a timeline bar sent as a to-do lost its days. Now it keeps them, for example Permit (starts 1 Dec), due 5 Dec.",
            "Fixed: a box already in the Inbox became a second task when the board was then sent as a note or a project. Now it is a plain line, Permit (already in the Inbox).",
            "Fixed: a frame's name was lost in PARAGON. Now a frame with a name is a heading in the note, the project and Copy the board as text, with its things under it, in reading order.",
            "Fixed: the Send page's words. Before: to-dos showed the next time PARAGON opens. Now it says they appear in PARAGON when you switch to it (PARAGON 340 or newer). When every ticked box is already in PARAGON it says so, for example All 4 to-do boxes are already in PARAGON., and a timeline's project row says All 6 bars and 1 milestone become tasks with their days.",
            "PARAGON: every Send keeps a list of what became a task, so the board can find its tasks in PARAGON again later.",

            // In and out (picks 22, 23, 24, 28)
            "Share: Share… takes the place of Share a picture…, in More and in the File menu. A small plate asks What (Whole board, What is chosen, Each frame a page) and As (Picture, PDF, Words), with a line saying what you get, then Cancel or Share. Share this… in the menu of a thing opens it at What is chosen.",
            "Share: a PDF. Drawn as lines and real letters, so it stays sharp at any size and its words can be found. A4 (Letter in the United States, Canada and Mexico), on its side by itself when the board is wider than tall, the board's name and the date small at the top. Each frame a page gives one page per named frame, with its name as the heading.",
            "Share: Words gives a small text file with the board's words. The form you shared in last is picked the next time.",
            "Print: File, Print… ⌘P on the Mac, and Print… in More on the iPhone and iPad. It prints the whole board on one page, fitted.",
            "Sharing to Seedboard: Seedboard is in the Share button of every app on the iPhone, iPad and Mac. Words, a link and up to 10 pictures go to a new board or one of your boards. The page Send to Seedboard shows what came, New board first, then your Pinned and Recent boards. It is on the board the next time you open Seedboard, beside what is there, and a line says, for example, 4 things came in from Share.",
            "Shortcuts: Add to board. Adds a thought, a list or a picture to a board without opening Seedboard; with no board chosen, to the board changed last. On a board that is one mind map, one line becomes a new branch. What came is chosen the next time you open that board.",
            "Shortcuts: Board as text and Board as picture give a board's words or picture to a shortcut. Find Boards finds boards by name, by when they were changed, pinned or sent.",
            "Shortcuts: New board offers your own starters. Before: only the seven built-in ones. Now: every starter on your Starters page, in your order; left empty, a blank board. A shortcut saved earlier with a starter picked may need it picked once more.",
            "Shortcuts: new phrases for Siri, such as Add a thought in Seedboard, Add to Summer cabin in Seedboard, Get Summer cabin as text from Seedboard and Get Summer cabin as a picture from Seedboard.",
            "Search on the device: before, it knew a board's name and its first 300 letters. Now it knows every word on a board and shows the board's small picture.",
            "Handoff: the board you have open offers itself on your other devices, and opens there at the same zoom and spot. When that device does not have the newest version yet, the board waits, unchangeable, with the line Waiting for the newest version from iCloud…, for 20 seconds at most.",

            // Mac (pick 29)
            "Fixed: the Mac's Help menu. Before: it said help was not available. Now: How Seedboard Works (⌘?) and What's New open the guide in a small window of its own, beside the board. Escape closes it.",
            "Mac: a Dock menu. Press the icon in the Dock long for New Board, your pinned boards and the five boards changed last.",
            "Mac: a picture from the iPhone or iPad. File, Import from iPhone or iPad, and the same line in the right-click menu of empty board: Take Photo, Scan Documents or Add Sketch on the iPhone, and it lands on the board, at the spot you clicked.",
            "Mac: Services, New Seedboard Board from Selection. Mark words in any app and make a board of them: a list becomes a mind map, anything else a sticky note.",
            "Mac: click the board's name in the top bar to rename it; right-click it for Rename…, Pin to the top, Make a copy and Save as a starter…. The iPad's name has the same small menu.",
            "Mac: Space on one chosen picture shows it large in Quick Look; the arrow keys step through the board's pictures.",

            // iPhone and iPad (pick 31)
            "iPhone: search and New board under the thumb. Before: the green New board button and Search boards stood at the top of the list. Now: the list starts right under the title, and at the bottom sit the round search field and a round green + (44 points). While you search they ride on the keyboard. Mac and iPad are as before.",
            "iPhone and iPad: press the app icon long for New board, New mind map, New board from the clipboard and your first two pinned boards. At most four lines: New mind map is left out when two boards are pinned.",
            "iPhone and iPad: the camera on the Picture tile. Hold the tile for a plate, Add a picture: Photos, Take a photo, Scan a page. Scan a page straightens paper or a whiteboard and puts up to 12 pages on the board.",
            "iPhone and iPad: three fingers swiped left undo, right redo, and a shake offers Undo. One gesture always takes back exactly one step.",

            // Pencil (pick 30)
            "Pencil: Tidy shapes. A box, an oval or a diamond drawn in one line, with the tip held still for a moment before lifting, becomes a real shape; a line from one thing to another becomes an arrow. One Undo gives the raw line back. It ships switched OFF: turn it on with the new switch Tidy shapes in the Draw plate. It can be tried with the mouse on the Mac too.",
            "Pencil: writing straight onto a shape is built but switched off until it can be tried with a real Pencil.",

            // Safety (DS 1 to 12)
            "Never losing a board: a starter you are changing survives a stop of the app. Before: it lived in memory only and was gone without a word. Now: it is kept on the device as you go and opens again at the next start, with the line You were changing the starter “Weekly review”. Your changes are back.",
            "Never losing a board: a rename or a move of a starter on one device no longer puts an older starter board back on the others. Before: the copy changed last won whole. Now: the name and place come from the newest rename, the board from the newest change of the board, and a starter board that loses is kept aside for 30 days.",
            "Never losing a board: starters follow the same iCloud rules as boards: a file that has not arrived is never written over, and of two copies the newer wins.",
            "Never losing a board: a board that was closed never saves. Before: photos still loading when you left a board were laid by the closed board and could save over newer changes. Now: they are not laid, and a red line says The pictures were not laid: the board was closed.",
            "Never losing a board: the amber dot tells the truth about pictures. Before: a picture whose upload failed (iCloud full) lost its dot after 3 seconds. Now: it keeps the dot and is tried again, every 3 seconds for a minute, then every 30 seconds.",
            "Never losing a board: Export all boards says what is missing, for example Exported 14 boards, 2 starters and 31 pictures. 3 pictures are not on this device yet and are not in the file. Your own and changed starters are packed too, in a folder Starters.",
            "Never losing a board: Undo after Send never makes a task go twice. Before: Undo brought back the older board with its older sent marks, so a second Send put the same tasks into the Inbox again. Now: what went to PARAGON is kept through Undo and Redo.",
            "Never losing a board: many photos on one board stay in sight. Before: about 13 phone photos filled the memory and pictures blinked to Picture not here yet. Now: the board keeps a lighter copy of each and reads the full one when you zoom in.",
            "Never losing a board: leaving the app never waits on starters behind iCloud. Import gives a picture back exactly as it was and never writes over one in iCloud. A picture file is deleted only after it was found unused twice, at least 7 days apart.",
            "Never losing a board: a copy of a board forgets what the original sent, so its tasks can be sent again from it.",
            "Never losing a board: a board changed in 0.5 is stored in a fifth format, because of doors, done bars and the list of what became a PARAGON task. A device still on 0.4 or older can look at such a board but not change it, until that device is updated. This is the same rule as before.",

            // Fixes and marks
            "Fifteen new hand-drawn marks: door, next note, next shape, turn into, tidy, by colour, break into notes, done, catch up, share, PDF, words, print, camera and scan.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.4", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            // Caption
            "Caption: words under a picture. Before: a picture had no words at all. Now: a chosen picture has a button, Caption, in its bar and in its menu (press and hold, or right-click). Two quick presses on the picture do the same. The typing starts right under the picture. Return ends it, Escape cancels it, Shift and Return make a new line. Empty words mean no caption.",
            "Caption: how it looks. It stands a little under the picture, in the middle, as wide as the picture, in small grey letters, and longer words wrap onto more lines. It moves, grows and is copied with its picture. Press on the caption and the picture is chosen. Show the whole board, the picture for PARAGON, Share and Export, and the small pictures in the list leave room for it.",
            "Caption: found and carried along. Search boards finds the words under a picture. Copy the board as text writes, for example, A picture: After the makeover (before: A picture). The PARAGON note says A picture: After the makeover (see the picture). A picture without a caption reads as before.",
            // Crop
            "Crop: show only a part of a picture. Before: a picture was always shown whole. Now: a chosen picture has a button, Crop, in its bar and in its menu. It opens a page, Crop the picture, with the whole picture, a frame over it with a round grip in each corner, and the outside dimmed. Drag a grip, or drag inside the frame. The frame stays inside the picture and is never very small.",
            "Crop: the page has Cancel (Escape on the Mac), Whole picture and Save, in full colour. After Save the picture on the board shows only that part, at the same size as before, and the part you kept stays exactly where it was.",
            "Crop: never cut for good. The picture itself is not changed. Press Crop again and Whole picture, then Save, and everything is back. One Undo takes a crop back too. Making a cropped picture bigger by its corner keeps its cropped shape. Duplicate, Copy and Paste take the crop and the caption along.",
            "Crop: grey while a picture says Picture not here yet, because there is nothing to crop until it has arrived.",
            "Pictures: the bar of a chosen picture now has Caption, Crop, Duplicate, Choose more (iPhone and iPad) and Remove. Its menu has Caption, Crop, Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Arrow from here and Remove. Before: no Caption, no Crop, and two quick presses did nothing.",
            // iCloud and the starter choice
            "The amber dot waits for pictures. Before: the tiny amber dot at the end of a board's row, and the line in Settings, Your boards, only said that the board's newest change was not in iCloud yet. A photo is much bigger than a board, so the dot could go out while the photo was still on its way up. Now: the dot stays until every picture the board shows is in iCloud too.",
            "Settings, Your boards: a second line while pictures wait, 1 picture is not in iCloud yet. It is safe on this device. or, for several, for example, 3 pictures are not in iCloud yet. They are safe on this device. It is checked every 3 seconds while something waits. At the start of the app, a picture that never reached iCloud's folder (a write that failed) is put there again. Not seen with real iCloud yet.",
            "New board, the choice of starters: its heading. Before: it read Start with, in small letters at the left. Now: it reads Starters, large, in the middle. Edit stays small at the right.",
            // Names
            "Who does what: the example person in the starter, and in the sample boards, has a new, neutral name: Alex. Nothing else about the starter changed.",
            // Never losing a board
            "Never losing a board: a board changed in 0.4 is stored in a fourth format, because of the crop. A device still on 0.3 or older can look at such a board but not change it, until that device is updated. This is the same rule as before.",
            "Limits: the room kept for a caption is a careful guess. On a very narrow picture with long words the real words can take one line more, and the last line can reach a little past the edge that Show the whole board measures. Line up and Even gaps look at the picture only, not at its caption.",
            "Two new hand-drawn marks: Crop and Caption.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.3", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            // Pictures
            "Pictures: put a picture on a board. Before: a board held ideas, shapes, notes, text, frames, arrows and drawings, and no pictures. Now: a photo or a screenshot lies on the same endless board, between everything else, and travels to your other devices with the board.",
            "Pictures: a new tool, Picture, the 9th in the tool row (before: 8). On the iPhone and iPad it opens Apple's photo picker, up to 12 pictures at once, with no permission question. On the Mac it opens a window called Choose a picture with the button Choose, and several files at once.",
            "Pictures: Paste. ⌘V, Paste in the Edit menu, or Paste in the board's menu puts a screenshot or a copied picture in the middle of the view. On the Mac, picture files copied in the Finder come in too. If you copied words, the words win, because a table or a web page you copy brings a picture of itself along.",
            "Pictures: Drop (Mac and iPad). Drag a picture file, or a picture from another app, onto the board and it lands where you let go. Several pictures land 28 points further down and right, one after the other.",
            "Pictures: on the Mac, the key P (with nothing chosen), Insert, Picture…, and Picture… in the right-click menu of empty board, where the picture lands where you pressed.",
            "Pictures: a new picture is laid at most 320 by 320 points big and keeps its shape. A tiny one is made at least 120 long. It is chosen at once.",
            "Pictures: a picture is moved, resized, copied, duplicated, cut, removed and undone like a sticky note. Drag a corner square to resize: the picture keeps its shape, the opposite corner stays where it is, and it never gets smaller than 44 by 28 points.",
            "Pictures: arrows can start and end at a picture. Line up and Even gaps work on pictures. Same size leaves them alone, because a picture keeps its own shape.",
            "Pictures: the bar of a chosen picture has Duplicate, Choose more (iPhone and iPad) and Remove. Its menu has Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Arrow from here and Remove. No words, no colour, no To do, and two quick presses or a letter key do not start typing.",
            "Pictures: the board's count line says 1 picture or 2 pictures. Copy the board as text writes A picture. The board's picture for PARAGON, Share and Export, and the small pictures in the list, show the pictures.",
            "Pictures: to PARAGON, a picture is in the board's picture. In the note or project text it is one plain line under Notes: A picture (see the picture). It never becomes a task.",
            "Pictures: not allowed on a Timeline board or a Who does what board. The line says: A picture cannot be put on this board.",
            "Pictures: when a picture has not arrived on this device yet, a soft plate with a picture mark says Picture not here yet. The board fills in by itself when the picture comes.",
            "Pictures: when something cannot be read as a picture, a line says That could not be read as a picture, or, for several, for example 3 could not be read as pictures.",
            "Pictures: kept light and safe. A big picture is made smaller when you put it on (its long side is at most 2048 dots), and a see-through one gets a white background. Each picture is saved once and never changed, on this device and on all your devices, next to your boards.",
            "Pictures: removing a picture from a board never deletes the picture itself, so Undo always works. A picture that no board uses any more is tidied away after 30 days, and only when no board, no removed board and no safety copy and no starter uses it. The check runs once, a little after the app starts. If anything is unclear, nothing is deleted.",

            // Starters
            "Starters: save your own. Before: 7 starters that came with the app, and no way to add one. Now: More, Save as a starter…, on the Mac also File, Save as a Starter…, and in the list of boards, press and hold a row (right-click on the Mac), Save as a starter…. A small page asks for the name (the board's name is filled in), with Cancel and Save. A name that is taken becomes, for example, Trip plan 2, and a line says Saved as the starter “Trip plan 2”.",
            "Starters: your own stand beside the 7 built-in ones wherever starters are shown: the home page, the New board choice and File, New Board. A board from a newer Seedboard cannot be saved as a starter.",
            "Starters: a new board from your starter is a fresh copy. It is not pinned, not marked as sent to PARAGON, and has the starter's name. A starter made from a timeline moves with the calendar, so the plan sits as far from today as on the day you saved it.",
            "Starters: a new Starters page. Before: none. Now: the small Edit button beside the STARTERS heading on the home page, the Edit button in the New board choice, File, Starters… on the Mac, and Edit starters… in a starter card's menu all open it. It lists every starter with its picture, name and grey line. Drag a row to move it (handles on the iPhone and iPad). Done closes it.",
            "Starters: each row has an Edit menu with Rename… (name and grey line, Cancel and Save), Change…, Remove… and, for a built-in you changed, Back to the original. Before: the 7 built-in starters could not be touched. Now: all of them can be renamed, moved, changed and removed.",
            "Starters: Remove… asks first (Remove the starter “Name”?). The Blank board has no Remove, because a new board must always be possible. A removed starter of your own is kept 30 days and then deleted. Bring back the built-in starters shows at the bottom of the page only when a built-in one was removed.",
            "Starters: press and hold a starter card (right-click on the Mac) for Rename…, Change…, Remove… and Edit starters….",
            "Starters: Change… opens the starter's board as a working copy, with a bar at the top, Changing the starter “Name”, and Cancel and Save (Save in full colour). Cancel after changes asks Discard the changes? with Discard and Keep changing. Going to another board, back to the list or (Mac) quitting without Cancel or Save keeps your changes: the starter is saved and a line says Saved the starter “Name”. In this mode the board has no Send to PARAGON, Pin, Remove or Save as a starter, and Rename renames the starter.",
            "Starters: kept on all your devices. If two devices change the same starter, the newer change stays. Seedboard looks again when it comes to the front, and when the Starters page opens.",
            "Starters: Shortcuts and Siri still offer the 7 built-in starters, as they were. Your own are not offered there.",

            // Typing and tools
            "Typing: Shift Return, or Option Return, makes a new line exactly where the cursor stands, and the typing goes on from there, in a box, a note, text, a frame and an idea. Before: the new line could land at the end of the words. Return alone still ends the typing, and in an idea starts the next idea. The one-line names (bars on a timeline, names and messages on a Who does what board, lane names) stay one line.",
            "The tool row: 9 tools now (Mind map, the shape tile, Diamond, Arrow, Sticky note, Text, Frame, Picture, Draw). Before: the 8 tiles were always 40 points wide and the row had to be slid sideways on an iPhone narrower than 394 points. Now: the tiles shrink to fit, 40 points, else 36, else 32. The row slides sideways only when even 32 does not fit.",
            "Mac: the key S for Draw (S for Sketch). Before: Draw had no letter. Now: S switches Draw on and off when nothing is chosen and no words are typed, like the other letters. The help text says Draw (S), and the Insert menu has a new line, Draw, after Picture… and below a line. On a timeline or a Who does what board the key does nothing and the menu line is grey.",
            "Two new hand-drawn marks: Picture and Save as a starter.",

            // Fixed
            "Fixed: a message with a missing name. Before: on a Who does what board, a message that names someone who is not on the board was counted (the list line, the numbers, the copied text, the note and project for PARAGON) but never drawn. Now: it counts only when both its names are on the board. It is left out of the count line, the numbers (1, 2, 3 follow the drawn ones), the copied text and what is sent, and Select all and the arrow keys skip it. It is not thrown away.",
            "Fixed: ⌘0 on a timeline (Mac). Before: nothing happened. Now: it goes back to the standard size, the Weeks scale, and the day in the middle stays put. ⌘1 is as before.",
            "Fixed: where a Who does what board opens on a small screen. Before: a big board opened at 75 % on a wrong spot, the empty top left corner. Now: it opens on the box of the first name. The change is small and was not seen on a screen.",
            "Fixed: the count when sending to PARAGON as a project. Before: shapes inside the lanes of a frame were counted as ideas (all 2 ideas become tasks). Now: they count as shapes (Nothing is ticked: all 2 shapes become tasks), like the flow shapes outside lanes.",
            "Fixed: the iPad with a trackpad or a mouse on a Timeline board and a Who does what board. Before: two-finger scroll and ⌘ with the wheel did nothing there (ordinary boards already had them in 0.2). Now: two fingers or the wheel slide the board, and ⌘ with the wheel zooms round the pointer.",

            // Never losing a board
            "Never losing a board: Export all boards now packs the pictures too. Before: the packed file held each board twice, as a Seedboard file and as a text outline. Now: every picture your boards show is in it as well, as a picture file beside the boards. Import boards takes those picture files back when you choose them together with the boards, and says, for example, 2 pictures came in.",
            "Never losing a board: a starter you are changing is never dropped by accident. If you go to another board, back to the list, or quit the Mac app without pressing Cancel or Save, what you changed is kept. Only Cancel throws it away, and it asks first.",
            "Never losing a board: a board changed in 0.3 is stored in a third format, so a device still on 0.2 can look at it but not change it, until that device is updated. This is the same rule as from 0.1 to 0.2.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.2", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            // Timeline board (pick 1)
            "Timeline board: a new kind of board. Before: 5 ways to start a board, and nothing knew about dates. Now: a 6th start, Timeline, opens a plan over time, with one row for each thing to do and a bar from its first day to its last.",
            "Timeline board: the names stand in a column at the left and the dates in a band at the top. Both stay in place while you slide.",
            "Timeline board: drag the middle of a bar to move it to another day or another row. Drag the round grip at an end to make it longer or shorter. A bar is always whole days and never shorter than 1 day.",
            "Timeline board: 3 scales instead of a zoom in percent: Days (a day is 28 points wide), Weeks (a week is 56) and Months (a month is about 72). The zoom buttons step through them, and one button shows the whole timeline.",
            "Timeline board: a red Today line, and a Today button that brings it into view.",
            "Timeline board: Comes after, an arrow from the end of one bar to the start of the next. Move or lengthen a bar and the bars after it are pushed along, so none starts before the one before it has ended. A line says how many moved, for example 2 bars after it moved along.",
            "Timeline board: a Milestone is one day, drawn as a purple diamond. A Phase is a heading: the rows under it get a soft band in its colour, and the phase shows one thin bar from its first to its last day.",
            "Timeline board: 4 tools with their word under them: Bar, Milestone, Phase, Comes after. The bar for a chosen row shows its dates in words and has Name, Colour (9 colours), Dates, To do, Copy and Remove.",
            "Timeline board: a Dates page with Starts and Ends, Cancel and Save.",
            "Timeline board: Return on a chosen bar makes the next bar in the row below, starting the day after, already joined. Drag a name in the left column to reorder the rows.",
            "Timeline board: keys of its own. B bar, M milestone, P phase, A comes after, T today. ← and → move the chosen bar a day, with Shift a week.",
            "Timeline board: the boards list counts it in its own words, for example 6 bars · 1 milestone · 2 phases.",
            "Timeline board: to PARAGON as a project, the phases become headings and the bars become tasks in date order. PARAGON has no start day for a task. So a bar goes over with its last day as the due date and its first day in words, for example Foundation (starts 8 Dec). The project's own due date is the last day on the timeline.",
            "Timeline board: to PARAGON as a note, the timeline's picture and every row with its dates, for example Permit: 1 Dec – 5 Dec 2026 · 5 days, then what comes after what.",
            "Drawing by hand: you can draw on a board. Before: a board held ideas, shapes, notes, text, frames and arrows, nothing drawn by hand. Now: lines drawn with the Apple Pencil, a finger or the mouse lie on the same endless board, between everything else.",
            "Drawing by hand: the Pencil just draws. On the iPad, put the Pencil on empty board and it draws at once, with no tool to choose first. On a shape, a note or an idea the Pencil still chooses or moves it, as a finger does. A finger never draws by itself; it slides the board as before.",
            "Drawing by hand: a new tool, Draw, the 8th in the tool row (before: 7). While it is on, a finger (iPhone, iPad) or the mouse or trackpad (Mac, iPad) draws too, on empty board and across things. Two fingers still slide and zoom. Press the tool again, press Done, or press Escape to leave it.",
            "Drawing by hand: a small plate while Draw is on, with Pen or Rubber, three thicknesses (thin 2 points, medium 4, thick 7.5), 10 colours (plain ink first, then the 9 board colours) and Done. The thickness and colour you used last come back next time on that device.",
            "Drawing by hand: the rubber takes whole lines. Rub across a line and the whole line goes. Everything rubbed out in one go comes back with one Undo.",
            "Drawing by hand: lines that belong together are one sketch. Lines drawn within 5 seconds of each other and no more than 40 points apart become one sketch; a pause or a jump starts a new one. A sketch is chosen, moved, copied, put in a frame, lined up, removed and undone like a sticky note, and it travels to your other devices the same way. Dragging its corner knob stretches its lines.",
            "Drawing by hand: a sketch is caught only on its lines, never anywhere in its box, so a note or a word inside a drawn ring is still easy to press. A finger catches a line within 22 points on the screen, the mouse or Pencil within 6. The Pencil over a sketch adds to it; a Pencil tap on one of its lines chooses it.",
            "Drawing by hand: one line, one Undo. Each line you draw is one step.",
            "Drawing by hand: the Pencil feels its pressure. Pressing lightly makes the line up to 35 % thinner, pressing hard up to 35 % thicker.",
            "Drawing by hand: the Pencil's double-tap (and squeeze on a Pencil Pro) follows Settings, Apple Pencil: switch between tool and eraser swaps pen and rubber, show colour palette opens or closes the Draw plate, off does nothing. When the Pencil rubs and no plate is showing, a small line says The Pencil rubs out, with a Pen button.",
            "Drawing by hand: Only Draw with Apple Pencil (iPad Settings) is honoured. With it on, a finger slides the board even while Draw is on, and the plate says so.",
            "Drawing by hand: a line keeps a colour name, so it changes with day and night. Plain ink is dark by day and light by night.",
            "Drawing by hand: a sketch has no words, no To do and no colour button. Its bar has Duplicate, Choose more and Remove.",
            "Drawing by hand: to PARAGON the picture shows the sketches. In the note or project text a sketch is one plain line under Notes: A sketch (see the picture). It never becomes a task.",
            "Drawing by hand: the board's count line now counts sketches, for example 2 notes · 3 sketches. The home page has one more made-up board, a sketch board.",
            "Drawing by hand: Seedboard 0.1 shows a board with a sketch on it, but cannot change it.",
            "Drawing by hand: writing into a box with the Pencil (Scribble) needed nothing new. The typing place is an ordinary text field, so the iPad's own Scribble should work there.",

            // Who does what (pick 3)
            "Who does what: a new kind of board. Before: 6 ways to start a board; to show who asks whom for what, in which order, you had to place boxes and arrows by hand. Now: a 7th start, Who does what (People in a row, messages in order), opens a board of its own kind. The app places everything.",
            "Who does what: the names (people, or things like a bank) stand in a row at the top, each in a box of its own colour (the first 8 get blue, teal, orange, purple, green, pink, red, yellow), each with a thin dashed line hanging down. The lines stand 150 points apart, and further apart only where the words between them need more room.",
            "Who does what: a message is a level arrow from one dashed line to another, with its words above it. Messages run from top to bottom and are numbered 1, 2, 3 by the app, in a small round mark before the words. A message takes the colour of the name it comes from.",
            "Who does what: an answer is drawn with dashes and an open arrow head. A message to itself is a small loop on the name's own line.",
            "Who does what: a note is a soft band across all the lines. A section is a heading on a thin line across the whole diagram. Neither is numbered.",
            "Who does what: 4 tools with their word under them: Who, Message, Note, Section.",
            "Who does what: three ways to add a message. Pull sideways from one dashed line to another (a thin line shows between which two messages it will stand); or choose a name, press Message to… and press the other name; or choose a message and press Return for the next one under it.",
            "Who does what: drag a message, note or section up or down to reorder, a name box sideways. The numbers follow by themselves.",
            "Who does what: the bar of a chosen message has Words, Answer, Turn round, Colour, To do, Copy and Remove. The bar of a chosen name has Name, Colour, Message to… and Remove. On the iPhone and iPad a message also has Move up and Move down.",
            "Who does what: the names stay in sight. Slide down a long diagram and the name boxes stay pinned at the top.",
            "Who does what: removing a name removes its messages too, and a line says how many, with Undo, for example: Bank went with its 2 messages.",
            "Who does what: the boards list counts it in its own words, for example 4 people · 9 messages · 1 note · 1 section. Search finds names and words.",
            "Who does what: to PARAGON as a note, the picture and a numbered list, for example 1. Alex → Bank: Ask for loan and 2. Bank answers Alex: Yes, with notes and sections in place. As a project, the ticked messages become tasks (with nothing ticked, all of them do).",
            "Who does what: the made-up boards got a 10th, Loan steps (4 names, 9 messages, 1 note, 1 section, 1 message to itself).",
            "Fixed: the Timeline starter's small picture on the home page was blank. Before: the starter card Timeline showed an empty picture, because its example was made for 1 January 2001 and 25 years had to fit into 240 points, so the bars became too small to see. Now: the example is made for today, and its 3 bars and its milestone show in the card. The new Who does what card shows its three names and three messages.",

            // Arrows (picks 12 and 13)
            "Arrows: soft corners. Before: a right-angle arrow had sharp corners. Now: every corner is rounded with a radius of 8 points.",
            "Arrows: side by side. Before: two arrows on the same side of a shape lay on top of each other and looked like one. Now: they stand 14 points apart. An arrow there and an arrow back are two lines, each with its own word.",
            "Arrows: round a shape. Before: an arrow ran straight on, hidden behind any shape in its way. Now: it goes round shapes, sticky notes and ideas, 12 points off.",
            "Arrows: moving an end. Before: an arrow chose its own sides and could not be moved to another shape. Now: a chosen arrow shows a small dot at each end. Drag a dot to another side of the same shape, or onto another shape. A side you chose is kept when the shapes move.",
            "Arrows: ends. Before: every arrow had one head, and nothing could change that. Now: 3 choices in the bar: One way, Both ways, No ends.",
            "Arrows: dashed. Before: every line was solid. Now: Dashed turns a line into dashes. The heads stay solid.",
            "Arrows: the word. Before: it sat in the middle of the line on a patch, so Yes swallowed a short arrow. Now: it stands beside the line near its start, above a level line and to the right of an upright one, with no patch. The typing field opens in the same place.",
            "Arrows: the bar of a chosen arrow has 11 buttons (before: 7): Words, Right angles, Straight, Curved, One way, Both ways, No ends, Dashed, Turn round, Colour, Remove. (Later in 0.2 they were folded into 7: see The bar.)",
            "Arrows: a press on a curved arrow now follows the curve itself. Before: the straight line between its two ends.",
            "Arrows: Tab makes the next shape. Before: only the green plus did; Tab did nothing on a shape. Now: with a shape chosen, or while you type in it, Tab makes the next shape with its arrow, in the direction the flow is running, ready to type. ⌘ with an arrow key does the same in that direction.",
            "Arrows: after a diamond or a round box comes a plain Box of the standard size (150 by 56). Before: another diamond, another round box. Every other shape is still followed by a copy of itself.",
            "Arrows: the first arrow out of a diamond says Yes and the second says No, by themselves. Before: no words. This holds for Tab, the plus and the Arrow tool.",
            "Arrows: the next shape stands 100 points away when its arrow carries a word, so the word has room. Before: always 70. A diamond's second Tab goes down, so No gets its own side.",
            "Arrows: the picture that goes to PARAGON shows all of this exactly as the board does.",

            // Shapes and lanes (picks 15 and 16)
            "Shapes and lanes: four more shapes. Before: 4 (Box, Round box, Oval, Diamond). Now: 8. New are Person (a figure with its words under it), Document (a sheet with a wavy bottom edge), Slanted box (something goes in or comes out) and Store (a cylinder, where things are kept).",
            "Shapes and lanes: the new shapes do all a Box does: the 9 colours, To do, words typed in place, arrows, the green plus, Copy, and the picture and text for PARAGON. Words always stay inside the shape.",
            "Shapes and lanes: arrows end on the real edge: on the slanted side of a Slanted box, on the wave of a Document, at the shoulders of a Person.",
            "Shapes and lanes: one plate with all shapes. Before: a Box tile, and Round box and Oval could only be made by changing a Box. Now: hold the Box tile, or press the small corner mark on it (Mac and iPad), and a plate shows all 8. A plain press adds the shape you picked last, and the tile shows its mark. Diamond keeps its own tile.",
            "Shapes and lanes: the bar of a chosen shape has 1 button, Shape, instead of 4 small shape buttons. It opens the same plate, and a shape can be changed into any of the 8 kinds (before: 4).",
            "Shapes and lanes: lanes in frames. Before: a frame was one plain area. Now: a chosen frame has Lanes in its bar: no lanes, or 2, 3, 4 or 5, as rows or as columns. Each lane has a name. The line between two lanes can be dragged; no lane gets narrower than 60 points.",
            "Shapes and lanes: what belongs to a frame. Before: only a thing lying wholly inside the frame travelled with it. Now: a thing belongs to the frame, and the lane, its middle is in. Moving a frame carries its things; sizing a frame moves nothing.",
            "Shapes and lanes: while a thing is dragged, the frame or the lane under its middle lights up softly, so you see where it will belong.",
            "Shapes and lanes: to PARAGON, a note lists the things of a frame with lanes lane by lane, under the lane's name. A project makes one group of tasks for each lane.",

            // Lining up (picks 11 and 14)
            "Lining up: things fall in line. Before: a dragged thing stopped exactly where you let go, so two boxes were almost never on one line and the arrow between them had a small kink. Now: a dragged thing catches its neighbours' edges and middles when it comes within 6 points on screen, at any zoom. It also catches the spot where the gaps are equal.",
            "Lining up: a thin pink line shows what is in line, and small pink marks show equal gaps. They vanish when you let go.",
            "Lining up: the same catch works while you size a thing, and while you pull a new shape out of a green plus. A dashed outline shows where the new shape will land, in line with the one it came from, so the arrow is one straight piece.",
            "Lining up: hold ⌘ on the Mac while dragging to switch the catch off. Ideas inside a mind map do not take part. The arrow keys move by 10 and 40 as before.",
            "Lining up: a soft dot grid. Before: the board was one plain colour. Now: a soft dot every 24 points behind everything. Zoomed out, dots are left out; below 40 % they fade and at 20 % they are gone. The dots are never in a picture.",
            "Lining up: Line up, Even gaps, Same size. Before: with several things chosen the bar had 3 buttons (To do, Copy, Remove). Now: 3 more. Line up needs 2 things, Even gaps 3, Same size 2 shapes, notes or frames. Each is one step for Undo. On the Mac they are also in the Board menu.",

            // Mind maps (picks 19 and 20)
            "Mind maps: ideas in another order. Before: the order of the ideas under a branch could not be changed at all. Now there are 3 ways: the keys ⌘↑ and ⌘↓, the buttons Move up and Move down in the bar (iPhone and iPad), and dragging an idea between two of its sisters, where a thin green line shows where it will land.",
            "Mind maps: a main branch moves among the branches on its own side. At the end of a side it crosses over to the other side. It can also be dragged straight into the other side's column.",
            "Mind maps: tearing an idea off as its own map now needs a drag of about 120 points away from its family. Before: 50 points in any direction.",
            "Mind maps: dropping an idea on the idea it already hangs under does nothing. Before: it jumped to the last place.",
            "Mind maps: a bar above the keyboard on the iPhone and iPad. Before: 1 button, Done. Now, while you type an idea, 4: Idea under (as Tab), Next idea (as Return), Out one level (as Shift Tab) and Done. On the iPhone the words are short: Under, Next, Out.",
            "Mind maps: the keyboard stays up while you go from idea to idea. For boxes, notes, text and arrows the bar still has just Done.",

            // Home page and colours (picks 6 and 8, and part of 26)
            "Home page: a home page instead of Choose a board (Mac and iPad). Before: the big area beside the list showed a mark, a line and one button, about 80 % empty. Now: under the heading Plant a seed, a row of 6 starter cards, each with a small drawing, and below them your boards as cards under Pinned, Recent and Sent to PARAGON, each with the board's small picture, its colour dot, its name and, for example, Changed 2 days ago.",
            "Home page: search. Before: none. Now: Search boards, under New board, finds boards by their name and by the words written on them. Every word you type must be there; capitals and accents do not matter. Nothing found says No board has these words.",
            "Home page: the iPhone list. Before: name and, for example, 5 shapes · 5 notes. Now: each row also shows the board's small picture at the right, and the second line starts with when it was changed.",
            "Home page: New board. Before: the big button on the empty page made a blank board at once, and the list's button showed a list of 5 rows. Now: both show the same cards with drawings, 3 in a row on the Mac and iPad, 2 in a row on the iPhone.",
            "Home page: opens where you left. Before: Seedboard always started with no board open, and every board opened shown whole. Now: the board that was open comes back, at the zoom and the place you left it. Every board remembers its own. This is for each device by itself and is not shared between them.",
            "Colours: Send to PARAGON is purple. Before: the same solid green as New board and the chosen board, so three green blocks fought for the eye. Now: PARAGON's purple, still a full-colour main button with white words.",
            "Colours: the chosen board in the list is a soft tint. Before: a solid green bar with white words. Now: a soft green tint with the name in bold green. New board is the only solid green in the list.",
            "Colours: starter boards come coloured. Before: Flow and Plan in steps were all white boxes. Now: in Flow the Start is green, the decision purple, the three steps blue, the Yes arrow green and the No arrow red. In Plan in steps the five boxes are blue.",
            "Colours: shapes lie a little above the board. Before: flat. Now: by day every shape has a faint shadow. None by night.",
            "Colours: sticky notes stay bright at night. Before: at night a yellow note turned olive and a pink one maroon, with light words. Now: all 6 note colours stay bright paper with dark words.",
            "Colours: a ring shows the colour in use. Before: the colour row gave no hint which colour a thing has. Now: a ring sits round the dot of the colour in use.",

            // The bar, the menu, copy and paste, Home (picks 7, 17 and 18)
            "The bar: it is beside the chosen thing (Mac and iPad). Before: the bar sat at the top edge of the board, however far away the thing was. Now: it floats 12 points above the chosen thing (above its plus dots when it has some), or 12 points below when there is no room above. It stays 8 points inside the visible board, follows the thing while the board slides or zooms, and is out of the way while the thing is dragged or typed in. The same holds for several chosen things and for a chosen arrow.",
            "The bar: one row on the iPhone. Before: a block of up to 3 rows at the top. Now: always exactly one row, still at the top under the title. The main buttons keep their word, the others show a mark only, and what does not fit goes into a … button that opens the rest as a short list from the bottom. A narrow iPad or Mac window does the same: words go first, then buttons, never a second row.",
            "The bar: one colour per bar. Before: up to four colours side by side (pink, blue, green, purple, grey). Now: every button wears the colour of what is chosen: pink for an idea, blue for a shape, amber for a note, teal for a frame, grey-blue for an arrow, green for several things. Only To do (purple) and Remove (red, before grey) stand apart at the end, behind a thin line. The timeline's bar is orange in the same way.",
            "The bar: the arrow's bar fits one row. Before: 10 buttons that wrapped to two rows on the Mac. Now: 7. Words, Line (opens Right angles, Straight, Curved), Ends (opens One way, Both ways, No ends), Dashed, Turn round, Colour, Remove.",
            "The bar: Copy is now called Duplicate. It always made a second one right beside the first, and ⌘D is as before. This holds for the timeline's bar too. Real Copy, Cut and Paste are new.",
            "The bar: plus dots are small. Before: four green 18-point plus buttons round every chosen shape, also on a side where an arrow already left, so they sat on arrow heads and on the arrows' words. Now: a side that already has an arrow has no plus. The others are small 10-point outline dots that grow to 18 points and show their plus when the pointer comes near (Mac). On the iPhone and iPad they show at 18 points with the plus, and the press area is still at least 32 points. A plus never sits on an arrow's word.",
            "The bar: the frame follows the shape. Before: every chosen thing got a rectangle, a diamond too. Now: the green frame follows the thing's own outline with a 3-point gap. A diamond gets a diamond, an oval an oval, a round box a round frame, a Person its head and shoulders, a Document its wavy edge, a Slanted box its slant, a Store its cylinder.",
            "The bar: size squares on all four corners. Before: 1, bottom right. Now: 4, one on each corner of a shape, a note and a frame. Dragging a corner keeps the opposite corner exactly where it is, also at the smallest size and when the words need more room. The edges still catch their neighbours' edges.",
            "A menu on every thing. Before: no menu on the board at all. Now: right-click a thing on the Mac (or Control-click), click with two fingers on an iPad trackpad, or rest a finger or the Pencil on it for half a second. On a thing: Words, Colour, To do, Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Arrow from here, Remove; an idea also Idea under, Next idea, Fold; a frame Lanes; a shape Shape. With several things: To do, Copy, Cut, Duplicate, Line up, Even gaps, Same size, Remove. On an arrow: Words, Line, Ends, Dashed, Turn round, Colour, Remove. On empty board: Paste, New note here, New box here, New idea here, Select all, Show the whole board; the new thing lands exactly where you pressed.",
            "The menu: on the Mac it is the system's own menu. On the iPhone and iPad it is a small plate just above your finger, in the colour of what you pressed, with Cancel.",
            "The menu: press and hold does not fight dragging. A press that moves is a drag, as before (an idea still lifts after a quarter of a second). Only a press that has not moved for half a second opens the menu, and if you then move more than 10 points, the menu goes away and the drag carries on.",
            "Mac: the pointer shows what it will do. Over a size square it becomes a diagonal resize arrow, over a plus dot or an arrow's end dot a pointing hand, and the plus dot under it grows.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: ⌘C, ⌘X and ⌘V work on the board (Mac, and iPad or iPhone with a keyboard). Before: no copy or cut, and ⌘V could only paste text. They are the Edit menu's lines and are in the menu of a thing.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: Copy takes the chosen things with the arrows between them; a chosen idea comes with everything under it, folded or not. It goes onto the clipboard in 3 forms at once: Seedboard's own, the words (for a text field, Notes, Mail), and a picture.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: Paste makes new things, with the arrows kept, at the pointer, at the spot you pressed in the menu, or in a free place near the middle of the view. Pasting the same again at the same spot lands each one 28 points further right and down. It works in another board too. Ideas pasted while an idea is chosen hang under it; a branch pasted on empty board becomes a small map in the branch's colour.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: text from elsewhere: one line becomes a sticky note, a list a mind map, as before, but now at the pointer or the pressed spot.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: drag words from Safari, Notes or any app onto the board and a sticky note appears there (a list becomes a mind map). Before: nothing happened.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: Option-drag copies (Mac). Hold Option while you let go and a copy lands there; the things themselves go back. One step for Undo.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: a choosing box with a finger. While Choose more is on, a finger drag on empty board draws the box and adds what it catches. Before: a finger could only slide the board.",
            "Copy, cut and paste: while words are typed, ⌘C, ⌘X, ⌘V and ⌘A belong to the words. On a timeline board Copy, Cut and Paste do nothing.",
            "Home: a Home row at the top of the boards list (Mac and iPad), above Pinned. Before: once a board was open there was no way back to the home page. Now: press Home and the page with the starters and all boards is back. On the iPhone the list itself is the home, as before.",

            // iPhone and iPad touch (picks 24 and 10)
            "iPhone and iPad: two fingers slide and zoom at the same time. Before: two fingers could only zoom, pinned to the spot where they first landed. Now: the board follows your fingers and grows or shrinks round the point between them, in one movement. Zoom still goes from 20 % to 300 %.",
            "iPhone and iPad: two fingers never choose or move a thing. Before: the first finger of a pinch often chose a shape and shoved it a little, and the shove stayed. Now: when the second finger lands, what the first finger did is taken back.",
            "iPhone and iPad: two quick presses on empty board now show the whole board. Before: they made a sticky note. On the Mac two clicks on empty board still make a sticky note.",
            "iPad with a trackpad or mouse: before, two-finger scrolling did nothing and a pinch zoomed round the wrong place. Now: two-finger scroll and the mouse wheel slide the board; a pinch, or ⌘ with the wheel, zooms round the pointer. A click and drag on empty board draws the choosing box, and Shift with a click adds to the choice, as on the Mac.",
            "iPhone and iPad: the Pencil is told from a finger. It chooses and moves things like a finger, an idea follows it at once, and a hand resting on the glass is ignored.",
            "iPhone: the swipe back from the left edge no longer slides the board or moves a thing while the page goes back.",
            "iPhone and iPad: every button on the board screen has a press area of at least 44 by 44 points. The bar buttons are 36 high (before 28) and 8 apart (before 5). The colour dots are 30 points (before 22) and 14 apart (before 7); on the iPhone they take two rows. Undo and Redo on the iPhone are 36 by 36 (before 30 by 28) and 8 apart (before 2).",
            "iPhone and iPad: the green plus signs and the corner square keep a press area of at least 32 by 32 points at every zoom. Before: at 50 % zoom the plus had 28 and the square 25.",
            "iPhone and iPad: small things are easy to hit when zoomed out. A thing smaller than 44 points on screen is caught in a 44-point area round it; between two close things the nearer one wins.",
            "iPhone: a board opens readable. Before: a large board opened whole at 50 %, with tiny words. Now: a board that would have to shrink below 75 % opens at 75 % on its main idea.",
            "iPhone: typing zooms in. When the view is smaller than 100 % and you start typing, it zooms to 100 % round the thing you type in, and goes back when you finish.",

            // Mac menus and keys (pick 25)
            "Mac menus: a fuller menu bar. Before: 1 menu of Seedboard's own, Board, with 6 lines. Now: 5 menus. The same lines work on an iPad with a keyboard and show there when ⌘ is held.",
            "Mac menus: File has 6 lines: New Board ⌘N (shows the starts), New Blank Board Shift ⌘N, Rename, Send to PARAGON ⌘ Return, Share a Picture, Remove Board.",
            "Mac menus: Edit. Delete and Select All ⌘A now act on the board; before they did not reach it. New: Duplicate ⌘D. Before it was called Copy the Chosen Things, in the Board menu, with the same key. The button in the bar is still called Copy.",
            "Mac menus: View has 4 lines: Zoom In ⌘+, Zoom Out ⌘-, Actual Size ⌘0, Show the Whole Board ⌘1.",
            "Mac menus: one key changed. Before: ⌘0 showed the whole board. Now: ⌘0 is Actual Size (100 %) and the whole board is ⌘1, as in other Mac apps.",
            "Mac menus: Insert is new, with 9 lines: the 7 tools with their letters, and Round box U and Oval O.",
            "Mac menus: Board now has To Do, Line Up, Even Gaps, Same Size, Move the Idea Up ⌘↑, Move the Idea Down ⌘↓ and Paste a List as a Mind Map.",
            "Mac menus: lines that cannot act are grey: with no board open, or with nothing chosen.",
            "Mac: Space and a drag slide the board. Before: the board moved with two fingers on the trackpad only. Now: hold Space, the pointer becomes an open hand, and drag anywhere, also on top of a shape. A short press of Space with one thing chosen still starts typing in it.",

            // Raycast, Siri and Spotlight (pick 26)
            "Raycast, Siri and Spotlight: reachable from outside. Before: the only way in was the link in a PARAGON note. Now: 3 actions for the Shortcuts app, Siri, the Action button and Raycast: New board from text, New board, and Open board.",
            "Raycast, Siri and Spotlight: New board from text takes a title and any text. A list becomes a mind map, anything else one sticky note. Seedboard opens on the new board.",
            "Raycast, Siri and Spotlight: 7 phrases to say or type, such as New Seedboard board from text, Start a thought in Seedboard and Open Summer cabin in Seedboard.",
            "Raycast, Siri and Spotlight: the search on the device finds a board by its name and by the first words on it, up to 300 letters, and opens it. A removed board leaves the search.",

            // Removed boards and earlier versions (pick 4)
            "Removed boards: bring back. Before: a removed board went into a hidden folder for 30 days, and the app had no way to get it back. Now: Settings, Your boards, Removed and earlier versions lists every board removed in the last 30 days, with a small drawing, its name, what is on it, and, for example, removed 12 Nov · 18 days left.",
            "Removed boards: Bring back returns the board as the same board, on every device, so links from PARAGON notes open it again. Remove for good asks first and cannot be undone.",
            "Earlier versions: before, the daily copies (14 days) and the versions set aside when two devices disagreed could not be seen in the app. Now: the same page lists every board that has any. Each version says where it came from and its date. Open as a copy makes a new board, for example Summer cabin (version from 18 Nov 10:00). The board itself is never replaced.",
            "Earlier versions: a clash is no longer silent. Before: when two devices changed the same board, the newer won and the other was put aside without a word. Now: a line across the top of the window says so, with Show, which opens the page at that board.",
            "Removed boards: a link to a removed board. Before: one sentence for 4 seconds. Now: the sentence stays as a line across the top, with a Bring back button. When the 30 days are over, the line says so.",
            "Earlier versions: tidy. Before: set-aside versions were never cleared away. Now: they are deleted after 30 days. Daily copies keep their 14 days.",

            // Export (pick 5)
            "Export: Export all boards. Before: boards could only leave the app one at a time, as a picture or to PARAGON. Now: Settings, Your boards, Export all boards makes 1 packed file with every board in it twice: as a Seedboard file and as a plain text outline. The line below says when you last exported.",
            "Export: Import boards reads Seedboard files from such an export. Each comes in as a new board; none replaces a board. A name that is taken gets (imported).",

            // Never losing a board (S1 to S5)
            "Never losing a board: typing against another device. Before: when another device's change arrived while you were typing, it was skipped and never offered again. Now: it waits and is offered the moment you stop typing. If you changed something, yours stays and the other version is kept under Earlier versions.",
            "Never losing a board: a removal against a newer change. Before: a removal always won, even over a change made later on another device. Now: the removal carries its time, and a later change wins. The board comes back on every device.",
            "Never losing a board: versions iCloud put aside. Before: when two devices wrote at once, iCloud's put-aside version was deleted unread, and its stray copies were skipped for ever. Now: they are read first. The newer wins and the other is kept under Earlier versions.",
            "Never losing a board: an older Seedboard and a newer board. Before: an older app showed a thing it did not know as a box and could save the board back without it. Now: such a board can be looked at and not changed, and what the older app does not know is carried along untouched.",
            "Never losing a board: the boards are now stored in a second format. A board changed by 0.2 can be looked at in 0.1 but not changed there, until that device is updated. Boards made in 0.1 open as before.",
            "Never losing a board: saving. Before: saving waited behind iCloud and could fail without a word. Now: the device copy is written at once. A tiny amber dot in the list marks a board that is not in iCloud yet, and Settings says how many wait. A save that fails is said, and tried again by itself.",

            // Send to PARAGON (pick 21, S6, S7)
            "Send to PARAGON: As a project follows the ticks. Before: every idea under a main branch became a task, ticked or not; the sample board Summer cabin gave 13 tasks. Now: when at least one idea is ticked To do, only ticked things become tasks and the others stay as plain lines under their heading. The same board gives 2 tasks and 11 plain lines. With nothing ticked, everything becomes a task as before.",
            "Send to PARAGON: the Send page shows one line with the counts under As a project, for example 2 ticked things become tasks, 11 stay as plain lines.",
            "Send to PARAGON: Send this branch. With one idea chosen, the Send page has a switch, Only the branch, off at first. Switched on, just that idea and everything under it is sent, the name becomes the branch's words, and the picture shows just that branch. The board is not marked as sent for it.",
            "Send to PARAGON: finding PARAGON. Before: Seedboard looked for PARAGON's notes only when it started, and could pick a folder PARAGON never used. Now: it looks again at every Send, and takes only a folder PARAGON really uses.",
            "Send to PARAGON: nothing left behind. Before: when the note could not be saved, its picture stayed in PARAGON's Pictures. Now: the picture is taken away again.",
            "Send to PARAGON: tasks never go twice. Before: a second Send in a quick moment could send the same to-do boxes again. Now: the ticks are saved first, and a second Send while one is on its way is refused.",
            "Send to PARAGON: the link back. Before: Open the board in Seedboard failed when Seedboard was not running. Now: the link is held until the boards are loaded, and then the board opens. A board that has not arrived yet opens by itself when it does.",

            // Fixed (B1 to B8)
            "Fixed: keys on the iPad. Before: with a keyboard on the iPad the ⌘ keys did nothing, and the board stopped hearing the keys after a button was pressed. Now: all the ⌘ keys work, holding ⌘ shows them, and the board takes the keys back after every button.",
            "Fixed: the view jumped back to the whole board after a finger slide. Now it stays where you left it.",
            "Fixed: the Send page was cut off on the iPhone and half empty on the iPad. Now it fills the screen on the iPhone and is a card just as tall as its words on the iPad.",
            "Fixed: a main branch keeps its side. Before: adding or removing a branch could make others jump across the middle. Now every main branch stays on the side it is on.",
            "Fixed: a torn-off branch could not be hung back, and on a touch screen an idea tore off by accident. Now: drop the middle idea of a map onto an idea of another map and it hangs there. With a finger an idea lifts only after a quarter of a second.",
            "Fixed: Shift Return started the next idea, although the guide promised a new line; Shift Tab did the same as Tab. Now Shift Return makes a new line, and Shift Tab moves the idea out one level.",
            "Fixed: changing a shape into another kind threw away its size, and a diamond grew only taller. Now the size stays, and a shape grows wider first (a box up to 300 points, an oval 320, a diamond 340), then taller.",
            "Fixed: a growing mind map ran over what was beside it. Now notes, shapes, text and other maps step aside, 8 points clear.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "0.1", date: "2026-11-18", items: [
            "Boards: the first Seedboard. One app for iPhone, iPad and Mac.",
            "Boards: a list at the side with the groups Pinned, Recent and Sent to PARAGON. Each row has a coloured dot, the name, and a count of ideas, shapes and notes.",
            "Boards: New board, with five ways to start: Blank board, Mind map, Flow, Pros and cons, Plan in steps. A small pop-up under the button on the iPad and Mac, up from the bottom on the iPhone.",
            "Boards: press and hold a row (right-click on the Mac) to Rename, Pin to the top, Make a copy, change the Colour, Open in PARAGON or Remove.",
            "Boards: a blank board that is still called New board takes the first words you write. A new board of the same start gets a 2, a 3 and so on.",
            "Boards: the board has seven tools: Mind map, Box, Diamond, Arrow, Sticky note, Text, Frame. The new thing appears in the middle of the view, ready to type.",
            "Boards: Undo and Redo, up to 120 steps, at the top (on the iPhone, bottom left above the tools). More menu with Rename, Pin, Paste a list as a mind map, Copy the board as text, Share a picture and Remove.",
            "Boards: zoom from 20 % to 300 % with Smaller, Bigger and Show the whole board (never below 50 %: on a small screen you see the middle and move around). Pinch on the iPhone and iPad. Two-finger scroll, pinch and ⌘ with scroll on the Mac.",
            "Boards: on the iPhone and iPad, drag on empty board to move the view. On the Mac, dragging on empty board draws a box that chooses things.",
            "Boards: choose several things on the Mac with Shift, the box, or ⌘A. Move, copy and remove them together, or move them with the arrow keys.",
            "Boards: on the iPhone and iPad, a Choose more button in the bar lets each press add to what is chosen.",
            "Boards: the bar for the chosen thing wraps onto a second row on a narrow screen instead of scrolling.",
            "Boards: the Mac has a Board menu with Copy, To Do, Paste a List as a Mind Map (Shift ⌘V, also with an iPad keyboard), Bigger, Smaller and Show the Whole Board, and Undo and Redo in the Edit menu.",
            "Typing: Return, or pressing elsewhere, keeps your words. Escape cancels: the words go back and a thing just made goes away again.",
            "Typing: on the iPhone and iPad a Done button sits above the keyboard.",
            "Typing: what you type is kept as you type. Leaving the app or closing it finishes the typing and writes everything at once.",
            "Mind maps: type an idea, press Return for the next idea and Tab for an idea under it. A new idea left empty goes away again.",
            "Mind maps: choose an idea and just start typing, and your words go on after what is there.",
            "Mind maps: the arrow keys walk the map. A plus beside a chosen idea makes an idea under it. The middle idea has one plus, on the right; the new branch lands as the Both sides setting says.",
            "Mind maps: an idea has a Copy button in its bar too.",
            "Mind maps: drag an idea onto another to move it with everything under it. Drag it far out onto empty board to make a map of its own.",
            "Mind maps: Fold hides what is under an idea and shows how many are hidden. Both sides splits a map to the right and the left.",
            "Mind maps: each main branch takes its own colour, and you can change the colour of a whole branch.",
            "Mind maps: paste a list and it becomes a map. Lines pushed in become ideas under the one above. Bullets, numbers, tick boxes and headings are understood.",
            "Shapes and arrows: box, round box, diamond and oval. Words inside each of them.",
            "Shapes and arrows: a plus on each side of a chosen shape makes the next shape already joined by an arrow. Pull a plus onto another shape to join them. The plus signs are small and shrink a little when you zoom out.",
            "Shapes and arrows: the Arrow tool joins two shapes by pressing them one after the other. Arrows follow when a shape moves. A frame takes no arrows: pressing one passes over it and the tool stays on.",
            "Shapes and arrows: words on an arrow, three line styles (Right angles, Straight, Curved), Turn round, and a colour.",
            "Shapes and arrows: make a shape bigger by pulling the small square at its corner.",
            "Notes, text and frames: sticky notes in six colours. Two quick presses on empty board make one, when nothing was chosen before the first press.",
            "Notes, text and frames: free text with no box.",
            "Notes, text and frames: frames in nine colours. A frame carries everything that lies completely inside it.",
            "Notes, text and frames: mark any idea, shape, note or text as To do. A small purple ring with a tick shows it.",
            "Send to PARAGON: send a board as a note. It becomes a note in Resources, with the board's picture, an outline, the flow, the notes and the tasks.",
            "Send to PARAGON: send a board as a project. Branches become headings, ideas become tasks, flow shapes become steps. It goes into Projects.",
            "Send to PARAGON: send only the to-do boxes. Each one becomes a task in the PARAGON Inbox, all in one write (all or none). PARAGON files them itself the next time it opens.",
            "Send to PARAGON: a to-do box is never sent twice, unless you take its tick off and tick it again. The lines of a sticky note are joined with a space. A note also leaves one line in the Inbox that points to it, when the Inbox can be reached.",
            "Send to PARAGON: you choose the name. If it is taken, the note gets (board 2) and so on. Seedboard only adds notes. It never changes one you already have.",
            "Send to PARAGON: every note has a line that opens the board again in Seedboard. Press it while reading the note in PARAGON.",
            "Send to PARAGON: Open in PARAGON from the Send page and from a board's menu. Settings tells you whether PARAGON was found, with a Check again button.",
            "Safety: boards are kept on the device and in iCloud, on all your devices. Without iCloud they stay on the device.",
            "Safety: when two devices change the same board, the newer change wins and the other is kept as a safety copy.",
            "Safety: a safety copy of all boards once a day, kept for 14 days. Settings says when the last one was made.",
            "Safety: a removed board leaves all your devices. A small mark is left in iCloud, and each device moves its copy to a Removed folder, kept 30 days from the day of removal.",
            "Safety: a board made by a newer Seedboard is shown, so you can look at it, but not changed, and is never written over.",
            "Safety: when another device's newer version of the open board arrives, Undo starts over. Settings also says when iCloud is off.",
            "Safety: a file that this version cannot read is never written over.",
            "Look: each part of the app has its own colour: boards green, mind maps pink, shapes blue, arrows slate, notes amber, frames teal, PARAGON purple.",
            "Look: day and night colours. Icons drawn by hand in the shared icon library. The app icon is a white seed with three branches on teal.",
            "Look: Settings with How it works, What's new, PARAGON, Your boards and the version. On the Mac it is its own window (⌘,); on the iPhone and iPad a page with Done.",
            "iPhone: the whole board works there too. The boards list comes first and a board opens over it.",
        ]),
    ]
